58
00:05:59,936 --> 00:06:14,096
There are three main pillars of cybersecurity that we deal with since the inception of computers, and they're known as the confidentiality, integrity, and availability triad, also known as CIA, not to be confused with the American Intelligence Agency.

59
00:06:14,336 --> 00:06:24,976
but here we're looking at three different pillars where we want our data to remain confidential, the integrity of the data to be intact, and the data to be made available at all points in time.

60
00:06:25,216 --> 00:06:28,016
So let's talk about these three aspects.

61
00:06:28,270 --> 00:06:34,576
The principle of confidentiality asserts that the information and functions can be accessed only by authorized parties.

62
00:06:34,736 --> 00:06:39,936
So, for example, even if you password protect your file, what is it that you're trying to do?

63
00:06:40,096 --> 00:06:47,376
You're trying to prevent other users accessing your data and peeping into your files so that your data remains confidential.

64
00:06:47,536 --> 00:06:50,976
It is only shared with people who know the password.

65
00:06:51,216 --> 00:06:51,856
Integrity.

66
00:06:52,016 --> 00:06:54,896
This is where the trustworthiness of that data comes into the picture.

67
00:06:55,136 --> 00:07:09,124
Where if the data is going to be changed, for example, you have a spreadsheet which has a lot of information about users and their login activities and whatnot, and you want to ensure that that data is not modified by any unauthorized user.

68
00:07:09,296 --> 00:07:16,896
So you're going to verify that the information is correct and is not modified by anybody who's unauthorized.

69
00:07:17,102 --> 00:07:23,696
The availability part ensures that this data is made available to all authorized users when and where they want it.

70
00:07:23,886 --> 00:07:24,176
Right?

71
00:07:24,576 --> 00:07:32,896
The principles of availability assert that in systems functions and data must be available on demand according to agreed upon parameters based on levels of service.

72
00:07:33,056 --> 00:07:35,856
Now, this is where your service level agreements would come in.

73
00:07:36,016 --> 00:07:42,496
For example, when we log on to Gmail, we always assume that Gmail is going to work and it's going to be available online.

74
00:07:42,736 --> 00:07:50,336
At no point in time, or very few times has it ever occurred that you've gone onto the internet typed in gmail.com and the website is not available.

