08:24:22,656 --> 08:24:28,164
It takes you from the basics to advanced concepts, all explained in simple and easy to understand way.

5329
08:24:28,336 --> 08:24:31,460
This one is perfect if you want everything in one place.

5330
08:24:32,110 --> 08:24:38,176
We first get a recap on what asymmetric encryption is and how is it any different from symmetric encryption?

5331
08:24:38,894 --> 08:24:42,692
We learn what digital signatures are and how they are used in today's world.

5332
08:24:43,726 --> 08:24:48,900
Next, we understand the digital signature algorithm and see how it works step by step.

5333
08:24:50,478 --> 08:24:56,996
Moving on, we learn about the advantages DSA provides when it comes to managing digital signatures in a corporate environment.

5334
08:24:58,446 --> 08:25:03,236
Next, we understand the RSA algorithm and see how it works from a layman's perspective.

5335
08:25:04,686 --> 08:25:10,308
We understand how RSA can be used to encrypt and decrypt general data apart from digital signatures.

5336
08:25:10,734 --> 08:25:17,732
And finally, we learn about the advantages RSA provides when it comes to managing digital signatures and encrypting information.

5337
08:25:19,502 --> 08:25:21,764
Let's take a look at asymmetric encryption.

5338
08:25:24,142 --> 08:25:27,140
Asymmetric encryption uses a double layer of protection.

5339
08:25:28,206 --> 08:25:32,228
There are two different keys at play here: a private key and a public key.

5340
08:25:32,910 --> 08:25:39,620
The public key is used to encrypt the information pre-transit, and the private key is used to decrypt the data post-transit.

5341
08:25:40,366 --> 08:25:43,588
This pair of keys must belong to the receiver of the message.

5342
08:25:44,206 --> 08:25:49,988
The public keys can be shared by a messaging, blog post, or key servers since there are no restrictions.

5343
08:25:50,862 --> 08:25:54,148
As you can see in the image, the two keys working in the system.

5344
08:25:54,766 --> 08:26:00,324
The sender first encrypts the message using the receiver's private key, after which we receive the ciphertext.

5345
08:26:00,814 --> 08:26:04,484
The ciphertext is then transmitted to the receiver without any other key.

5346
08:26:05,294 --> 08:26:12,420
On getting the ciphertext, the receiver can use his or own private key to decrypt the information and get the clear text back.

5347
08:26:13,198 --> 08:26:20,836
There has been no requirement of any key exchange throughout this process, therefore solving the most glaring flaw faced in symmetry key cryptography.

5348
08:26:21,710 --> 08:26:29,412
The public key known to everyone cannot be used to decrypt messages, and the private key which can decrypt messages need not be shared with anyone.

5349
08:26:30,062 --> 08:26:36,292
The sender and receiver can exchange personal data using the same set of keys for as often as long as possible.

5350
08:26:39,118 --> 08:26:42,084
In this example, we have two people, Nancy and John.

5351
08:26:42,958 --> 08:26:48,484
If Nancy wants to send a message to John, John's public key should be used to encrypt the information.

5352
08:26:49,134 --> 08:26:55,908
The ciphertext is then transferred to John, who can use his private key to decrypt the data and get the plain text back.

5353
08:26:57,038 --> 08:27:03,300
This goes to highlight how we have overcome the problem of key sharing when it comes to asymmetric encryption.

5354
08:27:04,878 --> 08:27:11,268
Now that we have a proper revision, let's understand what digital signatures are before moving on to the algorithm.

5355
08:27:13,646 --> 08:27:18,788
The objective of digital signatures is to authenticate and verify documents and data.

5356
08:27:19,406 --> 08:27:26,660
This is necessary to avoid tampering and digital modification or forgery of any kind during the transmission of official documents.

5357
08:27:27,598 --> 08:27:31,716
They work on the public key cryptography architecture with one exception.

5358
08:27:32,526 --> 08:27:38,116
Typically, an asymmetric key system encrypts using a public key and decrypts with a private key.

5359
08:27:38,606 --> 08:27:41,828
For digital signatures, however, the reverse is true.

5360
08:27:43,118 --> 08:27:47,876
The signature is encrypted using a private key and is decrypted with the public key.

5361
08:27:48,398 --> 08:27:58,180
Because the keys are inked together, decoding it with the public key verifies that the proper private key was used to sign the document, therefore verifying the signature's provenance.

5362
08:27:58,894 --> 08:28:02,256
Let's go through each step to understand the procedure thoroughly.

5363
08:28:02,766 --> 08:28:12,708
In step one, we have M, which is the original plain text message, and it is passed on to a hash function denoted by H hash to create a digest.

5364
08:28:14,702 --> 08:28:20,772
Next, it bundles the message together with the hash digest and encrypts it using the sender's private key.

5365
08:28:23,854 --> 08:28:29,028
It sends the encrypted bundle to the receiver, who can decrypt it using the sender's public key.

5366
08:28:32,174 --> 08:28:38,628
Once the message is decrypted, it is passed through the same hash function each hash to generate a similar digest.

5367
08:28:39,822 --> 08:28:44,708
It compares the newly generated hash with the bundled hash value received along with the message.

5368
08:28:45,038 --> 08:28:47,684
If they match, it verifies data integrity.

5369
08:28:48,654 --> 08:28:54,276
In many instances, they provide a layer of validation and security to messages through a non-secure channel.

5370
08:28:55,278 --> 08:29:01,924
Properly implemented, a digital signature gives the receiver reason to believe that the message was sent by the claimed sender.

5371
08:29:02,574 --> 08:29:12,228
Digital signatures are equivalent to traditional handwritten signatures in many respects, but properly implemented digital signatures are more difficult to forge than the handwritten type.

5372
08:29:13,294 --> 08:29:20,000
Digital signature schemes in the sense used here are cryptographically based and must be implemented properly to be effective.

5373
08:29:20,430 --> 08:29:29,924
They can also provide non-repudiation, meaning that the signer cannot successfully claim that they did not sign a message while also claiming their private key remains secret.

5374
08:29:30,606 --> 08:29:39,332
Further, some non-reparation schemes offer a timestamp for the digital signature so that even if the private key is exposed, the signature is valid.

5375
08:29:40,558 --> 08:29:52,612
To implement the concept of digital signature in real world, we have two primary algorithms to follow: the RSA algorithm and the DSA algorithm, but the latter is a topic of learning today.

5376
08:29:53,166 --> 08:29:57,412
So let's go ahead and see what the digital signature algorithm is supposed to do.

5377
08:30:00,366 --> 08:30:07,460
Digital signature algorithm is a FIPS standard, which is a federal information processing standard for digital signatures.

5378
08:30:07,840 --> 08:30:17,040
It was proposed in 1991 and globally standardized in 1994 by the National Institute of Standards and Technology, also known as the NIST.

5379
08:30:17,280 --> 08:30:25,604
It functions on the framework of modular exponentiation and discrete logarithmic problems, which are difficult to compute as a force-brute system.

5380
08:30:26,830 --> 08:30:33,380
Unlike DSA, most signature types are generated by signing message digest with the private key of the originator.

5381
08:30:33,966 --> 08:30:36,196
This creates a digital thumbprint of the data.

5382
08:30:36,750 --> 08:30:42,660
Since just the message digest is signed, the signature is generally much smaller compared to the data that was signed.

5383
08:30:43,278 --> 08:30:51,748
As a result, digital signatures impose less load on processors at the time of signing execution and they use small volumes of bandwidth.

5384
08:30:52,494 --> 08:30:59,600
DSA, on the other hand, does not encrypt message digest using private key or decrypt message digest using public key.

5385
08:30:59,790 --> 08:31:09,668
Instead, it uses mathematical functions to create a digital signature consisting of two 160-bit numbers, which are originated from the message digests and the private key.

5386
08:31:10,382 --> 08:31:18,116
DSAs make use of the public key for authenticating the signature, but the authorization process is much more complicated when compared with RSA.

5387
08:31:18,990 --> 08:31:26,084
DSA also provides three benefits, which is the message authentication, integrity verification, and non-repardiation.

5388
08:31:26,720 --> 08:31:31,012
In the image we can see the entire process of DSA validation.

5389
08:31:31,406 --> 08:31:38,500
A plain text message is passed on to a hash function where the digest is generated, which is passed on to a signing function.

5390
08:31:39,150 --> 08:31:47,620
Signing function also has other parameters like a global variable g, a random variable k, and the private key of the sender.

5391
08:31:48,878 --> 08:31:53,348
The outputs are then bundled onto a single pack with the plain text and sent to the receiver.

5392
08:31:53,966 --> 08:32:00,676
The two outputs we receive from the signing functions are the two 160 bit numbers denoted by S and R.

5393
08:32:02,734 --> 08:32:08,836
On the receiver end, we pass the plaintext to the same hash function to regenerate the message digest.

5394
08:32:09,678 --> 08:32:19,620
It is passed on to verification function, which has other requirements such as the public key of the sender, global variable g, and SNR received from the sender.

5395
08:32:22,254 --> 08:32:25,732
The value generated by the function is then compared to R.

5396
08:32:26,574 --> 08:32:31,844
If they match, then the verification process is complete and data integrity is verified.

5397
08:32:32,750 --> 08:32:36,132
This was an overview of the way the DSA algorithm works.

5398
08:32:36,590 --> 08:32:43,236
We already know it depends on logarithmic functions to calculate the outputs, so let us see how we can do the same in our next section.

5399
08:32:44,846 --> 08:32:48,900
We have three phases here, the first of which is key generation.

5400
08:32:49,582 --> 08:32:52,452
To generate the keys we need some prerequisites.

5401
08:32:53,006 --> 08:32:55,908
We'd select a Q which becomes a prime divisor.

5402
08:32:56,430 --> 08:33:01,156
We select a prime number P such that P minus 1 mod Q equal to 0.

5403
08:33:01,742 --> 08:33:07,908
We also select a random integer G which must satisfy the two formulas being mentioned on the screen right now.

5404
08:33:08,752 --> 08:33:12,868
Once these values are selected, we can go ahead with generating the keys.

5405
08:33:13,422 --> 08:33:20,996
The private key can be denoted by x and it is any random integer that falls between the bracket of 0 and the value of q.

5406
08:33:21,646 --> 08:33:29,316
The public key can be calculated as y equal to g to the power x mod p, where y stands for the public key.

5407
08:33:30,190 --> 08:33:37,508
The private key can then be packaged as a bundle which comprises of values of p, q, g and x.

5408
08:33:37,998 --> 08:33:45,312
Similarly, the public key can also be packaged as a bundle having the values of p, q, g and y.

5409
08:33:46,478 --> 08:33:51,556
Once we're done with key generation, we can start verifying the signature and its generation.

5410
08:33:54,286 --> 08:33:57,892
Once the keys are generated, we can start generating the signature.

5411
08:33:58,894 --> 08:34:03,152
The message is passed through a hash function to generate the digest edge first.

5412
08:34:03,822 --> 08:34:08,516
We can choose any random integer k which falls under the bracket of 0 and Q.

5413
08:34:09,358 --> 08:34:17,796
To calculate the first 160-bit number of a signing function of R, we use the formula g to the power k mod p into mod q.

5414
08:34:18,318 --> 08:34:25,284
Similarly, to calculate the value of the second output that is s, we use the following formula that is shown on the screen.

5415
08:34:26,126 --> 08:34:29,952
The signature can then be packaged as a bundle having R and S.

5416
08:34:32,974 --> 08:34:37,252
This bundle along with the plain text message is then passed on to the receiver.

5417
08:34:39,310 --> 08:34:42,212
Now with the third phase, we have to verify the signature.

5418
08:34:42,734 --> 08:34:48,036
We first calculate the message digest received in the bundle by passing it to the same hash function.

5419
08:34:48,846 --> 08:34:55,012
We calculate the value of W, U1, and U2 using the formulas shown on the screen.

5420
08:34:55,790 --> 08:35:02,308
We have to calculate a verification component which is then to be compared with the value of R being sent by the sender.

5421
08:35:02,862 --> 08:35:06,852
This verification component can be calculated using the following formula.

5422
08:35:07,758 --> 08:35:11,300
Once calculated, this can be compared with the value of R.

5423
08:35:11,566 --> 08:35:22,980
If the values match, then the signature verification is successful and our entire process is complete starting from key generation to the signature generation all the way up to the verification of the signature.

5424
08:35:26,478 --> 08:35:32,996
With so many steps to follow, we are bound to have a few advantages to boot this, and we would be right to think so.

5425
08:35:33,806 --> 08:35:41,412
DSA is highly robust in the security and stability aspect when compared to alternative signature verification algorithms.

5426
08:35:41,934 --> 08:35:49,988
We have a few other ciphers that aim to achieve the simplicity and the flexibility of DSA, but it has been a tough ask for all the other suits.

5427
08:35:51,502 --> 08:35:56,100
The key generation is much faster when compared to the RSA algorithm and such.

5428
08:35:56,590 --> 08:36:05,668
While the actual encryption and decryption process may falter a little in comparison, a quicker start in the beginning is well known to optimize a lot of frameworks.

5429
08:36:07,246 --> 08:36:10,916
DSA requires less storage space to work its entire cycle.

5430
08:36:11,566 --> 08:36:20,324
In contrast, its direct correspondent, i.e. RSA algorithm, needs a certain amount of computational and storage space to function efficiently.

5431
08:36:20,846 --> 08:36:26,532
This is not the case with DSA, which has been optimized to work with weaker hardware and lesser resources.

5432
08:36:28,014 --> 08:36:33,476
The DSA is patented, but NIST has made this patent available worldwide royalty free.

5433
08:36:34,062 --> 08:36:47,300
A draft version of the speculation FIPS 1865 indicates that DSA will no longer be approved for digital signature generation, but it may be used to verify signatures generated prior to the implementation date of that standard.

5434
08:36:51,054 --> 08:36:58,500
The RSA algorithm is a public key signature algorithm developed by Ron Revest, Adi Shamir, and Leonard Edelman.

5435
08:36:59,342 --> 08:37:11,204
The paper was first published in 1977, and the algorithm uses logarithmic functions to keep the working complex enough to withstand brute force and streamlined enough to be fast post-deployment.

5436
08:37:11,918 --> 08:37:19,588
RSA can also encrypt and decrypt general information to securely exchange data along with handling digital signature verification.

5437
08:37:20,080 --> 08:37:22,052
Let us understand how it achieved this.

5438
08:37:22,766 --> 08:37:30,980
We take our plain text message M, we pass it through a hash function to generate the digest H, which is then encrypted using the sender's private key.

5439
08:37:31,566 --> 08:37:36,000
This is appended to the original plain text message and sent over to the receiver.

5440
08:37:36,974 --> 08:37:47,200
Once the receiver receives the bundle, we can pass the plain text message to the same hash function to generate a digest and the ciphertext can be decrypted using the public key of the sender.

5441
08:37:47,360 --> 08:37:49,220
The remaining hashes are compared.

5442
08:37:49,520 --> 08:37:54,880
If the values match, then the data integrity is verified and the sender is authenticated.

5443
08:37:55,086 --> 08:38:03,492
Apart from digital signatures, the main case of RSA is encryption and decryption of private information before being transmitted across communication channel.

5444
08:38:04,206 --> 08:38:06,560
This is where the data encryption comes into play.

5445
08:38:06,894 --> 08:38:12,292
When using RSA for encryption and decryption of general data, it reverses the key set usage.

5446
08:38:12,686 --> 08:38:21,412
Unlike signature verification, it receives the receiver's public key to encrypt the data and uses the receiver's private key in decrypting the data.

5447
08:38:21,806 --> 08:38:25,028
Thus, there is no need to exchange any keys in this scenario.

5448
08:38:25,294 --> 08:38:28,560
There are two broad components when it comes to RSA cryptography.

5449
08:38:28,720 --> 08:38:30,240
One of them is key generation.

5450
08:38:30,480 --> 08:38:38,468
Key generation employs a step of generating the private and the public keys that are going to be used for encrypting and decrypting the data.

5451
08:38:39,040 --> 08:38:42,400
The second part is the encryption and decryption functions.

5452
08:38:42,560 --> 08:38:49,156
These are the ciphers and steps that need to be run when scrambling the data or recovering the data from the ciphertext.

5453
08:38:49,614 --> 08:38:52,868
You will now understand each of these steps in our next subtopic.

5454
08:38:53,230 --> 08:39:03,140
Keeping the previous two concepts in mind, let us go ahead and see how the entire process works, starting from creating the key pair to encrypting and decrypting the information.

5455
08:39:04,160 --> 08:39:10,212
You need to generate the public and private keys before running the functions to generate ciphertext and plaintext.

5456
08:39:10,510 --> 08:39:13,956
They use certain variables and parameters, all of which are explained.

5457
08:39:14,640 --> 08:39:19,300
We first use two large prime numbers which can be denoted by P and Q.

5458
08:39:20,078 --> 08:39:27,748
We can compute the value of n as n equal to p into q and compute the value of z as p minus 1 into q minus 1.

