6550
10:44:32,302 --> 10:44:35,332
Let us now understand what Nmap is from a layman's perspective.

6551
10:44:38,094 --> 10:44:43,972
Nmap, which stands for Network Mapper, is a free and open source utility for network discovery and security auditing.

6552
10:44:44,878 --> 10:44:53,764
Many systems and network administrators also find it useful for tasks such as network inventory, managing service upgrade schedules, and monitoring host and service uptime.

6553
10:44:54,510 --> 10:45:02,852
The program is most commonly used via a command line interface and is available for many different operating systems such as Linux, FreeBSD, and Gentoo.

6554
10:45:04,238 --> 10:45:11,620
It is most beneficial in the early stages of ethical hacking where a hacker must figure the possible entry point to a system before running the necessary exploits.

6555
10:45:12,718 --> 10:45:17,444
Nmap was developed for enterprise scale networks and can scan through thousands of connected devices.

6556
10:45:17,870 --> 10:45:22,660
However, in recent years Nmap is being increasingly used by smaller companies as well.

6557
10:45:23,566 --> 10:45:36,996
Nmap uses raw IP packets in novel ways to determine what hosts are available on the network, what services these hosts are offering, what operating systems they are running, what type of packet filters and firewalls are in use, and dozens of other characteristics.

6558
10:45:37,806 --> 10:45:42,308
It was designed to rapidly scan large networks but works fine against single hosts as well.

6559
10:45:42,990 --> 10:45:50,020
The rise of the IoT in particular now means that networks used by these companies have become more complex and therefore harder to secure.

6560
10:45:50,414 --> 10:45:59,588
Since every application that connects to a network needs to do so via a port, the wrong port or server configuration can open a can of worms that leads to a thorough breach of the system.

6561
10:45:59,982 --> 10:46:13,060
The recent emergence of IoT botnets like Mirai has also simulated interest in Nmap, not least because of its ability to interrogate devices connected via the UPNP protocol, but also to highlight any devices that may be malicious.

6562
10:46:14,542 --> 10:46:21,476
Now that we understand what Nmap is, let us take a look at the workflow of how an ethical hacker uses this tool in penetration testing.

6563
10:46:23,278 --> 10:46:29,540
At a practical level, Nmap is used to provide detailed real-time information on your networks and on the devices connected to them.

6564
10:46:30,126 --> 10:46:38,724
We have the hacker running Nmap on a system with a Victim machine running a standard installation of the operating system, be it Windows, Mac OS or Linux.

6565
10:46:40,718 --> 10:46:46,020
The Nmap interface will send specially crafted packets to generate some reply from the victim machine.

6566
10:46:46,734 --> 10:46:54,820
The victim machine in return will send some information back to the Nmap host with replying some of the services and hosts that are being run on the computers.

6567
10:46:55,758 --> 10:47:01,616
Nmap allows the network admins to find which devices are running, discover some open ports and other services.

6568
10:47:01,902 --> 10:47:07,396
This in turn helps discover the vulnerabilities and the possible entry points for hackers to breach into.

6569
10:47:21,136 --> 10:47:21,456
The

6570
10:47:21,518 --> 10:47:29,348
ping sweep is a simple type of Nmap scan where it pings to all the available IP addresses to check which IPs respond to ICMP protocol.

6571
10:47:30,190 --> 10:47:35,396
If the users need to know only the number of IP addresses and not many details, the Ping Sweep is very useful.

6572
10:47:35,918 --> 10:47:39,428
It's fast and hence the results to be known are fetched very easily.

6573
10:47:42,382 --> 10:47:46,788
The SYN scan is the most useful type of Nmap scan which does work very quietly.

6574
10:47:47,086 --> 10:47:53,444
It sends an SYN packet via the TCP protocol or the transfer control protocol to all the intended ports.

6575
10:47:53,998 --> 10:47:58,468
If an acknowledgement pack is received to the system, it is sure that a port is opened there.

6576
10:47:58,990 --> 10:48:02,276
No response means that the port is either closed or not available.

6577
10:48:02,830 --> 10:48:07,652
Here the acknowledgement pack is not sent back to the system, assuming that the connection is not valid.

6578
10:48:08,558 --> 10:48:15,172
The scan is not shown in most of the scan logs and hence it is safe to use SYN scan to identify the open ports.

6579
10:48:17,102 --> 10:48:24,164
The TCP Connect scan is similar to SYN scan in many aspects as it uses the TCP layer to send packets and is passed to all the ports.

6580
10:48:24,784 --> 10:48:29,444
Here, the difference is that the full connection is done by sending the acknowledgement packets back.

6581
10:48:30,030 --> 10:48:36,484
The logs can easily find the TCP scan and need more power from the machines to do the work, but it is more accurate.

6582
10:48:37,678 --> 10:48:47,652
If all the accesses related to the OS are available to the user, it is better to do a TCP scan than an SIN scan as all the low-level and high-level accesses are required for the scan.

6583
10:48:48,334 --> 10:48:54,084
The network is also loaded more and hence users must be careful about overloading the system and the networks.

6584
10:48:56,142 --> 10:49:01,504
The idle scan is really used to check whether any malicious attacks are planned on any particular network.

6585
10:49:02,126 --> 10:49:06,884
Users need not control the external host, but an IP address and a port should be given to the same.

6586
10:49:07,598 --> 10:49:09,988
All other requirements are taken from the scanner itself.

6587
10:49:11,534 --> 10:49:17,188
The RPC scans or remote procedure calls are done by hackers to make the system vulnerable to virus attacks.

6588
10:49:17,710 --> 10:49:22,660
It is thus necessary to know whether our systems answer such calls and make our system open to malware.

6589
10:49:23,694 --> 10:49:28,804
RPC scan is done to check this by finding the ports open with certain commands being run by RPC.

6590
10:49:30,126 --> 10:49:37,124
The Windows scan is a simple scan where the application scans the acknowledgement packets received from the ports once the SYN packets are sent.

6591
10:49:37,646 --> 10:49:45,508
If there are any abnormalities in the ACK packets received, the scan reports the same and helps in recognizing which ports are functioning in a different manner.

6592
10:49:47,054 --> 10:49:50,820
The bound scan is used to check the security in the file transfer protocol layer.

6593
10:49:51,534 --> 10:50:01,060
FTP layers mostly do not accept any packets, and once it is rejected from the FTP layers, there are chances that it might be sent to an internal layer so that it can access the internal machines.

6594
10:50:01,774 --> 10:50:08,996
Bound scan checks this loophole by doing exactly the same process and identifies whether our FTP layer is open to vulnerability or not.

6595
10:50:10,638 --> 10:50:18,980
The FIN scan is similar to SYN scan where the system that sends the packets receives the response back and it will be mostly be a TCP FIN packet.

6596
10:50:19,694 --> 10:50:25,092
If the system sends an RST packet, it is a false alarm and users need not be worried about the same.

6597
10:50:26,926 --> 10:50:36,228
The null scan is useful for other systems than Windows where the systems can easily identify what kind of packets they have received and respond back with either TCP packets or non responses.

6598
10:50:36,974 --> 10:50:41,984
Null scans are not useful for Windows as they may not always produce the desired results.

6599
10:50:42,862 --> 10:50:53,380
When it comes to looking at alternatives, there is a wide range of free network monitoring utilities as well as free open source vulnerability scanners available to network administrators and security auditors.

6600
10:50:54,286 --> 10:51:00,644
What makes Nmap stand out as the tool IT and network security managers need to know is its flexibility and power.

6601
