7933
13:27:42,544 --> 13:27:55,012
Therefore, SLAs specify the underlying operational specifics for agreed upon services which, if measured and managed, will deliver the commitments that meet customer expectations.

7934
13:27:55,504 --> 13:27:59,428
The main area of coverage is IT service management.

7935
13:27:59,904 --> 13:28:04,676
We shall look at IS service management in the following few screens.

7936
13:28:05,550 --> 13:28:17,828
IT Service Management comprises processes and procedures for efficient and effective delivery of IT services relative to business expectations to ensure.

7937
13:28:18,384 --> 13:28:23,824
ITSM can be split into IT support services and IT delivery services.

7938
13:28:24,144 --> 13:28:40,548
IT support services include service desk, at times referred to as to technical support, help desk, incident management, problem management, configuration management, change management, system and infrastructure change, and release management.

7939
13:28:41,184 --> 13:28:52,708
IT delivery services include service level management, IT financial management, capacity management, IT service continuity management, and availability management.

7940
13:28:53,646 --> 13:29:03,076
Although each of these management areas is a separate process by its own right, each process is highly interdependent with other processes.

7941
13:29:03,424 --> 13:29:10,116
For instance, repeated incidents undergoing incident management may require problem management to be initiated.

7942
13:29:10,544 --> 13:29:21,348
Resolving the problem may require a change, change management process followed in infrastructure configuration item, which invokes the configuration management process.

7943
13:29:22,350 --> 13:29:33,700
By virtue of being a service organization, the success of the IS department is dependent upon satisfying users and adhering to targets set within the service level agreements.

7944
13:29:34,224 --> 13:29:48,292
A service level agreement, commonly referred by the acronym SLA, is an agreement between the IT organization, internal IT department, or external service provider, and the customer, the business.

7945
13:29:48,624 --> 13:29:57,984
It details the services to be provided, for example, email, ERP, Internet connectivity, Internet website hosting, etc.

7946
13:29:58,544 --> 13:30:04,292
IS service level agreements SLA, assist in better management of IS services.

7947
13:30:05,230 --> 13:30:17,956
An SLA will have various targets that will include availability, response time, financial performance, such as ensuring costs are reduced, and risk management through compliance with company's policies.

7948
13:30:18,304 --> 13:30:25,316
Others include keeping staff turnover at a particular number and overall improvement in efficiency and effectiveness.

7949
13:30:25,742 --> 13:30:32,356
We shall look at tools that can be used in measuring effectiveness and efficiency of IS service management.

7950
13:30:33,102 --> 13:30:41,444
Exception reports are automated reports that identify all applications that did not successfully complete or otherwise malfunctioned.

7951
13:30:41,838 --> 13:30:50,864
They may indicate poor application design, inadequate operations, instructions, operation support, operator training, etc.

7952
13:30:51,504 --> 13:31:00,452
System application logs can be generated from various systems and applications should be considered to identify all application problems.

7953
13:31:00,864 --> 13:31:05,988
The size and complexity of these logs make it difficult to manually view them.

7954
13:31:06,384 --> 13:31:13,092
There are various programs that have been developed to analyze and report on defined aspects and items.

7955
13:31:13,664 --> 13:31:19,824
Let us look at other tools of measuring the efficiency and effectiveness of an IS.

7956
13:31:21,678 --> 13:31:29,892
Operator problem reports are manual reports used by operators to log computer operations problems and their resolution.

7957
13:31:30,512 --> 13:31:39,332
IS Management should review operator actions to determine if they were appropriate andor whether additional operator training is required.

7958
13:31:39,872 --> 13:31:46,820
Operator work schedules are reports maintained manually by IS Management to assist in human resource planning.

7959
13:31:47,312 --> 13:31:54,116
Proper staffing of operations support personnel will assure that service requirement of end users will be met.

7960
13:31:54,766 --> 13:32:02,212
The CISA candidate should have knowledge of techniques for monitoring third-party compliance with the organization's internal controls.

7961
13:32:02,592 --> 13:32:09,764
It is essential to know the latest approaches in contracting strategies, processes, and contract management practices.

7962
13:32:10,192 --> 13:32:19,204
Outsourcing IT can help reduce costs and or complement an enterprise's own expertise, but may introduce additional risks.

7963
13:32:19,822 --> 13:32:24,516
The following slide lists the main areas covered in this knowledge statement.

7964
13:32:25,102 --> 13:32:34,468
The key areas covered in this knowledge are sourcing practices, IS roles and responsibilities, and reviewing contractual commitments.

7965
13:32:34,926 --> 13:32:41,348
Note that these areas have already been covered in domain two, governance and management of IT.

7966
13:32:42,286 --> 13:32:50,404
The CISA candidate should have knowledge of operations and end user procedures for managing scheduled and non-scheduled processes.

7967
13:32:50,832 --> 13:32:56,868
Operations management is critical in providing effective, efficient, and appropriate technical solutions.

7968
13:32:57,312 --> 13:33:16,352
The roles and responsibilities of operations management represent a high risk, not only to the day-to-day running of the IT organization, but to the protection of information assets both in the areas of restricting access to authorized people and the availability of IT.

7969
13:33:16,832 --> 13:33:26,980
The main areas to be covered here are information system operations, network infrastructure, and implementation reviews and scheduling reviews.

7970
13:33:27,406 --> 13:33:31,300
We shall look at information system operations in the next screen.

7971
13:33:32,654 --> 13:33:43,812
IT operations are processes and activities that support and manage the entire infrastructure, systems, applications, and data focusing on day-to-day activities.

7972
13:33:44,272 --> 13:33:50,592
Infrastructure Operations Management is responsible for accurate and efficient IS operations.

7973
13:33:51,086 --> 13:34:08,804
The tasks of IT operations staff include executing and monitoring scheduled jobs, facilitating timely backup of computer files, monitoring unauthorized access and use of sensitive data, and monitoring and reviewing the extents of adherence to IT.

7974
13:34:09,312 --> 13:34:24,228
Others include the operations procedures as established by IS and Business Management, participating in Test of disaster recovery plans, DRPs, and facilitating troubleshooting and incident handling.

7975
13:34:24,832 --> 13:34:29,764
We shall continue with information system operations in the following screen.

7976
13:34:30,574 --> 13:34:40,432
Procedures detailing instructions for operational tasks and procedures coupled with appropriate IS management oversight are necessary parts of IS control.

7977
13:34:41,392 --> 13:34:58,660
This documentation should include procedures for operations, monitoring systems and applications, detecting systems and applications errors and problems, backup and recovery, handling IT problems and unresolved issues, among other procedures.

7978
13:34:59,054 --> 13:35:02,788
Let us look at job scheduling software in the next screen.

7979
13:35:04,014 --> 13:35:08,000
Scheduling is making operations automated without any attendance.

7980
13:35:08,366 --> 13:35:19,172
The schedule includes the job that must be run, the sequence of the jobs that must be run, the sequence of job execution, and the conditions that cause problem execution.

7981
13:35:19,680 --> 13:35:22,692
Job should be scheduled according to SLA.

7982
13:35:23,374 --> 13:35:35,268
High priority jobs should be given optimal resource availability, while maintenance functions such as backup and system reorganization should, if possible, be performed during non-peak hours.

7983
13:35:35,950 --> 13:35:42,692
Job scheduling software is software used by installations that process large number of batch routines.

7984
13:35:43,040 --> 13:35:48,964
It sets up daily work schedules and automatically determines which jobs to be submitted for processing.

7985
13:35:49,520 --> 13:36:03,108
Advantages of job scheduling includes that information is set up only once, reducing chance of error, job dependencies are defined so that if a job fails, subsequent jobs relying on its output will not be processed.

7986
13:36:03,520 --> 13:36:12,240
In addition, such software will ensure records are maintained of all job successes and failures and there is less reliance on operations.

7987
13:36:13,134 --> 13:36:23,588
A CISA candidate is expected to have knowledge of the technology concepts related to hardware and network components, system software, and database management systems.

7988
13:36:24,014 --> 13:36:30,724
The IS auditor must be familiar with the functionality of information system hardware and its network components.

7989
13:36:31,200 --> 13:36:43,492
This includes understanding the importance of the physical part of IS IT solutions that support the organizational objectives and goals, as well as key control and risks involving system software.

7990
13:36:43,950 --> 13:36:56,548
Although the CISA exam does not test technical knowledge of the working of individual components, an understanding of the risks associated with and possible control functions of each component is expected.

7991
13:36:56,960 --> 13:37:08,548
An example of this is knowledge of the risk that router access passwords may be shared, but that if properly programmed, passwords can make a major contribution to network resilience.

7992
13:37:09,070 --> 13:37:13,732
The following screen lists the main areas of coverage in this knowledge statement.

7993
13:37:14,606 --> 13:37:28,900
Main areas of coverage are IS network infrastructure, hardware reviews, network infrastructure and implementation reviews, operating systems, utility programs, and operating system reviews.

7994
13:37:29,326 --> 13:37:33,572
Let us look at enterprise network architectures in the next screen.

7995
13:37:34,254 --> 13:37:38,160
Knowledge of enterprise network architectures is important.

7996
13:37:38,894 --> 13:37:50,372
This is because today's networks are part of a large, centrally managed inter-networked architecture solution of high-speed local and wide area computer network in an organization.

7997
13:37:50,800 --> 13:38:04,100
Such networks might include network segments or blocks which may include web-based front-end application servers, public or private, application and database servers, and mainframe servers.

7998
13:38:04,640 --> 13:38:20,160
Organizations may implement service-oriented architectures, SOA, with web software components using Simple Object Access Protocol, SOAP, and Extensible Markup Language, XML.

7999
13:38:20,640 --> 13:38:28,772
Within such an environment, information is highly accessible and available anytime, everywhere, and stored centrally.

8000
13:38:29,294 --> 13:38:34,404
Let us learn about the Internet and related terms in our next slide.

8001
13:38:35,438 --> 13:38:43,268
A simple way to understand the Internet is to look at what it is comprised of and some key terms used in relation to the Internet.

8002
13:38:43,822 --> 13:38:47,456
First, the Internet is a vast global network, NW.

8003
13:38:47,696 --> 13:38:51,012
The global network is comprised of interconnected networks.

8004
13:38:51,376 --> 13:38:55,524
The purpose of these interconnected networks is to exchange information.

8005
13:38:56,016 --> 13:38:59,588
There are also routers to direct traffic within the networks.

8006
13:38:59,936 --> 13:39:04,516
Routers use routing tables to direct traffic through the most efficient routes.

8007
13:39:05,134 --> 13:39:10,052
Internet service providers, ISPs, enable users to connect to Internet.

8008
13:39:10,446 --> 13:39:22,596
Networks are connected via least lines, integrated service for digital networks, ISDN, fiber, satellite, and regional networks are connected through Network Access Points, NAP.

8009
13:39:23,136 --> 13:39:26,340
No single person or organization runs the Internet.

8010
13:39:26,656 --> 13:39:33,764
It can be said to be an electronic anarchy that uses TCP IP protocol suite as the de facto standard.

8011
13:39:34,256 --> 13:39:39,460
Let us look at the common terms used in relation to the Internet in the following screen.

8012
13:39:40,174 --> 13:39:57,924
Common terms that you will come across in relation to the Internet include ISP, which stands for Internet Service Provider, NAP, Network Access Point that connects regional networks, traffic concentration point that refers to a point of convergence for many ISPs.

8013
13:39:58,416 --> 13:40:01,508
Telnet is a remote terminal control protocol.

8014
13:40:02,126 --> 13:40:08,772
DNS stands for domain name service that translates host names into IP addresses and vice versa.

8015
13:40:09,296 --> 13:40:12,016
DNS servers offer the translation service.

8016
13:40:12,336 --> 13:40:19,460
LAN or mainframes connected to the Internet, providing full access to the Internet is referred to as a direct connection.

8017
13:40:19,950 --> 13:40:23,588
Let us look at common internet terms in the next screen.

8018
13:40:24,366 --> 13:40:28,484
Online services allow users to tap the full power of the Internet.

8019
13:40:28,910 --> 13:40:32,176
Popular use of Internet is to download or upload files.

8020
13:40:32,336 --> 13:40:36,096
This is enabled through the File Transfer Protocol, FTP.

8021
13:40:36,496 --> 13:40:46,212
With anonymous FTP, anyone can get access with user identification, ID, and password, BW, although this is considered risky.

8022
13:40:46,736 --> 13:40:54,116
Simple Mail Transfer Protocol, SMTP, relates to or facilitates email exchange on the Internet.

8023
13:40:54,702 --> 13:41:05,380
Simple Network Management Protocol, SNMP, is a means to monitor and control network devices, manage configurations, performance, and Internet security.

8024
13:41:05,870 --> 13:41:09,604
Let us now discuss the World Wide Web in the next screen.

8025
13:41:10,574 --> 13:41:24,100
World Wide Web or WWW is a client server model where the client uses a browser, for example, Internet Explorer, to access a web server that could be running on NT or Unix operating system.

8026
13:41:24,576 --> 13:41:29,188
The World Wide Web is the fastest growing and most innovative part of the Internet.

8027
13:41:29,696 --> 13:41:38,212
Navigation of the WWW is done through typing URL of the site and use of hypertext links to jump to other sites.

8028
13:41:38,656 --> 13:41:50,756
World Wide Web uses Hypertext Markup Language, HTML, for web pages, and hypertext transfer protocol, HTTP, for web server browser communication.

8029
13:41:51,216 --> 13:41:57,508
Let us continue to look at common terms in relation to the World Wide Web in the following screen.

8030
13:41:58,030 --> 13:42:04,580
World Wide Web terms that you will come across include Common Gateway Interface, CGI scripts.

8031
13:42:05,136 --> 13:42:13,540
This is an executable, machine-independent software program that can be accessed by a web page to perform specific tasks.

8032
13:42:13,936 --> 13:42:17,220
Cookies help a web page to remember preferences.

8033
13:42:17,536 --> 13:42:25,092
A cookie is a message sent from a web server to a browser to identify users and prepare customized pages.

8034
13:42:25,536 --> 13:42:32,356
Applets are downloaded from web servers to execute in browsers, for example, to generate input forms.

8035
13:42:32,974 --> 13:42:37,860
In the following screen, we look at the client server model and its related terms.

8036
13:42:38,574 --> 13:42:46,084
In a client server architecture, an application is split to run on different computers, some on the client and some on the server.

8037
13:42:46,496 --> 13:42:48,804
These tasks are mutually dependent.

8038
13:42:49,136 --> 13:42:53,668
On the client side, the graphic user interface can be thin or thick.

8039
13:42:54,016 --> 13:43:00,900
The server is usually a multi-user system, such as mainframe, mini computer, or personal computer.

8040
13:43:01,326 --> 13:43:07,296
Some of the server functions include file, print, database communication, facsimile, etc.

8041
13:43:07,856 --> 13:43:15,684
Advantages if client server architecture includes distributing work among computers and sharing of system and data resources.

8042
13:43:16,096 --> 13:43:21,796
In the following screen, we will discuss different tiers of client server architectures and middleware.

8043
13:43:22,830 --> 13:43:33,476
A multi-tiered architecture can be used to show how tasks are shared among computers at the graphic user interface through business rules and application logic and database levels.

8044
13:43:33,808 --> 13:43:36,452
There are different client-server architectures.

8045
13:43:36,768 --> 13:43:41,803
These include two-tier, three-tier, and multi-tiered, more than two tiers.

8046
13:43:42,446 --> 13:43:45,968
Let us look at two-tier architecture in the following screen.

8047
13:43:46,350 --> 13:43:58,308
In a two-tier client-server architecture, there is a client, for example, graphic user interface and business rules, which forms the front end and the database server, which forms the back end.

8048
13:43:58,688 --> 13:44:11,088
Limitations of this architecture include a fat client syndrome, for example, big complex programs requiring more memory and CPU, and this architecture is not highly scalable.

8049
13:44:11,248 --> 13:44:14,980
For example, problems arise when more users are added.

8050
13:44:15,408 --> 13:44:19,172
Let us look at three-tiered architecture in the following screen.

8051
13:44:20,206 --> 13:44:30,724
In a three-tiered client server architecture, the front end is represented by a graphic user interface, while the back end is comprised of the application and database servers.

8052
13:44:31,088 --> 13:44:38,596
Advantages of this architecture compared to a two-tiered include a thin client and it's more scalable.

8053
13:44:38,798 --> 13:44:41,732
For example, can support thousands of users.

8054
13:44:42,128 --> 13:44:45,380
Let us discuss middleware in the following screen.

8055
13:44:46,158 --> 13:44:56,100
Middleware manages interaction between the graphic user interface and servers by offering identification, security, authorization, and directory services.

8056
13:44:56,528 --> 13:45:04,708
This in turn supports communication between clients and servers, load balancing, failover, component management, and queue management.

8057
13:45:05,102 --> 13:45:08,260
In the next screen we cover uses of middleware.

8058
13:45:09,166 --> 13:45:17,092
In the Transaction Processing Monitors Middleware, used to handle and monitor database transaction mainly does load balancing.

8059
13:45:17,568 --> 13:45:26,756
Middleware in Object Request Broker ORB technology enables use of shared, reusable business objects in a distributed environment.

8060
13:45:27,086 --> 13:45:30,928
For example, Korba and Microsoft's COMDCom.

8061
13:45:31,408 --> 13:45:40,612
In a remote procedure call, RPC Middleware is the protocol that enables client to execute a program on the remote computer, server.

8062
13:45:41,008 --> 13:45:50,084
Lastly, in messaging servers, middleware programs asynchronously prioritize, queue, process messages using dedicated servers.

8063
13:45:50,448 --> 13:45:54,724
Let us look at risks and controls of middleware in the following screen.

8064
13:45:55,822 --> 13:46:03,248
The major risk of middleware is loss of system integrity from multiple operating environments and lack of change control.

8065
13:46:03,728 --> 13:46:11,588
However, controls that can be used to manage this include testing systems adequately, managing modifications, and versions.

8066
13:46:11,968 --> 13:46:15,844
In the next screen, we move on to our computer hardware.

8067
13:46:16,686 --> 13:46:27,268
The diagram on the screen shows a broad classification of computer hardware components and the communication between the input, processing, and output components of a computer.

8068
13:46:27,648 --> 13:46:34,628
Input devices are used to capture data and information into the central processing unit of the computer.

8069
13:46:35,088 --> 13:46:42,180
The central processing unit comprises of the arithmetic logic unit, registers, and the main memory.

8070
13:46:42,528 --> 13:46:53,092
The diagram also shows that the central processing unit is connected to the output devices through which processed data and information is communicated to the user.

8071
13:46:53,568 --> 13:46:58,756
A secondary storage is also connected to the main computer central processing unit.

8072
13:46:59,248 --> 13:47:03,780
Each of these components is explained further in the following screens.

8073
13:47:04,686 --> 13:47:09,316
The central processing unit forms the processing components of a computer.

8074
13:47:09,648 --> 13:47:20,388
There is an arithmetic logic unit which performs mathematical and logical operations, a control unit, and internal memory used for processing transactions.

8075
13:47:20,910 --> 13:47:28,996
Input and output components pass transaction information to the computer and display or record the output generated by the computer.

8076
13:47:29,408 --> 13:47:37,220
Computer peripherals such as the keyboard and mouse are input only devices, whereas printers form output only devices.

8077
13:47:37,728 --> 13:47:45,728
Other components of a computer include motherboard, random access memory, RAM, and read only memory, ROM.

8078
13:47:46,128 --> 13:47:50,468
Let us now look at types of computers in the following few screens.

8079
13:47:51,470 --> 13:47:58,340
Computers can be categorized based on several criteria, mainly processing power, size, and architecture.

8080
13:47:58,704 --> 13:48:17,104
The different categories include supercomputers, high-end and mid-range servers, mainframe, mini computer, personal computers, PCs or microcomputer, notebook, laptop computers, smartphones, and personal digital assistants, PDAs.

8081
13:48:17,664 --> 13:48:28,864
Supercomputers are very large and expensive computers with the highest processing speed used for specialized purposes, use complex mathematical or logical calculations.

8082
13:48:29,024 --> 13:48:38,116
For example, huge computing power is used to determine weather patterns, such as probability of tsunami or earthquake occurring in a given location.

8083
13:48:38,446 --> 13:48:46,020
High-end and mid-range servers are multiprocessing computer systems capable of supporting thousands of simultaneous users.

8084
13:48:46,384 --> 13:48:50,884
These computers differ from mainframes by virtue of being less expensive.

8085
13:48:51,264 --> 13:49:04,196
The higher end ones utilize Uni Operating System OS and may be used as database servers, while the lower end ones utilize Windows OS and may be used as application print servers.

8086
13:49:04,878 --> 13:49:15,492
Mainframe computers are large, general purpose computers operating in a multi-user, multiprocessing environment and usually serves thousands of internal external users.

8087
13:49:15,904 --> 13:49:23,236
Many computers are used by mid-sized organizations and operate in a multi-user, multi-processing environment.

8088
13:49:23,598 --> 13:49:34,884
Personal computers, PCs, also known as microcomputers, are small, inexpensive computers referred to as PCs or workstations and designed for individual users.

8089
13:49:35,118 --> 13:49:39,044
They are inexpensive and use microprocessor technology.

8090
13:49:39,504 --> 13:49:50,116
Notebooks, laptop computers are lightweight, under 10 pounds, 5 kilograms, personal computers powered by normal AC connection or rechargeable battery pack.

8091
13:49:50,464 --> 13:49:52,996
They are preferred because of their portability.

8092
13:49:53,264 --> 13:50:01,584
However, local area network by being portable, they are vulnerable to theft or connectivity hijacked in a LAN wireless connection, etc.

8093
13:50:02,222 --> 13:50:09,508
Smartphones and personal digital assistance PDAs are small handheld digital devices the size of a calculator.

8094
13:50:09,824 --> 13:50:23,716
Also known as Palm Tops, used as a personal organizer and planners, telephone, fax have networking capabilities and can interface with personal computers and are used for field data collection and for communication.

8095
13:50:24,064 --> 13:50:27,076
They use pen-based stylus instead of keyboard.

8096
13:50:27,344 --> 13:50:36,324
They can also be used as schedulers, phone, address book, to-do list, and for office automation, spreadsheets, text editor, for example, MS Word.

8097
13:50:36,624 --> 13:50:39,332
Some have handwriting recognition capability.

8098
13:50:39,744 --> 13:50:47,140
In the next screen, we will continue our discussion of computer hardware and architectures and learn about peripheral devices.

8099
13:50:47,918 --> 13:51:00,708
Computer peripheral devices include input-output devices, audio video cards, CD-ROMs, read-only memory, DVDs, tape drives, disk drives, printers, and modems.

8100
13:51:01,166 --> 13:51:04,676
Some commonly used computer terminologies include the following.

8101
13:51:05,104 --> 13:51:12,708
Multitasking, which refers to running of two or more tasks concurrently by allocating time slots slices to each task.

8102
13:51:13,184 --> 13:51:18,148
Multiprogramming, on the other hand, refers to running two or more programs concurrently.

8103
13:51:18,574 --> 13:51:26,276
Multiprocessing is another term used when two or more processors share memory and execute programs simultaneously.

8104
13:51:26,624 --> 13:51:35,268
In multi-user or time-sharing environments, multiple users access and use computer and the operating system simultaneously.

8105
13:51:35,664 --> 13:51:43,428
Multi threading is describes a process in which several sub activities take place when a program is being executed.

8106
13:51:43,824 --> 13:51:50,500
There are numerous computer hardware developments that have occurred over time, as we shall see in the next screen.

8107
13:51:51,726 --> 13:52:04,868
Some of the developments in computer hardware include memory cards, flash drives, pen drive, write once and read many, Worm, Memory Cards, Flash Memory, and Universal Serial Bus, USB.

8108
13:52:05,408 --> 13:52:13,636
Memory cards, flash drives, pen drive are a combination of USB and memory card technology that is useful in storing information.

8109
13:52:14,062 --> 13:52:22,724
However, they pose very serious security risks to the stored data if they are lost or stolen and can be used to bypass bootable security checks.

8110
13:52:23,168 --> 13:52:28,448
Large amounts of data and information can be transferred to flash memory very fast.

8111
13:52:29,008 --> 13:52:38,692
Write once and read many worm includes compact disks, CDs, like the ones used for music, up to 80 minutes, and for data files, 700 megabytes.

8112
13:52:39,008 --> 13:52:44,932
Compact Disc recordable, rewritable, CDR, CDRW, are technically not a worm.

8113
13:52:45,168 --> 13:52:52,964
Another example is digital video disc DVD that uses optical storage technology with large capacity, gigabytes.

8114
13:52:53,328 --> 13:52:56,324
Let us look at other developments in the following screen.

8115
13:52:56,974 --> 13:53:01,220
Memory cards, flash memory are alternative devices to store information.

8116
13:53:01,488 --> 13:53:11,972
They are small, non-volatile, removable, and easy to erase and store data information in and can be used with other peripherals like cameras, personal digital assistants.

8117
13:53:12,448 --> 13:53:19,236
Universal Serial Bus USB is a serial bus standard that interfaces devices with a host.

8118
13:53:19,568 --> 13:53:29,188
It was designed to allow connection of many peripherals to a single standardized interface sockets and improve the plug and play capabilities by allowing hot swapping.

8119
13:53:29,728 --> 13:53:33,796
In the next screen, we look at common enterprise backend devices.

8120
13:53:34,670 --> 13:53:57,540
Print servers which consolidate printing resources for cost saving, file servers that manage organization-wide access to files and program, application program servers that host applications, and allow users or access to centralized programs, and web servers, which provide information and services to internal employees or external public customers through web pages.

8121
13:53:57,968 --> 13:54:06,564
A web server is accessed using a Universal Resource Locator, URL, for example, www.google.com.

8122
13:54:06,848 --> 13:54:09,380
It also utilizes DNS services.

8123
13:54:09,888 --> 13:54:16,420
Proxy servers provide an intermediate link between users and resources as opposed to direct access.

8124
13:54:16,848 --> 13:54:21,700
Database server is a repository containing raw data and applications.

8125
13:54:22,048 --> 13:54:25,284
Web servers process data for user consumption.

8126
13:54:25,728 --> 13:54:36,448
Appliances are specialized devices like firewall, IDS, switches, routers that could be used to support virtual private networks, load balancing, etc.

8127
13:54:37,134 --> 13:54:40,772
Now let us consider hardware risks in the next screen.

8128
13:54:41,486 --> 13:54:55,940
Computer hardware is exposed to the following risks data and media loss, loss of flash drives, which may lead to unauthorized access to data, corruption of data, which can simply be caused by improper unplugging of USB drives.

8129
13:54:56,462 --> 13:55:03,108
Viruses and other malicious software contain ininfected documents and which can spread via flash disks.

8130
13:55:03,488 --> 13:55:24,708
Malicious code include key loggers to capture passwords for hackers, data theft, where hackers, corporate spies, disgruntled employees may steal data, use of social engineering to gain physical access to personal computers, losses of confidentiality that may come as a result of losing a memory stick with confidential data.

8131
13:55:25,166 --> 13:55:27,300
This may lead to legal liability.

8132
13:55:27,728 --> 13:55:30,756
HIPAA requirements for health information.

8133
13:55:31,168 --> 13:55:35,364
Let us look at controls to safeguard hardware in the following screen.

8134
13:55:36,238 --> 13:55:41,956
In order to safeguard computer hardware against these risks, the following controls can be put in place.

8135
13:55:42,510 --> 13:55:44,964
Encryption fully covered in domain 5.

8136
13:55:45,248 --> 13:55:49,316
Educating security personnel to be aware of the risk in their environment.

8137
13:55:49,648 --> 13:55:54,596
To enforce the lock desktop policy, duration based on risk of environment.

8138
13:55:55,008 --> 13:56:00,548
Update the antivirus policy and enforce mandatory scanning of USB flash drive.

8139
13:56:01,038 --> 13:56:06,436
Granular control using Microsoft Active Directory Group policy to implement controls.

8140
13:56:06,768 --> 13:56:09,888
User awareness programs for culture behavior change.

8141
13:56:10,128 --> 13:56:15,236
Use only secure devices which have security integrated into devices when buying them.

8142
13:56:15,408 --> 13:56:25,252
Include return information, include text file with contact details, excluding company name, to allow whoever collects the flash drive to return it to the owner.

8143
13:56:25,648 --> 13:56:31,568
In the next screen, we look at radio frequency identification, commonly known as RFID.

8144
13:56:32,686 --> 13:56:38,372
RFID uses radio waves to identify tagged objects within a limited radius.

8145
13:56:38,656 --> 13:56:49,924
It uses a tag which is a microchip to store information with the identification of the product and an antenna that transmits information to the radio frequency identification reader.

8146
13:56:50,336 --> 13:56:52,804
There are two modes of powering the tags.

8147
13:56:53,198 --> 13:57:03,460
Active tags that are powered by own batteries utilize higher frequencies, wider communication radius, have more data capacity, are reusable, but are more expensive.

8148
13:57:03,776 --> 13:57:09,092
And passive tags that draw power from the radio frequency identification reader radiation.

8149
13:57:09,376 --> 13:57:20,292
It has basic information, 2 kilobytes, uses lower frequencies, smaller communication radius, less data capacity, is not reusable, but it is less expensive.

8150
13:57:20,814 --> 13:57:25,316
Let us look at the applications of RFID in the following screen.

8151
13:57:26,190 --> 13:57:30,308
Radio frequency identification can be used in the following areas.

8152
13:57:30,670 --> 13:57:36,932
Asset management, for example, in the library because it does not require optical line of sight.

8153
13:57:37,422 --> 13:57:45,572
Tracking, authenticity verification, matching, for example, two pairs that have to go together, avoid confusion later.

8154
13:57:46,062 --> 13:57:50,852
In process control, to initiate processes based on tag identifier.

8155
13:57:51,296 --> 13:58:02,884
Access control, for example, restricted areas and office doors access and supply chain management, SCM, for example, tracking from manufacturer to retail.

8156
13:58:03,376 --> 13:58:08,196
In the next two screens, we will look at RFID risks and controls.

8157
13:58:09,006 --> 13:58:20,036
Radio frequency identification risks include business process risk where interference with radio frequency identification will result to interference with business processes.

8158
13:58:20,622 --> 13:58:28,260
Business intelligent risk, where a competitor can gain information from radio frequency identification and use it to harm the business.

8159
13:58:28,782 --> 13:58:33,296
Privacy risk where personally identifiable information can be compromised.

8160
13:58:33,536 --> 13:58:36,736
For example, tagged items can be traced to an individual.

8161
13:58:37,056 --> 13:58:51,620
Externality risk, where, for example, an adversary gains unauthorized access to computers on an enterprise network through Internet Protocol IP via enabled RFID readers if the readers are not designed and configured properly.

8162
13:58:52,430 --> 13:59:10,820
To safeguard against these risks, security controls that can be put in place include management oversight, for example, by way of implementing policies, operational controls for administrators and users, for example, physical controls and technical, for example, using technology to monitor usage.

8163
13:59:11,216 --> 13:59:15,056
In the next screen, we briefly look at hardware maintenance program.

8164
13:59:15,758 --> 13:59:18,576
Hardware must be routinely cleaned and serviced.

8165
13:59:18,830 --> 13:59:22,884
There should be a maintenance schedule that coincides with vendor specifications.

8166
13:59:23,216 --> 13:59:27,456
Maintenance requirements vary based on complexity and performance workloads.

8167
13:59:27,616 --> 13:59:39,428
Maintenance program should document reputable service company for each component, maintenance schedule, maintenance cost information, and history, planned and exceptional maintenance.

8168
13:59:39,856 --> 13:59:44,516
IS management should monitor, document, and justify deviations.

8169
13:59:45,006 --> 13:59:48,228
Excessive maintenance costs are a risk indicator.

8170
13:59:48,736 --> 13:59:55,056
There are several hardware monitoring practices and hardware auditing practices that can be put in place.

8171
13:59:55,310 --> 13:59:59,236
Let us look at hardware monitoring practices in the following screen.

8172
14:00:00,270 --> 14:00:20,016
These include error reports that are used to record failures and corrective action taken, availability reports that check for downtime that can be caused by inadequate facilities, excessive maintenance, lack of preventive maintenance, utilization reports automated that document utilization of machine and peripherals.

8173
14:00:20,526 --> 14:00:32,836
For instance, utilization of over 85% may indicate overcapacity, while utilization of less than 95% may call for a review of resource, capacity, and schedules.

8174
14:00:33,326 --> 14:00:42,564
Asset management reports that include network inventory, for example, connected equipment such as personal computers, servers, routers, and other devices.

8175
14:00:42,976 --> 14:00:46,340
In the next screen, we will discuss hardware auditing.

8176
14:00:47,758 --> 14:01:13,412
Auditing of hardware covers capacity management procedures that ensure continuous performance and whether performance management is objective, performance, evaluation, procedures, availability, and utilization reviews, change management controls such as approval, planning, scheduling, communication, minimize impact on business, operator documentation and hardware availability, and utilization reporting.

8177
14:01:13,934 --> 14:01:18,436
In the next screen, we look at information systems architecture and software.

8178
14:01:19,182 --> 14:01:26,212
Information systems architecture and software components hierarchy, as shown in the first diagram, is as follows.

8179
14:01:26,640 --> 14:01:35,716
The application system that sits on top of software utilities and programs, which are above the nucleus that interfaces with hardware and firmware.

8180
14:01:36,080 --> 14:01:50,276
Therefore, between the user and the hardware information system architecture is defined to include user, application, operating system, and hardware interfacing in that order, as is also shown in the second diagram.

8181
14:01:50,800 --> 14:02:00,068
Operating systems OS, makes computing power conveniently available to clients, for example, utilities, applications, human users.

8182
14:02:00,400 --> 14:02:08,000
The operating system manages computer resources including processors, memory, disks, timers, I.O.

8183
14:02:08,240 --> 14:02:09,700
devices, and data.

8184
14:02:10,254 --> 14:02:15,364
The following screen offers a detailed look of operating system functions and parts.

8185
14:02:16,110 --> 14:02:23,360
Functions of the operating system include holding of programs that interface between the user, processor, and application.

8186
14:02:23,360 --> 14:02:29,920
software, provides the primary means of controlling the sharing and use of computer resources, maintains.

8187
14:02:29,920 --> 14:02:35,200
the integrity of the system, manages and controls execution of programs, and shields.

8188
14:02:35,200 --> 14:02:37,348
the user from the details of the hardware.

8189
14:02:38,000 --> 14:02:43,364
Operating system parts include kernel or nucleus and utility programs.

8190
14:02:43,790 --> 14:02:52,452
In the next screen, we look at the operating system nucleus and software as we continue our discussion on the computer operating system.

8191
14:02:53,134 --> 14:03:03,012
The operating system nucleus is a highly privileged, restricted area that runs in kernel mode supervisory state and resides in the computer memory all the time.

8192
14:03:03,360 --> 14:03:05,668
Other utilities are called in as needed.

8193
14:03:06,000 --> 14:03:16,548
It performs the basic functions associated with the operating system, which include process management, input-output management, memory management, and file management.

8194
14:03:17,040 --> 14:03:28,068
In process management, the nucleus does interrupt handling, process creation destruction, dispatching, process synchronization, and interprocess communication.

8195
14:03:28,560 --> 14:03:39,620
In input-output management, the nucleus manages printers, KBs, disk drives, CD-ROMs, and all other input-output devices connected to the computer.

8196
14:03:40,206 --> 14:03:44,836
In memory management, the nucleus does allocation and deallocation.

8197
14:03:45,200 --> 14:03:51,940
File management, which involves create, read, write, delete, open, and close, is also done in the nucleus.

8198
14:03:52,613 --> 14:03:55,908
Let us look at OS facilities in the following screen.

8199
14:03:56,878 --> 14:04:29,028
Operating system facilities include user interface, program execution, management, file management, for example, create, delete, read, write, resource scheduling between clients, input-output support that covers device independence, uniform interface, uniform naming, protection against unauthorized access, authentication, access control, monitoring, non-interference, accounting, error detection and recovery, concurrent processing, and data sharing.

8200
14:04:29,520 --> 14:04:32,964
Let us look at OS software in the following screen.

8201
14:04:33,390 --> 14:04:46,596
Operating system software includes access control, data communications, and database management system, program library management, tape and disk management, and network management, job scheduling and utility programs.

8202
14:04:46,960 --> 14:04:51,684
Over the following few screens, we will understand operating system integrity.

8203
14:04:52,398 --> 14:05:02,692
The objective of operating system integrity is to protect the operating system from interference and compromise, and to protect applications from other applications.

8204
14:05:03,120 --> 14:05:14,148
This involves the operating system protecting itself from deliberate and inadvertent modifications, and ensuring privileged programs are not interfered with by user programs.

8205
14:05:14,480 --> 14:05:26,404
For integrity, process isolation occurs to ensure that multiple processes are protected from each other, for example, writing into each other's memory, and that there is enforcement of least privilege.

8206
14:05:27,182 --> 14:05:40,868
Supervisory state refers to the most privileged state with complete unrestricted access to all system resources, for example, memory, devices, and privileged instructions, and ability to bypass any security features in place.

8207
14:05:41,168 --> 14:05:43,172
The kernel operates in this state.

8208
14:05:43,568 --> 14:05:49,808
Some system utilities also operate in this state, making them highly risky, requiring strict control.

8209
14:05:50,574 --> 14:05:54,404
User state is the most restricted and less privileged state.

8210
14:05:54,768 --> 14:05:57,540
Users address their own restricted memory space.

8211
14:05:57,968 --> 14:06:03,620
User state relies on code running on supervisory state to perform privileged functions.

8212
14:06:03,968 --> 14:06:05,988
Applications run in this state.

8213
14:06:06,448 --> 14:06:21,668
User state is critically dependent on IS management's authorization techniques employed to prevent non-privileged users from executing privileged instructions and on configuration of files, directories, registry for options and parameters.

8214
14:06:22,190 --> 14:06:29,188
This must be controlled, configured, and updated properly with latest patches to avoid compromise by perpetrators.

8215
14:06:29,646 --> 14:06:34,500
For example, IBM 390 sys1.parmlib win registry.

8216
14:06:34,958 --> 14:06:45,764
Control features or parameters allow standard software to be customized to diverse environments and provide effective means of determining how controls are functioning within operating system.

8217
14:06:46,208 --> 14:06:51,044
Control features should be appropriate to the organization's workload and control structure.

8218
14:06:51,488 --> 14:06:59,332
Activity logging and reporting is the collection of specified pieces of information for purposes of analyzing system functions.

8219
14:06:59,648 --> 14:07:13,668
Activity logging and reporting areas include date file versions used for processing, accesses to sensitive data, utilities and service aid usage, and database management system efficiency and security.

8220
14:07:14,062 --> 14:07:18,212
Let us now look at access control software in the following screen.

8221
14:07:19,182 --> 14:07:25,284
Access control software developed for the computer must be compatible with its operating system.

8222
14:07:25,728 --> 14:07:34,660
It is designed to prevent unauthorized access to data, use of system functions and programs, and updates or changes to data.

8223
14:07:35,008 --> 14:07:39,812
The software is designed to detect and prevent unauthorized computer access.

8224
14:07:40,368 --> 14:07:46,116
Data communication software is used to transmit data from one point to another.

8225
14:07:46,608 --> 14:07:55,044
Also used for conversion, for example, of codes such as ASCII, EBCDIC, and Unicode.

8226
14:07:55,488 --> 14:08:04,676
Components of communication software include sender and receiver, message and information to be communicated, and the medium or channel.

8227
14:08:05,166 --> 14:08:10,212
Let us continue looking at access control software in the following screen.

8228
14:08:10,830 --> 14:08:17,968
System components include transmitter, source, receiver, sync, and transmission path, channel, line.

8229
14:08:18,368 --> 14:08:23,168
It also includes local area networks, LANs, and wide area networks, WANs.

8230
14:08:23,648 --> 14:08:45,188
Applications here include electronic funds, transfer, EFT systems, electronic data interchange, EDI, office information systems like electronic message systems, bulletin board, etc . interfaces with operating system, application programs, database management systems, telecommunication systems, and network control systems.

8231
14:08:45,568 --> 14:08:49,284
In the next screen we will take a look at telecommunication links.

8232
14:08:50,158 --> 14:08:54,916
Telecommunication links or lines for networks can be either analog or digital.

8233
14:08:55,168 --> 14:09:00,868
They're classified in several ways according to the type of provider or the type of technology.

8234
14:09:01,198 --> 14:09:07,396
They can be divided into dedicated circuit, also known as leased lines, or switched circuit.

8235
14:09:07,808 --> 14:09:12,740
Let us discuss circuit switching and packet switching in the following screen.

8236
14:09:13,262 --> 14:09:24,260
Circuit switching mechanism is typically used over the telephone network, plain old telephone service, POTS, for example, Integrated Services Digital Network, ISDN.

8237
14:09:24,608 --> 14:09:31,556
Switched circuits allow data connections that can be initiated when needed and terminated when communication is complete.

8238
14:09:32,128 --> 14:09:36,644
This works much like a normal telephone line works for voice communication.

8239
14:09:37,088 --> 14:09:40,036
ISDN is a good example of circuit switching.

8240
14:09:40,608 --> 14:09:46,180
Packet switching is a technology in which users share common carrier resources.

8241
14:09:46,448 --> 14:09:55,716
Packet switching allows the carrier to make more efficient use of its infrastructure, and the cost to the customer is generally much lower than with leased lines.

8242
14:09:56,174 --> 14:10:01,124
The next screen covers baseband and broadband telecommunication links.

8243
14:10:01,518 --> 14:10:11,620
In baseband networks, the signals are directly injected on the communication link so that one single channel is available on that link for transmitting signals.

8244
14:10:12,142 --> 14:10:22,244
As a result, the entire capacity of the communication channel is used to transmit one data signal, and communication can move in only one direction at a time.

8245
14:10:22,608 --> 14:10:24,580
Half-duplex communication.

8246
14:10:25,168 --> 14:10:40,708
In a broadband network, different carrier frequencies defined within the available band can carry analog signals, such as those generated by image processors or a data modem, as if they were placed on separate baseband channels.

8247
14:10:41,168 --> 14:10:50,116
Interference is avoided by separating adjacent carrier frequencies with a gap that depends on the band requirements of the carried signals.

8248
14:10:50,528 --> 14:11:00,324
The conditions when simultaneous data or control transmission reception takes place between two stations is called a full duplex connection.

8249
14:11:00,848 --> 14:11:05,092
Let us now turn to the different types of networks in the next screen.

8250
14:11:05,710 --> 14:11:19,748
Personal area networks, PANS, is a microcomputer network used for communications in telephones, PDAs, printers, cameras, scanners, among other computer devices being used by an individual person.

8251
14:11:20,288 --> 14:11:27,716
Local area networks, LANs, are computer networks that cover a limited area, such as a home, office, or campus.

8252
14:11:28,208 --> 14:11:36,516
Wide area networks, WANs, are computer networks that cover a broad area, such as a city, region, nation, or an international link.

8253
14:11:37,008 --> 14:11:40,528
The internet is an example, the largest example of a WAN.

8254
14:11:41,248 --> 14:11:46,820
Metropolitan Area Networks, MANS, are WANs that are limited to a city or region.

8255
14:11:47,168 --> 14:11:52,004
Usually, MANS are characterized by higher data transfer rates than WANs.

8256
14:11:52,528 --> 14:12:01,348
Storage area networks, SANS, are a variation of LANs and are dedicated to connecting storage devices to servers and other computing devices.

8257
14:12:01,728 --> 14:12:05,796
SANS centralized the process for the storage and administration of data.

8258
14:12:06,288 --> 14:12:11,108
In the next screen, we will look at wireless networks, which are also a type of network.

8259
14:12:12,046 --> 14:12:21,188
Wireless technologies enable one or more devices to communicate without physical connections, that is, without requiring network or peripheral cabling.

8260
14:12:21,568 --> 14:12:36,068
The technology in wireless networks uses radio frequency transmissions, electromagnetic signals, through free space as the means for transmitting data, whereas wired technologies use electrical signals through cables.

8261
14:12:36,608 --> 14:12:44,644
Wireless networks serve as the transport mechanism between devices and among devices and the traditional wired networks.

8262
14:12:45,088 --> 14:12:52,356
Wireless networks are many and diverse but are frequently categorized into four groups based on their coverage range.

8263
14:12:52,768 --> 14:13:01,892
These are wide area networks, local area networks, wireless personal networks, and wireless ad hoc networks.

8264
14:13:02,350 --> 14:13:06,340
The following screen lists the risks of a wireless network.

8265
14:13:06,926 --> 14:13:22,980
Risks of wireless networks include interception of sensitive information, loss or theft of devices, misuse of devices, loss of data contained in the devices, distractions caused by the devices, and possible health effects of device usage.

8266
14:13:23,408 --> 14:13:26,756
In the next screen, we look at types of wireless networks.

8267
14:13:27,406 --> 14:13:39,396
Wireless wide area network, WWAN, this is the process of linking different networks over a large geographical area to allow wider IT resource sharing and connectivity.

8268
14:13:39,808 --> 14:13:45,380
Wireless wide area networks are connected via radio, satellite, and mobile phone technologies.

8269
14:13:45,808 --> 14:13:55,684
Wireless wide area network using radio satellite and mobile phone technologies can complement and compete with more traditional systems of cable-based networking.

8270
14:13:56,208 --> 14:14:10,756
IEEE 82.11's wired equivalent privacy encryption uses symmetric private keys, which means the end user's radio-based network interface card NIC, and access point must have the same key.

8271
14:14:11,168 --> 14:14:17,220
This leads to difficulties periodically involved with distributing new keys to each network interface card.

8272
14:14:17,568 --> 14:14:24,644
With static keys, several hacking tools easily break through the relatively weak WEP encryption mechanisms.

8273
14:14:25,088 --> 14:14:28,964
Wireless networks types continue in the next screen.

8274
14:14:29,838 --> 14:14:44,772
Wi-Fi Protected Access, WPA, WPA2, is newer security protocols that utilize public key cryptography techniques to provide effective authentication and encryption between users and access points.

8275
14:14:45,248 --> 14:14:53,252
Wireless personal area networks, WPANs, are short-range wireless networks that connect wireless devices to one another.

8276
14:14:53,648 --> 14:14:57,956
The most dominant form of WPAN technology is Bluetooth.

8277
14:14:58,448 --> 14:15:02,148
Bluetooth links wireless devices at very short distances.

8278
14:15:02,448 --> 14:15:09,060
The oldest way to connect devices in a WPAN fashion is using infrared IR communications.

8279
14:15:09,488 --> 14:15:12,900
Wireless network types continues in the next screen.

8280
14:15:13,550 --> 14:15:20,612
Ad hoc networks are networks designed to dynamically connect remote devices such as cell phones, laptops, and PDAs.

8281
14:15:20,928 --> 14:15:30,980
These networks are termed ad hoc because of their shifting network topologies, whereas WLANs or WPANs use a fixed network infrastructure.

8282
14:15:31,248 --> 14:15:40,836
Ad hoc networks maintain random network configurations, relying on a system of mobile routers connected by wireless links to enable devices to communicate.

8283
14:15:41,408 --> 14:15:55,460
Wireless Application Protocol, WAP, is a multilayered protocol and related technologies that bring internet content to wireless mobile devices such as personal digital assistants, PDAs, and cell phones.

8284
14:15:55,918 --> 14:15:58,980
The next screen discusses virtual private networks.

8285
14:15:59,470 --> 14:16:13,188
A virtual private network, VPN, extends the corporate network securely via encrypted packets sent out via virtual connections over the public internet to distant offices, home workers, salespeople, and business partners.

8286
14:16:13,648 --> 14:16:20,068
Virtual private network allows network managers to cost-efficiently increase the span of the corporate network.

8287
14:16:20,528 --> 14:16:40,196
They also allow remote network users to securely and easily access their corporate enterprise, corporations to securely communicate with business partners, supply chain management to be efficient and effective, and service providers to grow their businesses by providing substantial incremental bandwidth with value-added services.

8288
14:16:40,686 --> 14:16:43,556
The following screen lists VPN types.

8289
14:16:44,142 --> 14:16:55,588
The different types of virtual private networks include remote access VPN, which is used to connect telecommuters and mobile users to the Enterprise WAN in a secure manner.

8290
14:16:56,048 --> 14:17:08,068
Intranet VPN, used to connect branch offices within an Enterprise WAN and extranet VPN, used to give business partners limited access to each other's corporate network.

8291
14:17:08,654 --> 14:17:11,876
In the next screen, let us look at network components.

8292
14:17:12,622 --> 14:17:20,356
Network components include network hosts, transmission media, network infrastructure components, and network software.

8293
14:17:20,768 --> 14:17:24,580
Network hosts include servers, PCs, printers.

8294
14:17:24,928 --> 14:17:31,140
Transmission media includes fiber, coaxial cable, and copper UTP cable, among others.

8295
14:17:31,648 --> 14:17:50,884
Network infrastructure components whose significant network components include servers, routers, switches, hubs, modems, and wireless networks and network software that provide the rules for exchange and control of data and process of communication across the different components from end to end.

8296
14:17:51,406 --> 14:17:53,732
The following screen lists network services.

8297
14:17:54,158 --> 14:18:12,128
Some network services include file sharing, email services, print services, remote access services or terminal emulation, directory services, network management, dynamic host configuration protocol, DHCP, and domain name service, DNS.

8298
14:18:12,558 --> 14:18:35,236
But when evaluating security of cloud providers, it's very important to understand the distinction between the security measures that the cloud service provider implements and operates, which is known as the security of the cloud, and the security measures that the customer implements and operates, related to security of the customer content and applications that make use of the cloud service provider services.

8299
14:18:35,790 --> 14:18:38,084
And this is called security in the cloud.

8300
14:18:40,334 --> 14:18:49,124
Basically, the AWS Shared Responsibility Model defines which security controls are yours and which are the responsibility of AWS.

8301
14:18:49,934 --> 14:18:54,308
In other words, you decide the security for your applications that run in the cloud.

8302
14:18:54,640 --> 14:19:05,412
For example, which ports are open, which IP addresses can access your resources, what patches are applied to the operating systems, do you have encryption enabled, etc.

8303
14:19:06,094 --> 14:19:10,468
And AWS guarantees the global security of the AWS cloud.

8304
14:19:10,894 --> 14:19:15,840
For example, the hardware, the data centers, the networks, etc.

8305
14:19:16,398 --> 14:19:19,780
And this diagram here gives you an overview of who owns what.

8306
14:19:21,966 --> 14:19:25,572
There are some exceptions to the AWS shared responsibility model.

8307
14:19:26,094 --> 14:19:36,356
For example, with AWS managed services like RDS, DynamoDB, and Redshift, AWS is responsible for more than just the hardware and the networks.

8308
14:19:36,814 --> 14:19:43,200
In these cases, AWS is responsible for the security configuration, like patching, antivirus, etc.

8309
14:19:43,630 --> 14:19:47,556
And you're just responsible for account management and user access.

8310
14:19:49,870 --> 14:19:54,788
Amazon Web Services takes cloud security and compliance extremely seriously.

8311
14:19:55,502 --> 14:20:02,084
One of the biggest concerns of consumers is that the cloud is not secure, and it's something I hear time and time again.

8312
14:20:02,638 --> 14:20:14,532
But in fact, that could not be further from the truth, as AWS invests huge resources into securing the AWS cloud and making sure that it's compliant with the required assurance programs.

8313
14:20:16,526 --> 14:20:26,244
AWS Cloud Compliance allows customers to understand exactly what controls have been put in place by Amazon to maintain cloud security and data protection.

8314
14:20:27,054 --> 14:20:37,092
However, AWS doesn't take full responsibility, as for systems that are built on top of AWS cloud infrastructure, the compliance responsibility belongs to the end user.

8315
14:20:37,998 --> 14:20:43,748
AWS meets a large amount of the assurance programs for finance, healthcare, government, and many more.

8316
14:20:44,110 --> 14:20:47,940
And here's a list of some of the assurance programs AWS is compliant with.

8317
14:20:48,430 --> 14:20:52,047
And you can see there's some big names in there like HIPAA, FedRAMP and ISO.

8318
14:20:53,307 --> 14:21:00,087
But just because AWS have all these compliance assurance programs, it doesn't mean that your applications are compliant.

8319
14:21:00,287 --> 14:21:05,316
There's still stuff you'll need to do with your applications to make them fully compliant with these programs.

8320
14:21:07,214 --> 14:21:09,348
Let's start with infrastructure as a service.

8321
14:21:09,998 --> 14:21:19,460
EC2, S3 and VPC are completely under customer control, so you have to perform all the security configuration and management tasks.

8322
14:21:20,142 --> 14:21:23,040
The virtual instances are completely controlled by you.

8323
14:21:23,310 --> 14:21:26,148
You have root access, AWS does not.

8324
14:21:26,638 --> 14:21:37,444
AWS has no access rights to your instances or guest operating systems, and they cannot SSH or RDP into your servers, so they have no idea what's going on on your instances.

8325
14:21:38,094 --> 14:21:46,788
And you'll find this out if you ever raise a support ticket with AWS that they are unable to log on to your instances to help you with your problems, you have to do screen sharing.

8326
14:21:47,118 --> 14:21:50,372
So that's the security attribute that AWS provides.

8327
14:21:52,398 --> 14:22:00,836
AWS has a storage decommissioning process that is designed to prevent customer data from being exposed to unauthorized individuals.

8328
14:22:01,358 --> 14:22:08,836
Customer instances have no access to raw disk devices, and instead you're presented with virtualized disks.

8329
14:22:09,294 --> 14:22:19,492
AWS proprietary disk virtualization layer automatically resets every block of storage used by the customer so that customer's data is never exposed to another.

8330
14:22:20,046 --> 14:22:29,700
In a similar way, memory is also scrubbed by the hypervisor when it's unallocated from a host and is unavailable for use again until it is completely scrubbed.

8331
14:22:31,726 --> 14:22:39,940
With the network, AWS protects against denial of service, man in the middle, IP spoofing, port scanning, and packet sniffing.

8332
14:22:40,654 --> 14:22:52,132
It prevents IP spoofing by AWS controlled, host-based firewall infrastructure, and it will not allow an instance to send traffic with a source IP address or MAC address other than its own.

8333
14:22:52,846 --> 14:23:03,460
Denial of service is prevented through the use of security groups, access control lists so that you can minimize public entry points and reduce the surface area of your applications.

8334
14:23:04,078 --> 14:23:15,076
You can protect databases and non-internet facing resources in private subnets and use Bastion servers for SSH and RDP access to instances hidden in private subnets.

8335
14:23:15,726 --> 14:23:20,964
You can put your elastic load balancers in security groups with inbound and outbound restrictions.

8336
14:23:22,702 --> 14:23:32,752
Amazon EC2 provides a firewall solution that by default is configured in deny all mode, so you have to open up the ports you require for your applications to work.

8337
14:23:33,134 --> 14:23:42,628
With network security, you can use HTTPS instead of HTTP, and you can use VPN to provide encrypted tunnel access to AWS.

8338
14:23:44,174 --> 14:23:53,092
AWS allows you to perform vulnerability scans, but you have to request permission in advance to perform one, and you have to limit it to your own instances.

8339
14:23:53,550 --> 14:23:55,552
And this is something you may see on the exam.

8340
14:23:55,632 --> 14:23:59,552
It's a popular question, are you able to run vulnerability scans?

8341
14:23:59,822 --> 14:24:03,812
And the answer is yes, but only after you've got permission from Amazon.

8342
14:24:04,942 --> 14:24:10,180
People often ask, is the Amazon corporate network segregated from the AWS network?

8343
14:24:10,542 --> 14:24:13,444
And the answer is yes, using network security.

8344
14:24:13,902 --> 14:24:22,052
Different instances running on the same physical machine are isolated from each other via the Xen hypervisor, which is what AWS uses.

8345
14:24:22,606 --> 14:24:31,396
Also, the AWS firewall resides within the hypervisor layer, between the physical network interface and the instance's virtual interface.

8346
14:24:31,854 --> 14:24:41,152
So all packets have to pass through this layer, meaning that any instances running alongside have no more access to that instance than any other host on the internet.

8347
14:24:41,774 --> 14:24:45,252
Physical RAM is also separated using similar mechanisms.

8348
14:24:46,926 --> 14:24:51,236
AWS provides multiple options to secure your user credentials.

8349
14:24:51,822 --> 14:25:02,212
You have good old username and passwords, we have multi-factor authentication, then there's the access keys and the key pairs, and also X509 certificates.

8350
14:25:02,702 --> 14:25:14,244
And these are a way of making media online secure by providing a certificate and a private key to a user so that only the user with the key and the certificate can view the media that you've placed online.

8351
14:25:15,566 --> 14:25:24,740
AWS provides the ability to encrypt with AES256 encryption so that data on EC2 instances or EBS storage is encrypted.

8352
14:25:25,230 --> 14:25:31,492
For it to happen with low latency and to be efficient, it's only available on the more powerful instance types.

8353
14:25:32,750 --> 14:25:36,420
AWS offers SSL termination on load balancers.

8354
14:25:36,974 --> 14:25:44,292
So this means any traffic that passes between elastic load balancers and web servers is unencrypted, so the load is taken off them.

8355
14:25:44,686 --> 14:25:54,468
So you can ensure that your data is fully encrypted all the way into Amazon and then once it's in there it can be unencrypted to speed up and make your applications work faster.

8356
14:25:55,822 --> 14:26:01,956
AWS Direct Connect provides an alternative to using the internet to utilising AWS Cloud Services.

8357
14:26:02,574 --> 14:26:07,236
So in all the demonstrations we've been using, I've been connecting to AWS via my web browser.

8358
14:26:07,758 --> 14:26:10,820
However, if you use Direct Connect, you can change this.

8359
14:26:11,470 --> 14:26:20,644
So data that would have previously been transported over the internet can now be delivered for a private network connection between AWS and your data center or your corporate network.

8360
14:26:21,070 --> 14:26:31,312
So it allows you to access public services such as S3 and private resources such as EC2 running within your VPC using your private IP space.

8361
14:26:31,758 --> 14:26:37,604
It also means you can extend your IP address range of your office into your virtual private cloud.

8362
14:26:39,150 --> 14:26:46,660
AWS Cloud Trail is a web service that records AWS API calls for your account and delivers log files to you.

8363
14:26:47,598 --> 14:27:01,188
The recorded information includes the identity of the API caller, the time of the API call, the source IP address of the API caller, the request parameters, and the response elements returned by the AWS service.

8364
14:27:02,158 --> 14:27:10,820
The AWS API call history produced by CloudTrail enables security analysis, resource change tracking, and compliance auditing.

8365
14:27:11,374 --> 14:27:23,204
Now as you know, auditing is a hot topic in today's IT world, so enabling CloudTrail to do this for you is very important, and you don't need to know much more about Cloud Trail other than what it's for and why you would use it.

8366
14:27:25,070 --> 14:27:31,748
Amazon CloudWatch is a monitoring service for AWS Cloud Resources and the applications you run on AWS.

8367
14:27:32,494 --> 14:27:39,332
CloudWatch enables monitoring for EC2 and other Amazon Cloud services so you can get alerts when things go wrong.

8368
14:27:40,046 --> 14:27:56,228
You can use Amazon CloudWatch to collect and track metrics, so you can get system-wide visibility into resource utilization, application performance, and overall operational health, and you can use these insights to react and keep your applications running smoothly.

8369
14:27:58,062 --> 14:28:00,112
CloudWatch offers two types of monitoring.

8370
14:28:00,334 --> 14:28:11,748
There's basic monitoring, which is included free of charge and polls every five minutes and gives you 10 metrics, five gigabytes of data ingestion and five gigabytes of data storage.

8371
14:28:12,352 --> 14:28:23,108
Then there's detailed monitoring, and this costs more as a price per instance per month, but it polls every minute, so if you want more detailed monitoring, then you can pay for it.

8372
14:28:24,270 --> 14:28:32,912
AWS CloudWatch allows you to record metrics for services such as EBS, EC2, Elastic Load Balancer, and Amazon S3.

8373
14:28:33,582 --> 14:28:40,388
And using these metrics, you can add them to dashboards to give visual or text-based notifications of what's going on.

8374
14:28:40,797 --> 14:28:44,868
And This is a diagram of a dashboard in Amazon CloudWatch.

8375
14:28:45,518 --> 14:28:52,644
Metrics are at the hypervisor level, so you can get things like CPU, disk, network, but you cannot see memory usage.

8376
14:28:53,326 --> 14:28:57,188
Metrics appear as you add more resources to your AWS account.

8377
14:28:58,510 --> 14:29:04,356
You can create events based on your CloudWatch monitoring, for example triggering Lambda functions.

8378
14:29:05,102 --> 14:29:14,020
So perhaps if an EBS volume fills up, you could trigger an event so that data is removed and archived from the volume or a new volume is created.

8379
14:29:14,318 --> 14:29:15,940
So there's many things you can do.

8380
14:29:17,102 --> 14:29:24,740
You can install CloudWatch agents on EC2 instances, and this will send monitoring data about the instance to CloudWatch.

8381
14:29:25,262 --> 14:29:32,132
So you can monitor things like HTTP response codes in Apache or you can count exceptions in application logs.

8382
14:29:35,022 --> 14:29:38,628
You can set alarms to warn based on resource usage.

8383
14:29:39,118 --> 14:29:44,196
For example, if CPU utilization is too high, then it could send a notification.

8384
14:29:44,910 --> 14:29:52,388
It can also auto-scale, so if your CPU is maxed out, you can get another instance launched to take care of some of the load.

8385
14:29:53,070 --> 14:30:01,796
Or you can send CloudWatch monitoring alarms to EC2 actions, to say recover an instance or reboot an instance if something happens.

8386
14:30:02,606 --> 14:30:07,072
You can also use alarms to shut down instances, it isn't just used for starting them up.

8387
14:30:07,342 --> 14:30:11,812
So if you have idle instances, you can get CloudWatch to shut them down for you.

8388
14:30:14,510 --> 14:30:20,832
In this demonstration, we're going to take a look at AWS CloudWatch and how we can use it to shut down idle instances.

8389
14:30:29,710 --> 14:30:36,868
So before I started this demonstration, I launched an Amazon Linux instance and let it run for 10 minutes just so we'd have some data.

8390
14:30:37,358 --> 14:30:41,092
So now I'm going to go to Management Tools and click on CloudWatch.

8391
14:30:43,278 --> 14:30:45,700
And this brings us to the CloudWatch dashboard.

8392
14:30:46,766 --> 14:30:56,004
So what we're going to do is we're going to create a new dashboard and we'll click on create dashboard so that we can have a look at the monitoring statistics for our new instance.

8393
14:30:56,430 --> 14:31:00,256
So let's just give it a name and we'll call it just simply learn.

8394
14:31:00,496 --> 14:31:01,700
Just for a change.

8395
14:31:03,278 --> 14:31:05,028
Okay, so it says create dashboard.

8396
14:31:05,582 --> 14:31:10,736
And now we get the option do we want to put text-base or metric graphs widgets to our dashboard?

8397
14:31:10,926 --> 14:31:12,676
And we're going to select metric graphs.

8398
14:31:13,102 --> 14:31:14,308
So we'll click on configure.

8399
14:31:15,022 --> 14:31:19,748
And then we're presented with all the CloudWatch metrics that I have available in my AWS account.

8400
14:31:20,270 --> 14:31:25,316
So obviously I've just launched an EC2 instance, so I'm going to click on EC2 metrics.

8401
14:31:26,414 --> 14:31:29,668
And my new server is called Simply Learn CloudWatch Demo.

8402
14:31:30,190 --> 14:31:34,736
So I want to add CPU utilization.

8403
14:31:34,896 --> 14:31:35,776
So let's click on that.

8404
14:31:35,918 --> 14:31:41,028
So let me create this widget, and then that now appears on our dashboard.

8405
14:31:41,582 --> 14:31:42,416
So we can add more.

8406
14:31:42,496 --> 14:31:54,820
So we click on add widget, we'll do another metric graph, we'll go another per instance metric, and let's choose network in and network out, and we'll create the widget.

8407
14:31:55,886 --> 14:32:00,496
Now as you can see there's not a lot happening here because I just launched this instance and I haven't really done anything on it.

8408
14:32:00,686 --> 14:32:04,736
But this is how you would create a dashboard, and it's quite useful and very easy to see.

8409
14:32:04,816 --> 14:32:07,812
So you can create a dashboard for your instance types.

8410
14:32:08,686 --> 14:32:10,656
So what we're also going to do is set up an alarm.

8411
14:32:10,862 --> 14:32:12,164
So let's go to alarms.

8412
14:32:13,102 --> 14:32:14,788
So now we want to create an alarm.

8413
14:32:15,086 --> 14:32:20,932
So we click on create alarm and we get to choose the metric we want to base the alarm on.

8414
14:32:21,136 --> 14:32:25,604
Now obviously we have an EC2 instance, so we're going to choose that option.

8415
14:32:25,966 --> 14:32:31,076
And we're going to do it based on CPU utilization of our Simply Learn CloudWatch demo instance.

8416
14:32:31,662 --> 14:32:34,244
So we'll click on that and we'll click next.

8417
14:32:34,894 --> 14:32:37,092
So now we get to give our alarm a name.

8418
14:32:37,296 --> 14:32:41,696
So just going to call it Simply Learn underscore alarm.

8419
