Just for the purposes of this demo, we can give it a description.

8420
14:32:45,614 --> 14:32:47,616
And now we get to set the alarm threshold.

8421
14:32:47,950 --> 14:33:02,468
So we're saying whenever CPU utilization is, and we're going to say less than or equal to 50% for one consecutive period, and remember a period is five minutes in basic monitoring, we want this alarm to fire.

8422
14:33:03,086 --> 14:33:04,176
So why would you do this?

8423
14:33:04,336 --> 14:33:11,588
Well imagine if you had a really high-powered server that we're using every night to do some highly intensive compute computations.

8424
14:33:12,366 --> 14:33:23,376
So you might know that it runs for a couple of hours and it's charging you quite a lot of money per hour to run, but when the CPU utilization drops below, say 50% or 10%, you know the job is complete.

8425
14:33:23,662 --> 14:33:26,404
So then you can get an alarm to fire and an action to happen.

8426
14:33:26,926 --> 14:33:31,696
So you could set a notification, so we could get it to send an email when this alarm fires.

8427
14:33:31,856 --> 14:33:36,100
We're not going to do that in this demonstration, but we can do an EC2 action.

8428
