8429
14:33:41,376 --> 14:33:48,772
So this instance is going to be stopped whenever the CPU utilization is less than 50% for five minutes.

8430
14:33:49,710 --> 14:33:51,376
And it's just telling us that there.

8431
14:33:51,630 --> 14:33:57,216
So now if I click on create alarm, and here is our alarm.

8432
14:33:57,296 --> 14:34:09,616
So it's just uh so it's telling us we're in alarm already because this instance has been up and running, it has monitoring data for the last five minutes, and it's saying the alarm has fired.

8433
14:34:09,934 --> 14:34:20,336
So let me click on it and we'll see why.0034 was less than equal to the threshold of 50%.

8434
14:34:21,166 --> 14:34:25,136
And it says when it's in alarm, it's going to stop the instance.

8435
14:34:25,838 --> 14:34:37,696
So if we go to the EC2 dashboard, and here we can see that the Simply Learn Cloud Watch demo instance has been stopped and it's in a status of alarm.

8436
14:34:37,998 --> 14:34:41,056
So it's telling us that our alarm fired and it took it straight down.

8437
14:34:41,376 --> 14:34:43,216
Obviously, that's a pretty dramatic example.

8438
14:34:43,296 --> 14:34:50,084
You wouldn't expect anything like that to happen in a real world, but it's a good way of seeing how alarms work.

8439
14:34:55,598 --> 14:35:05,956
AWS Trusted Advisor is an online resource to help you reduce costs, increase performance, and improve security by optimizing your AWS environment.

8440
14:35:06,638 --> 14:35:13,600
Trusted Advisor provides real-time guidance to help you provision your resources following AWS best practices.

8441
14:35:14,670 --> 14:35:25,700
AWS Trusted Advisor provides best practices or checks in four categories cost optimization, security, fault tolerance and performance improvement.

8442
14:35:26,478 --> 14:35:31,076
The status of the check is shown by using color coding on the dashboard page.

8443
14:35:31,374 --> 14:35:38,980
Red means action recommended, yellow investigation recommended, and green is no problem detected.

8444
14:35:40,942 --> 14:35:42,820
Let's start with cost optimization.

8445
14:35:43,374 --> 14:35:47,556
And using this function you can see how you can save money with AWS.

8446
14:35:47,950 --> 14:35:51,716
For example, you might see some reserved instances recommendations.

8447
14:35:51,950 --> 14:36:00,356
So if you have instances that have been running for a long time and they're always up and running, AWS might say, Well, move this to reserved instances and you'll save some money.

8448
14:36:00,942 --> 14:36:04,720
It'll also notify you about idle instances that aren't doing anything.

8449
14:36:05,097 --> 14:36:06,500
so You might want to shut these down.

8450
14:36:06,894 --> 14:36:14,180
Or perhaps even you might have some unassociated elastic IP addresses, which are costing you money because they're not allocated to an instance.

8451
14:36:16,110 --> 14:36:27,172
Trusted Advisor will help you improve the security of your applications by notifying you about gaps that can be closed, security features you're not using, and by examining your permissions.

8452
14:36:27,886 --> 14:36:32,740
So you might see a report saying that you have unrestricted access to certain ports.

8453
14:36:33,358 --> 14:36:37,796
So if you have your SQL Server port wide open to the internet, that's a bad thing.

8454
14:36:38,638 --> 14:36:48,644
It will tell you about S3 bucket permissions, maybe you have more read-write users than you need, and maybe you don't have multi-factor authentication set up on your root account.

8455
14:36:50,606 --> 14:37:03,460
Trusted Advisor will help you increase the availability and redundancy of your applications by suggesting that you take advantage of things like auto-scaling, health checks, multi-availability zones and backup capabilities.

8456
14:37:04,206 --> 14:37:13,284
So for example, you might have not taken a snapshot in a while, and Trusted Advisor will let you know that the age of your snapshots is too old and you should run some new backups.

8457
14:37:13,870 --> 14:37:18,596
It might suggest that you add more availability zones to your load balancer to make it more redundant.

8458
14:37:19,150 --> 14:37:22,980
And it also might suggest S3 bucket logging for your auditing.

8459
14:37:24,590 --> 14:37:35,652
Trusted Advisor will help you improve the performance of your service by checking your service limits, ensuring you take advantage of provisioned throughput, and monitoring for overutilized instances.

8460
14:37:36,622 --> 14:37:43,364
So if you have instances that are constantly maxed out, Trusted Advisor will let you know and suggest you move them to a different instance type.

8461
14:37:44,238 --> 14:37:51,620
It'll also let you know when your service limits are at 80% of the limit, so it gives you time to raise a ticket with AWS to increase it.

8462
14:37:52,206 --> 14:38:00,420
And if you have EBS magnetic volumes that are overutilized, it might be beneficial to switch to SSD and Trusted Advisor will let you know this.

8463
14:38:03,342 --> 14:38:11,876
This is a very quick demonstration to show you how AWS Trusted Advisor can help you optimize performance and security of your EC2 instances.

8464
14:38:20,110 --> 14:38:27,908
So if we click on management tools and go down to the bottom and click on trusted advisor, it brings up the trusted advisor dashboard.

8465
14:38:28,238 --> 14:38:32,356
Now I just ran this a couple of minutes ago, so to speed the demonstration up.

8466
14:38:32,718 --> 14:38:40,400
And you can see here's the four areas that we talked about in the lesson cost optimization, performance, security, and fault tolerance.

8467
14:38:40,718 --> 14:38:44,320
And then underneath it tells you whether you have any areas of concern.

8468
14:38:44,400 --> 14:38:48,580
So for cost optimization we haven't because I've got hardly anything running in this account.

8469
