14:40:33,774 --> 14:40:36,516
And this concludes the demonstration on trusted advisor.

8491
14:40:41,998 --> 14:40:46,628
You should and you can secure your virtual network at several different levels.

8492
14:40:47,246 --> 14:40:51,300
The most broadly scoped level of security is at the route table level.

8493
14:40:51,822 --> 14:41:00,932
Having a private subnet with no direct path to the internet is one of the best ways to protect your internal computing resources against unauthorized access.

8494
14:41:01,774 --> 14:41:04,260
The second level is the network ACLs.

8495
14:41:04,558 --> 14:41:08,804
Provide the ability to define default security behavior for your subnets.

8496
14:41:09,358 --> 14:41:14,404
The VPC or the subnet layer security is controlled by the network security team.

8497
14:41:14,894 --> 14:41:21,636
You can use security groups to control behavior at the instance or the ENI level, which is the third level.

8498
14:41:22,318 --> 14:41:36,964
At the fourth level, you can use a third-party host-based detection software that monitors individual EC2 instances for specific threats, such as malware intrusion, known operating system vulnerabilities, and security auditing.

8499
14:41:38,190 --> 14:41:57,540
According to AWS, you can use a network address translation or NAT instance in a public subnet in your VPC to enable instances in the private subnet to initiate outbound traffic to the Internet or other AWS services, but prevent the instances from receiving inbound traffic initiated by someone on the Internet.

8500
14:41:58,286 --> 14:42:02,980
The diagram on the slide represents the basic mechanics of how a NAT operates.

8501
14:42:03,502 --> 14:42:06,756
The network diagram has been simplified for the sake of discussion.

8502
14:42:07,086 --> 14:42:11,492
Regions, availability zones, routers, and gateways are not shown here.

8503
14:42:12,046 --> 14:42:18,180
In this example, a database server in a private subnet raises a request for a public internet resource.

8504
14:42:18,862 --> 14:42:27,492
Because of the routing table rules defined for the private subnet, AWS routes this non-local request to the NAT server via the NAT's private IP.

8505
14:42:28,142 --> 14:42:30,967
The NAT server performs the function of rewriting the request.

8506
14:42:31,438 --> 14:42:41,344
So to the external resource, it appears that the NAT is requesting this resource via its public IP of 154.10.1.3.

8507
14:42:41,934 --> 14:42:54,180
When the external resource responds, the NAT reverses the process, changes the packets originally addressed to the NAT's public IP address, and readdresses them to the private IP address of the database server.

8508
14:42:54,704 --> 14:43:02,564
This way, numerous private instances with only a private IP address can make public requests to the Internet via the NAT instance.

8509
14:43:03,182 --> 14:43:07,780
Keep in mind, the source and destination checking must be disabled on the NAT.

8510
14:43:08,974 --> 14:43:13,636
While configuring route tables for NAT, you have to keep in mind a few points.

8511
14:43:14,158 --> 14:43:18,020
You can create route tables and associate them each with a subnet.

8512
14:43:18,798 --> 14:43:22,660
Public subnets route non-local traffic through the Internet Gateway.

8513
14:43:23,374 --> 14:43:28,132
Private subnets route non-local traffic through the NAT instance or NAT gateway.

8514
14:43:29,070 --> 14:43:33,092
A common scenario that you might face with Nats is port forwarding.

8515
14:43:33,646 --> 14:43:38,340
You have learned how to use NAT to provide access for the private subnet to the Internet.

8516
14:43:38,958 --> 14:43:45,220
But what if you wanted to open up a specific resource in the private subnet and make it available from the Internet?

8517
14:43:45,646 --> 14:43:54,116
For example, assume that you have an internal defect tracking database running as a web server on port 80 on a machine in your private subnet.

8518
14:43:54,576 --> 14:44:02,820
You want to expose this resource to the internet via your NAT so that select customers and partners have visibility into your development process.

8519
14:44:03,662 --> 14:44:12,132
To enable this scenario using a NAT instance, you have to log in to the instance and configure it to forward all requests to port 80 of your NAT.

8520
14:44:13,006 --> 14:44:17,796
The traffic will be forwarded onto an Amazon EC2 instance in your private subnet.

8521
14:44:18,414 --> 14:44:22,148
However, the NAT gateway does not support port forwarding yet.

8522
14:44:22,862 --> 14:44:27,684
So if this feature is required in your infrastructure, you need to set up a NAT instance.

8523
14:44:29,326 --> 14:44:40,484
You can use a NAT instance in a public subnet in your VPC to enable instances in the private subnet to initiate outbound traffic to the Internet or other AWS services.

8524
14:44:41,102 --> 14:44:46,372
But you can prevent the instances from receiving inbound traffic initiated by someone on the internet.

8525
14:44:47,118 --> 14:44:54,308
This slide illustrates the communication between the instances running in the private subnet and the Internet through the NAT instance.

8526
14:44:55,182 --> 14:45:01,860
The main route table sends the traffic from the instances in the private subnet to the NAT instance in the public subnet.

8527
14:45:02,670 --> 14:45:06,852
The NAT instance sends the traffic to the Internet gateway for the VPC.

8528
14:45:07,246 --> 14:45:11,396
The traffic is attributed to the elastic IP address of the NAT instance.

8529
14:45:11,982 --> 14:45:15,492
The NAT instance specifies a high port number for the response.

8530
14:45:15,982 --> 14:45:23,620
If a response comes back, the NAT instance sends it to an instance in the private subnet based on the port number for the response.

8531
14:45:25,038 --> 14:45:31,748
To configure an Amazon EC2 instance as a NAT, you must enable IP masquerading on the instance.

8532
14:45:32,174 --> 14:45:39,716
It can be done manually or you can search for an AWS community AMI image that is pre-configured to serve as a NAT.

8533
14:45:40,462 --> 14:45:46,020
Ensure that you choose an image with the correct virtualization type for the instance family you are using.

8534
14:45:46,510 --> 14:45:53,188
For example, you cannot use a PV image for your NAT if you plan to deploy the NAT as a T2 instance.

8535
14:45:53,870 --> 14:45:57,828
Ensure that the source and destination checking is disabled on the NAT.

8536
14:45:58,446 --> 14:46:08,164
Source and destination checking is a feature on Amazon EC2 instances that informs the instance to drop network packets that are not specifically addressed to the instance.

8537
14:46:08,814 --> 14:46:17,060
As the job of a NAT instance is to serve as a proxy for the network traffic of other instances, this feature must be disabled on your NAT.

8538
14:46:18,286 --> 14:46:22,692
Keep in mind that a single NAT instance can become the single point of failure.

8539
14:46:23,054 --> 14:46:34,500
So if you are using a NAT instance and high availability is important for your setup, then consider implementing a two availability zone NAT instance design as a high availability failover solution.

8540
14:46:35,182 --> 14:46:38,692
It will prove more effective when used together with auto-scaling.

8541
14:46:39,310 --> 14:46:43,524
For web access, you can also use Squid Proxy architecture.

8542
14:46:44,622 --> 14:46:59,908
The most modern and recommended way to enable instances in a private subnet to connect to the Internet or other AWS services, but prevent the Internet from initiating a connection with those instances is to use a network address translation or NAT gateway.

8543
14:47:00,590 --> 14:47:06,244
A NAT gateway is a highly available and automatically scalable service managed by AWS.

8544
14:47:06,766 --> 14:47:09,892
However, a NAT gateway is not a free service.

8545
14:47:10,318 --> 14:47:14,244
A NAT gateway is charged for hourly usage and data processing.

8546
14:47:14,798 --> 14:47:19,716
In addition, Amazon EC two charges for data transfer also apply.

8547
14:47:20,526 --> 14:47:26,016
A NAT gateway is redundant inside the availability zone, but not in the whole region.

8548
14:47:26,574 --> 14:47:34,724
In the event that the availability zone of the NAT gateway is down, resources in the other availability zones will lose Internet access.

8549
14:47:34,958 --> 14:47:47,588
To create an availability zone independent architecture, you can create a NAT gateway in each availability zone and configure the routing to ensure that resources use the NAT gateway in the same availability zone.

8550
14:47:48,718 --> 14:47:55,140
A NAT gateway supports bursts of up to 10 gigabits per second or 10 gbps of bandwidth.

8551
14:47:55,758 --> 14:48:06,180
If you require more than a 10 Gbps burst, you can distribute the workload by splitting your resources into multiple subnets and creating a NAT gateway in each subnet.

8552
14:48:07,054 --> 14:48:10,340
A NAT gateway requires one elastic IP address.

8553
14:48:10,766 --> 14:48:16,292
You cannot disassociate an elastic IP address from a NAT gateway after it has been created.

8554
14:48:16,942 --> 14:48:30,948
If you need to use a different elastic IP address for your NAT gateway, you must create a new NAT gateway with the required address, update your route tables, and then delete the existing NAT gateway if it is no longer required.

8555
14:48:31,566 --> 14:48:39,204
A NAT gateway supports transfer control protocol, user datagram protocol, and the Internet Control Message Protocol.

8556
14:48:39,982 --> 14:48:43,172
You cannot associate a security group with a NAT gateway.

8557
14:48:43,726 --> 14:48:50,916
You can only use the security groups for your instances in the private subnet to control the traffic to and from those instances.

8558
14:48:51,822 --> 14:48:59,300
You can use a network ACL to control the traffic to and from the subnet in which the NAT gateway is located.

8559
14:48:59,758 --> 14:49:02,948
The network ACL applies to the NAT gateway's traffic.

8560
14:49:03,566 --> 14:49:08,740
A NAT gateway uses the ports 1024 through 65535.

8561
14:49:09,646 --> 14:49:19,524
When a NAT gateway is created, it receives an elastic network interface that's automatically assigned a private IP address from the IP address range of your subnet.

8562
14:49:20,142 --> 14:49:24,964
You can view the NAT Gateway's network interface in the Amazon EC2 console.

8563
14:49:25,742 --> 14:49:29,700
In this demonstration, we'll learn how to create and use NAT Gateway.

8564
14:49:30,158 --> 14:49:34,980
In the previous demonstration, we created a new VPC with private and public subnet.

8565
14:49:37,518 --> 14:49:41,284
For this demonstration, I have created an instance in a private subnet.

8566
14:49:44,142 --> 14:49:47,460
It does not have a public IP, but only a private one.

8567
14:49:47,822 --> 14:49:50,052
So it does not have access to the internet.

8568
14:49:51,854 --> 14:50:00,100
I created one more instance in a public subnet to be used as Bastion host, and then from that host, we'll use SSH for port forwarding.

8569
14:50:02,030 --> 14:50:07,812
Now let's log into the private instance and verify if it really can ping from outside the VPC.

8570
14:50:10,158 --> 14:50:12,196
First, let's check the Bastion host.

8571
14:50:14,190 --> 14:50:15,844
We'll try to ping something from it.

8572
14:50:19,054 --> 14:50:23,172
The Google URL proves very useful for testing network connections.

8573
14:50:36,238 --> 14:50:38,372
Okay, so we can see it is working.

8574
