8605
14:54:23,407 --> 14:54:29,287
Security groups control access to their member instances by protocol, port, and source or destination.

8606
14:54:30,190 --> 14:54:40,420
You can use a single IP address, CIDR block, or ID of the resource, for example, instance ID or security group ID as the source or destination.

8607
14:54:42,574 --> 14:54:50,724
Some important things about security groups are only 5x security groups can be associated with an ENI, which is a soft limit.

8608
14:54:51,214 --> 14:54:56,516
There is a limit of 100X security groups per VPC, which is a soft limit.

8609
14:54:57,102 --> 14:54:59,588
By default, no inbound traffic is allowed.

8610
14:54:59,886 --> 14:55:02,276
However, all outbound traffic is allowed.

8611
14:55:02,862 --> 14:55:06,724
All outbound traffic in response to an inbound request is permitted.

8612
14:55:07,182 --> 14:55:11,812
Instances that are part of the same security group cannot communicate with each other by default.

8613
14:55:12,462 --> 14:55:15,920
Let's take a look at some examples of security groups in action.

8614
14:55:16,080 --> 14:55:30,820
In the first example, there is a source description of 0.0.0.0.0 to specify that any computer from anywhere on the internet can access a web server on our instance that is listening on port 80.

8615
14:55:31,182 --> 14:55:38,884
Note that security groups that restrict access by IP actually specify an IP range using CIDR notations.

8616
14:55:39,662 --> 14:55:44,132
In the second example, access is allowed only from a specific IP address.

8617
14:55:44,814 --> 14:55:50,212
In the third example, SSH access is allowed only from a particular security group.

8618
14:55:51,630 --> 14:55:56,068
There are some important limitations for security groups that you need to keep in mind.

8619
14:55:56,846 --> 14:56:00,516
The number of rules per security group is soft limited by 50.

8620
14:56:01,390 --> 14:56:04,516
The number of security groups is soft limited by 5.

8621
14:56:05,454 --> 14:56:10,724
The maximum number of security groups is limited to 100 per VPC by default.

8622
14:56:12,302 --> 14:56:17,124
Your AWS account is limited to 100 security groups by default.

8623
14:56:17,710 --> 14:56:29,540
While you can increase this limit, having a large number of security groups can lead to a negative performance impact not just on your network, but on other networks hosted on the same hardware as well.

8624
14:56:30,222 --> 14:56:33,348
It's best to stay within the 100 security group limit.

8625
14:56:33,838 --> 14:56:48,100
And as the number of security groups in your VPC increases, you should create security groups based around CIDR blocks instead of around resource IDs such as EC2 servers, network interfaces, and other security groups.

8626
14:56:49,678 --> 14:56:55,268
Within a VPC, security is controlled using security groups and NACLs.

8627
14:56:55,854 --> 14:57:00,484
The NACLs are associated with specific subnets within a VPC.

8628
14:57:01,038 --> 14:57:08,004
As NACLs are stateless, both inbound and outbound rules, ingress and egress, must be defined.

8629
14:57:08,526 --> 14:57:15,780
You can define the inbound and outbound rules by specifying the type of rule, for example, custom TCP.

8630
14:57:16,558 --> 14:57:17,892
Providing a rule number.

8631
14:57:18,286 --> 14:57:21,764
Keep in mind, rules are processed from the lowest to the highest.

8632
14:57:22,702 --> 14:57:26,596
Defining the port range that will be allowed or denied in or out.

8633
14:57:27,662 --> 14:57:34,116
Specifying the source or destination IP address or range that will be allowed or denied in or out.

8634
14:57:35,214 --> 14:57:41,252
After the NACL has been defined, it can be associated to subnets within the VPC.

8635
14:57:42,286 --> 14:57:49,860
AWS officially recommends a set of network ACLs for each of the four configurations offered by the VPC wizard.

8636
14:57:50,446 --> 14:57:57,764
As these configurations involve supplying values that are specific to your private network, they must be implemented manually.

8637
14:57:58,958 --> 14:58:04,772
Placement groups are physical groupings of high performance instances in a single availability zone.

8638
14:58:05,422 --> 14:58:09,700
The instances use enhanced networking with maximized consistent throughput.

8639
14:58:10,414 --> 14:58:17,380
Placement groups are very useful for cluster databases and parallel processing for big data or rendering of graphics.

8640
14:58:18,574 --> 14:58:27,300
AWS Identity and Access Management, or IAM, is a web service that helps to securely control access to AWS resources.

8641
14:58:28,174 --> 14:58:34,756
IAM provides a centralized environment in which you can administer users, groups, roles, and permissions.

8642
14:58:35,406 --> 14:58:45,892
IAM is integrated with AWS, providing granular level access control at the service, application program interface or API, and resource levels.

8643
14:58:46,798 --> 14:58:52,196
AWS IAM is a free service which spans across all AWS regions.

8644
14:58:52,622 --> 14:58:58,628
With IAM, you can enable identity federation between your corporate directory and AWS.

8645
14:58:59,374 --> 14:59:10,852
This means you can use existing corporate identities to grant secure access to AWS resources, such as Amazon S3 buckets, without creating new AWS identities for the users.

8646
14:59:11,630 --> 14:59:18,020
You can create users and define groups and roles to securely control access to AWS resources.

8647
14:59:19,502 --> 14:59:24,004
AWS provides various ways of securing the AWS resources.

8648
14:59:24,782 --> 14:59:31,204
IAM users, IAM groups, IAM roles, IAM Access Keys.

8649
14:59:31,694 --> 14:59:34,436
You'll learn about each of them in detail in the lesson.

8650
14:59:36,654 --> 14:59:41,572
IAM users are not separate accounts, they are users within your account.

8651
14:59:41,998 --> 14:59:48,004
You can create IAM users and add or delete them from groups based on the permissions you want to provide.

8652
14:59:48,622 --> 14:59:53,700
IAM provides unique security credentials that can be used to access AWS.

8653
14:59:54,318 --> 15:00:03,076
IAM eliminates the need to share passwords or access keys and makes it easy to enable or disable a user's access as is considered appropriate.

8654
15:00:03,918 --> 15:00:12,484
AWS IAM enables you to create multiple users and manage the permissions for each of these users within their AWS account.

8655
15:00:13,102 --> 15:00:18,532
You can provide each user with its own password for access to the AWS management console.

8656
15:00:19,182 --> 15:00:24,228
You can manage the user's passwords, including strength, link, and password reset.

8657
15:00:24,942 --> 15:00:34,212
You can also create an individual access key for each user so that the user can make programmatic requests to work with resources in your account.

8658
15:00:34,510 --> 15:00:37,668
You can revoke the access key as and when required.

8659
15:00:38,382 --> 15:00:45,220
AWS IAM allows you to administer multifactor authentication, or MFA, for privileged users.

8660
15:00:45,806 --> 15:00:48,836
Note that a user does not necessarily have to be a human.

8661
15:00:49,262 --> 15:00:59,396
You can create an IAM user in order to generate an access key for an application that runs in your AWS or corporate network and needs AWS access.

8662
15:01:01,742 --> 15:01:09,892
A new IAM user by default is not authorized to perform any AWS action or access any AWS resources.

8663
15:01:10,446 --> 15:01:12,740
You need to provide access to the users.

8664
15:01:13,358 --> 15:01:22,564
You can assign administrative permissions to a few users, who in turn can administer your AWS resources and create and manage other IAM users.

8665
15:01:23,278 --> 15:01:29,296
An advantage of having individual IAM users is that you can assign permissions individually to each.

8666
15:01:29,486 --> 15:01:33,412
It's best to use a least privileged policy when assigning permissions.

8667
15:01:33,838 --> 15:01:42,340
Limiting a user's permissions to just the AWS actions and resources that the user needs for his or her job reduces chances of mistakes.

8668
15:01:42,862 --> 15:01:46,596
You can always increase access permissions as and when required.

8669
15:01:47,150 --> 15:01:53,316
However, assigning permissions to individuals in a big organization can become a maintenance nightmare.

8670
15:01:53,742 --> 15:02:00,196
As the list keeps getting longer, it becomes difficult to assess which particular user has been granted what permissions.

8671
15:02:00,814 --> 15:02:07,108
Using groups simplifies the task of adding or removing permissions to a large number of users simultaneously.

8672
15:02:08,782 --> 15:02:12,260
An IAM group is a collection of IAM users.

8673
15:02:12,750 --> 15:02:19,332
IAM groups allow you to define sets of permissions by function, department, or level of responsibility.

8674
15:02:19,822 --> 15:02:35,972
To provide added security to your AWS account, it is strongly encouraged that you create a set of groups corresponding to the least privileged principle, which is grant your users and applications the necessary permissions to perform only the tasks assigned to them.

8675
15:02:36,622 --> 15:02:46,148
For example, there is no reason to assign EC2 read permissions to a user whose only job with regard to AWS is to generate billing reports.

8676
15:02:46,894 --> 15:02:51,332
There are a few points that you need to keep in mind with regard to IAM groups.

8677
15:02:51,886 --> 15:02:56,804
A group can contain many users, and a user can belong to multiple groups.

8678
15:02:57,742 --> 15:02:59,332
Groups can't be nested.

8679
15:02:59,726 --> 15:03:02,564
They can only contain users and not other groups.

8680
15:03:03,534 --> 15:03:08,516
There's no default group that automatically includes all users in the AWS account.

8681
15:03:09,006 --> 15:03:14,180
If you want to have a default group, you need to create it and assign each new user to it.

8682
15:03:15,406 --> 15:03:21,444
There's a limit to the number of groups you can have and a limit to how many groups a user can be a part of.

8683
15:03:22,350 --> 15:03:26,500
Let's now study an example to understand how IAM groups work.

8684
15:03:28,206 --> 15:03:32,388
The diagram presents an example of groups created for a small company.

8685
15:03:32,910 --> 15:03:40,132
The company owner creates an admins group and adds users to it to create and manage other users as the company grows.

8686
15:03:40,814 --> 15:03:44,132
The admins group creates a development group and a test group.

8687
15:03:44,622 --> 15:03:50,084
Each of these groups consists of users, humans, and applications that interact with AWS.

8688
15:03:50,606 --> 15:03:53,860
Each user has an individual set of security credentials.

8689
15:03:54,574 --> 15:03:57,988
In this example, each user belongs to a single group.

8690
15:03:58,478 --> 15:04:01,476
However, users can belong to multiple groups as well.

8691
15:04:01,934 --> 15:04:09,604
For instance, if you need to grant a permission from the development group admin privileges, you can simply add that person to the admins group.

8692
15:04:10,286 --> 15:04:15,300
As a result, the person now belongs to both the development group and the admins group.

8693
15:04:16,878 --> 15:04:24,164
Till now, we have discussed accessing the AWS resources with the help of unique identities that are particular to a user.

8694
15:04:24,590 --> 15:04:30,276
However, you can also assume a role to temporarily take on permissions for a specific task.

8695
15:04:30,990 --> 15:04:40,452
A role lets you define a set of permissions to access the resources that a user or service needs, but the permissions are not attached to any IAM user or group.

8696
15:04:40,942 --> 15:04:44,836
Instead, at runtime, users can programmatically assume a role.

8697
15:04:45,390 --> 15:04:54,180
When a role is assumed, AWS provides temporary security credentials that the user or application can use to make requests to AWS.

8698
15:04:54,766 --> 15:05:02,052
Consequently, you don't need to create long term security credentials for each entity that requires access to a resource.

8699
15:05:02,734 --> 15:05:07,364
AWS rotates the security credentials automatically on a daily basis.

8700
15:05:07,726 --> 15:05:13,060
The new credentials become available at least five minutes prior to the expiration of the old ones.

8701
15:05:14,478 --> 15:05:22,340
You need to assign permissions to a user, group, or role as required so they can securely access the AWS resources.

8702
15:05:22,894 --> 15:05:30,468
To assign permissions, you need to create and attach a policy, which is a JSON document that explicitly lists the permissions.

8703
15:05:31,150 --> 15:05:34,756
A policy lets you specify the following Actions.

8704
15:05:35,182 --> 15:05:36,708
What actions will you allow?

8705
15:05:37,198 --> 15:05:40,080
Each AWS service has its own set of actions.

8706
15:05:40,398 --> 15:05:48,292
For example, you might allow a user to use the Amazon S3 list bucket action, which returns information about the items in a bucket.

8707
15:05:48,910 --> 15:05:52,132
Any action that you don't explicitly allow are denied.

8708
15:05:53,134 --> 15:05:57,412
Resources On which of these resources will you allow the action?

8709
15:05:57,998 --> 15:06:04,740
For example, on which specific Amazon S3 buckets will you allow the user to perform the list bucket action?

8710
15:06:05,390 --> 15:06:10,244
Users cannot access a resource that you have not explicitly granted permissions to.

8711
15:06:11,118 --> 15:06:15,440
Effect What will be the effect when a user requests access?

8712
15:06:15,726 --> 15:06:17,700
Are you going to allow or deny it?

8713
15:06:18,382 --> 15:06:21,860
AWS resources are denied to users by default.

8714
15:06:22,318 --> 15:06:27,588
You need to specify that you want to allow a particular user access to a specific resource.

8715
15:06:29,294 --> 15:06:35,300
Another way of securely accessing the AWS resources is via IAM access keys.

8716
15:06:35,758 --> 15:06:41,252
The IAM access keys are cryptographically generated keys that represent your account.

8717
15:06:41,902 --> 15:06:48,196
The access key consists of two components, the access key ID and the secret access key.

8718
15:06:48,782 --> 15:06:53,700
Both keys can be generated for an IAM user in the AWS Web Console.

8719
15:06:54,478 --> 15:06:59,812
The key ID uniquely identifies user credentials and can be considered public knowledge.

8720
15:07:00,462 --> 15:07:04,580
The secret access key is used to cryptographically sign all requests.

8721
15:07:04,974 --> 15:07:11,012
It's private to the user and should never be publicly posted or shared with unauthorized personnel.

8722
15:07:11,630 --> 15:07:14,884
If compromised, the key can and must be revoked.

8723
15:07:15,342 --> 15:07:20,800
You should never place your access keys anywhere where they can be potentially discovered by third parties.

8724
15:07:21,070 --> 15:07:31,492
Third parties can scan source code repositories, such as GitHub for AWS credentials, and use those credentials to spin up their own AWS resources at your expense.

8725
15:07:32,238 --> 15:07:39,076
You can create access keys for your master account, however, AWS strongly discourages this practice.

8726
15:07:39,534 --> 15:07:51,268
It is safer to generate a user in IAM, assign that user to a group with the subset of permissions required by your tools or application, and then generate an access key for that user.

8727
15:07:53,582 --> 15:07:57,200
Password only security is not enough to secure your account.

8728
15:07:57,582 --> 15:08:03,684
Passwords can be easily compromised through social engineering or by malwares, such as a keystroke logger.

8729
15:08:04,302 --> 15:08:11,204
To provide an additional level of account security, you can enable multifactor authentication or MFA.

8730
15:08:11,758 --> 15:08:16,932
With MFA, users need to supply a password and a one-time response token.

8731
15:08:17,358 --> 15:08:20,036
This token can be generated in one of two ways.

8732
15:08:20,750 --> 15:08:22,116
One by software.

8733
15:08:22,606 --> 15:08:30,372
Software applications, such as Google Authenticator for iPhone and Android, can be used to generate a token from a smartphone or a tablet.

8734
15:08:31,342 --> 15:08:32,900
Two, by hardware.

8735
15:08:33,518 --> 15:08:38,788
AWS supports key fob and display card devices sold through Gemalto.

8736
15:08:39,406 --> 15:08:49,220
Physical or hardware token generators provide an additional level of security over software devices, since software devices are harder to secure against unauthorized usage.

8737
15:08:50,030 --> 15:08:59,236
Both software and hardware devices are configured through a one-time process through which the token devices are synchronized with your AWS or IAM account.

8738
15:09:00,142 --> 15:09:03,108
Make sure to safeguard your MFA token generators.

8739
15:09:03,534 --> 15:09:13,764
If a physical token is lost or stolen, or a software generator is deleted, for example, as part of a device swipe, you will have to disable MFA on your account.

8740
15:09:14,350 --> 15:09:19,140
And before disabling, you will have to provide a proof of identity to AWS.

8741
15:09:19,950 --> 15:09:29,796
Now that you have learned how to use MFA to secure your AWS account, you will learn about the AWS recommended best practices regarding account security.

8742
15:09:30,542 --> 15:09:34,724
Do not circulate information about your master or root AWS account.

8743
15:09:35,630 --> 15:09:40,484
Delegate system administration functions to least privileged IAM admin groups.

8744
15:09:41,390 --> 15:09:45,668
Make multi factor authentication mandatory for root level access.

8745
15:09:46,574 --> 15:09:51,396
Physically secure hardware MFA devices in a safe place such as a vault.

8746
15:09:52,334 --> 15:09:56,932
Do not share master account information with anyone other than the account holder.

8747
15:09:57,934 --> 15:10:01,284
Use IAM roles to provide cross account access.

8748
15:10:02,702 --> 15:10:07,140
After securing your account, the next step is to secure your instances.

8749
15:10:07,534 --> 15:10:15,332
You can define the instance level security by deciding how to grant and revoke permissions as employees enter and leave the company.

8750
15:10:16,206 --> 15:10:25,732
For Windows instances, you can leverage the AWS directory service to grant and revoke access to machines based on existing Windows users and groups.

8751
15:10:26,734 --> 15:10:35,588
For Linux instances, you can add users, generate new key pairs, and append them to a new user's file with the commands displayed on the screen.

8752
15:10:36,398 --> 15:10:45,444
Devise a strategy for fleet-wide user management, such as creating Amazon Machine Images or AMIs containing all the current users of your company.

8753
15:10:46,606 --> 15:11:00,516
Use configuration management applications such as Chef, Puppet, or Ansible, to simplify the process of granting and revoking access across a large number of machines, often reducing the process to a few simple commands.

8754
15:11:01,550 --> 15:11:06,980
Let's now learn the AWS recommended best practices regarding instance security.

8755
15:11:07,694 --> 15:11:11,012
You should use IAM roles when launching instances.

8756
15:11:11,406 --> 15:11:14,052
Avoid giving permissions to individual users.

8757
15:11:14,798 --> 15:11:17,124
Use least privilege access policies.

8758
15:11:17,614 --> 15:11:20,580
Do not give the user more privileges than required.

8759
15:11:21,454 --> 15:11:24,548
Guard and manage access or secret key tokens.

8760
15:11:24,942 --> 15:11:27,940
Store your access key tokens in a secure place.

8761
15:11:28,782 --> 15:11:30,852
Keep security patches up to date.

8762
15:11:31,118 --> 15:11:39,236
Subscribe to security mailing lists of the operating systems and applications you're using, and apply patches and updates in a timely manner.

8763
15:11:40,206 --> 15:11:42,244
Use a NAT and Bastion Host.

8764
15:11:42,670 --> 15:11:47,108
Do not expose your EC2 resources to the Internet if it is not required.

8765
15:11:48,206 --> 15:11:53,028
You should not use root level or master account access or secret keys.

8766
15:11:53,454 --> 15:12:01,380
You do not need to use the master account regularly, so it is highly recommended to remove its access keys or avoid generating them.

8767
15:12:02,318 --> 15:12:06,564
Embed access or secret keys in the code or commit them to Git.

8768
15:12:07,182 --> 15:12:14,564
Remember that anyone who has your access keys has the same level of access as you to your AWS resources.

8769
15:12:18,062 --> 15:12:23,556
In this topic, you'll learn about the responsibilities shared between AWS and the customer.

8770
15:12:25,102 --> 15:12:32,484
The shared responsibility model defines the responsibilities shared between AWS and the customers with regard to security.

8771
15:12:33,358 --> 15:13:04,036
AWS is responsible for the security of the cloud, that is, the underlying infrastructure, and the customer is responsible for security in the cloud, that is, the resources built on the infrastructure, which include AWS, EC2 instances, and the operating systems and applications installed on them, accounts that access the instances, security group that allows outside access to the instances, VPC subnet within which the instances reside.

8772
15:13:04,718 --> 15:13:07,300
external access to the S3 buckets.

8773
15:13:09,326 --> 15:13:14,020
A crucial part of a customer's responsibility involves penetration testing.

8774
15:13:14,702 --> 15:13:22,820
Penetration testing is the practice of scanning a system, application, or network to search for vulnerabilities that an attacker can exploit.

8775
15:13:23,566 --> 15:13:28,868
To perform penetration testing of your EC two instances, you need to request for permission.

8776
15:13:29,390 --> 15:13:33,828
You need to keep in mind the following points regarding penetration testing requests.

8777
15:13:34,542 --> 15:13:37,444
Permission is required for all penetration tests.

8778
15:13:38,254 --> 15:13:46,596
To request permission, you must be logged into the AWS portal using the root credentials associated with the instances you want to test.

8779
15:13:47,022 --> 15:13:53,380
Otherwise, the AWS vulnerability penetration testing request form will not pre-populate correctly.

8780
15:13:54,478 --> 15:14:00,196
AWS policy only permits testing of EC2 and RDS instances that you own.

8781
15:14:00,750 --> 15:14:06,180
Tests against any other AWS service or AWS owned resources are prohibited.

8782
15:14:07,118 --> 15:14:17,060
Currently, Amazon does not permit testing of small or micro RDS instances, M1 small or T1 micro EC2 instances.

8783
15:14:18,880 --> 15:14:23,280
You have already learned what the customers' responsibilities regarding security are.

8784
15:14:23,918 --> 15:14:27,760
Let's now quickly learn what the responsibilities of AWS are.

8785
15:14:28,718 --> 15:14:36,000
AWS offers extensive networking and security monitoring systems to provide a secure environment for your instances.

8786
15:14:36,718 --> 15:15:03,876
AWS is responsible for ensuring the following: physical and environmental security, fire detection and suppression, uninterruptible power supply, maintaining a constant temperature for servers, management of electrical, mechanical, and life support systems, storage device decommissioning, business, continuity management, availability, incident response, company-wide, executive review.

8787
15:15:05,166 --> 15:15:39,940
Network security, secure network architecture, secure access, transmission protection, Amazon corporate segregation, or separation of duties between logical and physical, fault tolerant design, network monitoring and protection, AWS, access, account review and audit, background checks, password policy, secure design principles, secure software development, formal design reviews, threat modeling, risk assessments, static code analysis.

8788
15:15:40,974 --> 15:15:55,600
Change management, systematic review, test, approve, and communicate, phased deployment starting with lowest impact areas, metrics for impact, health, threshold, and alarms, root cause analysis.

