16:17:01,840 --> 16:17:06,320
Now the CISSP stands for the Certified Information System Security Professional.

9436
16:17:06,480 --> 16:17:19,840
This is a gold standard in in information security as far as certifications are concerned, and is normally gained by professionals with uh experience of more than five to ten years and is more of a management-related certification.

9437
16:17:20,080 --> 16:17:30,480
This certification ensures that you have the requisite knowledge to become an information assurance professional where you can implement security in an organization on an enterprise scale.

9438
16:17:30,640 --> 16:17:33,200
For the exam, there are two ways to go about with the exam.

9439
16:17:33,360 --> 16:17:52,960
Anyone can attempt the exam, but once you clear the exam, there is a verification that that takes place from the ISC Square, which is the certification authority for CISSP, where you have to prove that you have at least five years of experience in the information security field in at least two of the domains that are specified under CISSP.

9440
16:17:53,120 --> 16:18:05,600
If you do not have that experience, you can still get certified as an associate of ISC Square and then they give you six years to complete the relevant experience that is required, after which you become a CISSP certified professional.

9441
16:18:05,760 --> 16:18:18,160
The exam is quite difficult, it's known as a mile-wide inch deep exam because you don't have to be a master in each and every domain, but you have to understand and have the knowledge across all these domains that are specified.

9442
16:18:18,320 --> 16:18:23,280
The exam is quite tough and people take a long time to ready themselves for these exams.

9443
16:18:23,440 --> 16:18:28,080
The passing rate for the candidate in the very first attempt is well below 50%.

9444
16:18:28,560 --> 16:18:31,600
Now, this should not be taken as a negative point.

9445
16:18:31,760 --> 16:18:49,440
The reasons are plentiful because there are people who actually do not realize the exam itself or do not realize the seriousness of the exams, people without experience, without reading the requisite books think that it's just going to be a theoretical exam and thus they try to attempt it without being ready for it, right?

9446
16:18:49,600 --> 16:19:00,320
Uh, when you want to take the exam, people study for months together and they solve a lot of question banks, ensure that they have the prerequisite knowledge, so they read a lot of books that are out there.

9447
16:19:00,480 --> 16:19:10,640
Some of them are prescribed by ISC Square themselves, others are from different authors that they try to give in more insight to certain topics, and then once they feel that they are ready, they would give the exam.

9448
16:19:10,800 --> 16:19:20,400
I've seen people take question banks of at least two to three thousand questions, uh, ensure that they're scoring at least 80% in those test exams, and then attempt the exam itself.

9449
16:19:20,560 --> 16:19:25,520
So in CISSP, as with every security course, we start off with the CIA triad.

9450
16:19:25,760 --> 16:19:29,760
Now, here CIA is confidentiality, integrity, and availability.

9451
16:19:30,000 --> 16:19:32,560
So it starts off with every security mechanism.

9452
16:19:32,720 --> 16:19:37,360
When we talk about security and we want to keep things secure, what is it that we want to secure?

9453
16:19:37,520 --> 16:19:44,080
It is the data of an organization that is the most valuable asset to the organization, and that's what we want to secure.

9454
16:19:44,320 --> 16:19:49,360
When we say that we want the data to be secured, we basically talk about three aspects for that data.

9455
16:19:49,520 --> 16:20:01,120
The first and the foremost that the data needs to remain confidential, the second aspect that the data needs to be trustworthy, and the third concept that the data should be available to all authorized users when and where they require it.

9456
16:20:01,200 --> 16:20:05,840
So, going back to the first point, when we say we want data to be confidential, what do we mean by that?

9457
16:20:06,000 --> 16:20:17,760
By confidentiality, we talk about that data being made available only to authorized users after you have authenticated them, ensured or assured of their authenticity, and only then you're going to give access to that data, right?

9458
16:20:17,840 --> 16:20:22,720
So, even if you go into an organization, an organization has a hierarchy of governance, right?

9459
16:20:23,152 --> 16:20:30,992
So only certain people with certain clearances or certain job titles can have access to certain amount of data, whereas other people may not have access to it.

9460
16:20:31,152 --> 16:20:36,912
For example, a person working in the sales department will not have access to data that is accessed by the HR department.

9461
16:20:37,072 --> 16:20:43,792
Thus, that's what we are looking at classification of data and keeping it confidential, only making it available to those people who require it.

9462
16:20:43,952 --> 16:20:52,832
When we say trustworthiness or the integrity of that data, the integrity is where the data has been altered, modified, removed, deleted by unauthorized people.

9463
16:20:52,912 --> 16:20:54,032
So we want to prevent that.

9464
16:20:54,192 --> 16:21:03,152
Which means when we say we want the integrity intact, we want to ensure that only authorized people can modify, alter, or remove data if they are allowed to do so.

9465
16:21:03,438 --> 16:21:06,512
So that's where the authorization and authentication comes into the picture again.

9466
16:21:06,672 --> 16:21:08,272
And data needs to be available.

9467
16:21:08,432 --> 16:21:15,872
For me to safeguard data, I can just lock it up in a safe, throw the safe in the depth of an ocean, and say that the data is secure because it cannot be leaked out.

9468
16:21:15,982 --> 16:21:20,032
But that data is no longer available to authorized users, hence that data is useless.

9469
16:21:20,192 --> 16:21:29,152
For me, data would have value when the data remains confidential, has its trustworthiness intact, and is still available to all the users that are authorized to access it.

9470
16:21:29,312 --> 16:21:37,152
So when we talk about security, we want these three aspects to be implemented on any and every digital asset that the organization has and does.

9471
16:21:37,312 --> 16:21:44,832
Once these three points are guaranteed or at least assured to a certain extent, we can then say that the data or that particular asset is secured.

9472
16:21:44,992 --> 16:21:54,272
So when we talk about CISSP, what are the domains in CISSP as far as the exam and the certification is concerned, and how is CIA or information security related to it?

9473
16:21:54,432 --> 16:21:57,392
So to start off with, there are eight domains in CISSP.

9474
16:21:57,472 --> 16:22:11,952
One starts off with asset security, then you've got security architecture and engineering, then we have communication and network security, identity and access management, security operations, security assessment and testing, software development security, and security and risk management.

9475
16:22:12,142 --> 16:22:16,112
Alright, so talking about the eight domains, the first one being security and risk management.

9476
16:22:16,272 --> 16:22:28,752
This is from a governance perspective where we talk about the risk an organization would face, risk management a little bit, talk about compliance, laws, regulations that the organization has to look forward to or needs to adhere to.

9477
16:22:28,912 --> 16:22:39,152
So if you look at an organization's from an organization's perspective, you are looking at a business objective that an organization has and how security can be aligned with the business objectives.

9478
16:22:39,312 --> 16:22:46,592
For example, if you take an online retailer or as we call them an e-tailer, and we want to look at how security is relevant to them, right?

9479
16:22:46,766 --> 16:22:53,552
We first want to look at what are the compliances or what are the laws the country has in which they exist and what laws do they need to adhere to.

9480
16:22:53,712 --> 16:23:03,152
Could be corporate laws, could be computing laws, could be the Information Technology Act that we have in India or the privacy acts that we have across the world, GDPR, if that is required.

9481
16:23:03,392 --> 16:23:10,912
Regulations, for example, a financial organization would need to comply with GDPR if they are in Europe or if they're in USA, California.

9482
16:23:10,992 --> 16:23:14,992
There's a California Act that's there for privacy, the security that they want to deal with.

9483
16:23:15,072 --> 16:23:20,672
So if they want to implement ISO 27001 on top of that, what are the requirements for ISO 27000?

9484
16:23:20,912 --> 16:23:24,912
What is the scope of that standard that can be implemented in the organization?

9485
16:23:24,992 --> 16:23:32,272
So they want to check on these laws, regulations, compliances, and they need to check which they need to implement and which they need to adhere to.

9486
16:23:32,432 --> 16:23:35,712
And once they have identified that, they would look at the risk assessment.

9487
16:23:35,872 --> 16:23:52,192
They would see where they are positioned right now, what are the risks that the organization faces as far as these laws, regulations, and compliances are concerned, how they can mitigate those risks with security, and once security has been implemented, how they can move on with the governance of that enterprise over a longer period of time.

9488
16:23:52,352 --> 16:23:57,232
And then, this is broken down into rest of the modules where you can first talk about asset security.

9489
16:23:57,312 --> 16:24:06,752
So you first want to identify the assets of an organization, could be physical assets like computers, servers, storage servers, switches, routers, see how they can be secured.

9490
16:24:06,912 --> 16:24:08,912
Physical security comes into the picture as well.

9491
16:24:09,072 --> 16:24:16,112
Then you want to look at digital assets like data that is being stored, classification of data, maybe a little bit where you have identified data.

9492
16:24:16,272 --> 16:24:19,872
you have classified them as proprietary data, something that is trademarked.

9493
16:24:19,952 --> 16:24:21,552
something that is copyrighted, etc.

9494
16:24:21,792 --> 16:24:30,832
The value of those assets that the organization has towards them, and then how you're going to protect that asset based on the risk assessment strategy that you have identified in the first domain.

9495
16:24:30,992 --> 16:24:33,552
Then comes with the third module security engineering.

9496
16:24:33,632 --> 16:24:37,632
Now, this is where security by design starts as far as CISSP is concerned.

9497
16:24:37,806 --> 16:24:44,752
CISSP always encourages security to be involved at each and every phase of the life cycle of an organization.

9498
16:24:44,912 --> 16:25:06,992
So, for example, the third domain will start off with enterprise architectures like TOGAF and how security architectures like ISO 27001 or ISO 27002, 3, 4, 5 of any of these standards, or COBITs, for example, can be implemented and integrated with the enterprise architecture, thus giving you a good security architecture right from the inception point.

9499
16:25:07,152 --> 16:25:16,832
So the main aspect being security should not be bolted on, it should be secure by design, which means while you are creating it and implementing it, security should be implemented right from then.

9500
16:25:17,184 --> 16:25:21,424
Then we move on to the next module, module 4, communications and network security.

9501
16:25:21,584 --> 16:25:30,944
So in the previous module, you have identified the devices that you have talked about how the architecture of those devices would be, how data flows are going to happen, and then how you're going to secure those data flows.

9502
16:25:31,104 --> 16:25:39,344
Then you come to the communication and networking part of it: how these computers or devices are going to interact with each other, which protocols are going to be utilized.

9503
16:25:39,584 --> 16:25:51,584
the OSI layer comes into the picture, how you're going to secure yourself against various network-based attacks like ARP, spoofing, Mac spoofing, IP address, spoofing, sniffing attacks, man in the middle attacks, and things like that.

9504
16:25:51,744 --> 16:26:03,984
Now, this domain is slightly technical, does it does talk about OSI layers, TCP and UDP, but it doesn't go in depth about technicalities about how attacks are created or how attacks are executed, right?

9505
16:26:04,144 --> 16:26:06,784
So that's something that this training doesn't deal with.

9506
16:26:06,864 --> 16:26:21,024
So this is not a hacking training where you're actually going to learn how to hack, it's basically an overview of what threats or risks a network or a communication protocol may have, and thus you're going to identify the relevant security controls and implement them on those particular levels.

9507
16:26:21,104 --> 16:26:25,504
So, for example, in this mechanism, I want the OSI layer which has seven layers, right?

9508
16:26:25,584 --> 16:26:38,544
From the physical layer to the application layer, and then I want to identify a particular attack and which layer it affects, and thus want to have appropriate security control to monitor that layer so as to mitigate that particular attack, right?

9509
16:26:38,704 --> 16:26:45,984
So it's semi-technical, if you will, where you need to understand the OSI layer and the protocols, but you don't need to execute those attacks at all.

9510
16:26:46,158 --> 16:26:47,904
Then comes the identity and access management.

9511
16:26:48,064 --> 16:27:00,544
So if you've seen, if you go back to security and risk management, and we have talked about CIA, confidentiality, integrity, availability, and then we have talked about identifying assets, engineering with security as a design, networking, and all those.

9512
16:27:00,704 --> 16:27:05,584
Now, when we come back to confidentiality and we want to keep these things confidential, how do we do that?

9513
16:27:05,744 --> 16:27:07,744
The first thing is identity and access management.

9514
16:27:07,904 --> 16:27:09,584
So who is allowed to access what?

9515
16:27:09,744 --> 16:27:12,864
To what extent are they allowed, and what are they authorized to do?

9516
16:27:13,024 --> 16:27:20,304
Are they allowed to modify those particular data points, delete, remove them, or add to them, edit them, etc.

9517
16:27:20,544 --> 16:27:22,224
So who's authorized to do what?

9518
16:27:22,304 --> 16:27:25,744
And once they're authorized, you want the authentication mechanism to come to come in.

9519
16:27:25,824 --> 16:27:31,904
So we come back to the I triple A standards identification, authentication, authorization, and accountability.

9520
16:27:32,064 --> 16:27:41,584
So when I say I want user who has been through the identity and access management process, and only then will they be allowed to access, that's where your user management comes into the picture.

9521
16:27:41,744 --> 16:27:43,424
For example, let's take Gmail.

9522
16:27:43,584 --> 16:27:44,944
Everybody accesses Gmail.

9523
16:27:45,104 --> 16:27:46,224
What is it that you first do?

9524
16:27:46,384 --> 16:27:51,984
You open up a browser, that's where your asset security comes in, the browser becoming that asset where you need to secure it.

9525
16:27:52,144 --> 16:27:56,464
So you secure it by having anti fishing toolbars, maybe an antivirus and things like that.

9526
16:27:56,590 --> 16:28:06,784
The security engineering coming into the picture where you have integrated a particular browser with some particular extensions, how the browser is going to communicate using HTTP, HTTPS, right?

9527
16:28:06,944 --> 16:28:10,784
And then when you go to Google, you use the identity and access management module.

9528
16:28:10,864 --> 16:28:14,624
So it first asks you to identify yourself by using your username.

9529
16:28:14,784 --> 16:28:17,504
So you type in your email address, that's your identity.

9530
16:28:17,584 --> 16:28:20,464
So that's the uh IAA identity coming into the picture.

9531
16:28:20,544 --> 16:28:23,024
Then it asks for authentication, which is your password.

9532
16:28:23,184 --> 16:28:26,144
You may have enabled multi-factor authentication at that point in time.

9533
16:28:26,318 --> 16:28:33,264
So once you put in the password, if it's single authentication mechanisms, you enter it, and then that's where your authentication process comes into the picture.

9534
16:28:33,424 --> 16:28:35,264
After that is the authorization process.

9535
16:28:35,424 --> 16:28:37,824
What are you authorized to access as a user?

9536
16:28:37,984 --> 16:28:40,064
Do you get access to the entire Gmail server?

9537
16:28:40,144 --> 16:28:44,144
No, you only get access to the email account that you have been authorized to access.

9538
16:28:44,384 --> 16:28:51,744
Within that, if you're an administrator to that account, you can do whatever you want and uh they are still monitored, that's where the accountability part comes into the picture.

9539
16:28:51,824 --> 16:28:54,944
Where if you do something illegal, you may be penalized for it.

9540
16:28:55,024 --> 16:28:58,384
So that's the identity and access management module that comes into the picture.

9541
16:28:58,544 --> 16:29:01,584
Now, with the browser, I've just given you a very basic example.

9542
16:29:01,744 --> 16:29:14,451
But as you go into a larger scenario, we are looking at enterprises and you're looking at security over different assets like storage, databases, and so on and so forth, it becomes a huge task to have an identity access management module implemented.

9543
16:29:14,624 --> 16:29:17,344
Then comes the next domain called security operations.

9544
16:29:17,504 --> 16:29:22,224
So that's where your day-to-day operations, like a SOC, a security operations center, comes into the picture.

9545
16:29:22,384 --> 16:29:28,864
So incident management, disaster recovery, your investigations, forensics of what attacks have happened.

9546
16:29:28,944 --> 16:29:41,424
So your security operations centers might be the day-to-day activities that you deal with, let's say an IDS, IPS, firewalls, antiviruses, and it's basically a structure of how you're going to build an exercise to manage incidents as and when they happen.

9547
16:29:41,584 --> 16:29:51,584
So it's basically more of an operation scenario where you're looking at the day to day health of an organization rather than looking at an overview of the security of an organization.

9548
16:29:51,968 --> 16:30:09,648
Then comes security assessment and testing, which is basically your secure development lifecycle or SDLCs as they are known, where you'll be looking at how you can assess applications where you'll be looking at SAST, DAST, or dynamic application security testing, static application security testing, and so on so forth.

9549
16:30:09,808 --> 16:30:23,488
So here you're either procured an application from a third party and you want to test that application to see how it is going to or what risks it is going to expose your business to, or you're developing an application and you want to involve security right from the inception.

9550
16:30:23,648 --> 16:30:36,688
So if you're looking at that, then you can move on to the next domain where we talk about software development security, which talks about different software development lifecycle models like waterfall, agile, pyrol models, and so on so forth.

9551
16:30:36,848 --> 16:30:52,928
So if you look at the eight domains, they basically start off with the governance where they talk about security and rescue management, and they go to each and every aspect of your information technology and give you an overview of how security should be implemented right from the inception and build up from there to the execution level.

9552
16:30:53,102 --> 16:30:59,168
Now the exam and these domains are built in an ideal world, not a real world scenario.

9553
16:30:59,328 --> 16:31:13,808
So there are a few assumptions over here where you assume that you have all the required resources, you have all the required people to do those jobs, you're living in an ideal world, you have mapped all the roles and responsibilities properly, and then you're talking about these eight domains.

9554
16:31:13,968 --> 16:31:27,008
In the real world, things are quite different, and people who have got real world experience kind of struggle to map all those things as far as an ideal world is concerned, and that's where your reading and your understanding of these concepts come into the picture.

9555
16:31:27,248 --> 16:31:29,568
Moving on, let's talk about information security.

9556
16:31:29,808 --> 16:31:33,328
Information security is the process of protecting data and information systems.

9557
16:31:33,408 --> 16:31:37,648
So in the CIA, as we talked about it, when we said what do we want to protect?

9558
16:31:37,808 --> 16:31:44,768
We want to protect all the information assets or the technical assets that we have from any of the vulnerabilities that can be identified.

9559
16:31:44,928 --> 16:31:56,768
So we want to restrict unauthorized access and use, we want to restrict deletion, accidental or intentional if they have not been authorized to do so, modification of data, so the integrity part, and destruction of data.

9560
16:31:56,974 --> 16:32:05,328
Destruction could not only be deleting data, but it could be something like a ransomware where the data gets encrypted, so it's still there, it's just not accessible to you.

9561
16:32:05,488 --> 16:32:09,728
Now, information security ensures the implementation of these following aspects.

9562
16:32:09,902 --> 16:32:14,448
The first and foremost comes from information security policies, which is the governance aspect of it.

9563
16:32:14,608 --> 16:32:23,648
The policies are designed to have a implementation of security in an organization that helps the business processes to be executed in a secure manner.

9564
16:32:23,808 --> 16:32:25,328
Where are these policies coming from?

9565
16:32:25,518 --> 16:32:32,608
They come from standards or guidelines which are globally available and based on which we can start developing our security policies.

9566
16:32:32,768 --> 16:32:42,128
For example, if I want to develop a security policy in my organization, I might want to depend on frameworks like ISO 27001, COBIT or something similar.

9567
16:32:42,288 --> 16:32:45,408
Once I have these standards set, I would come back to procedures.

9568
16:32:45,648 --> 16:32:49,328
Procedures being how are these standards to be executed, right?

9569
16:32:49,568 --> 16:33:03,088
For example, in my policies, I ensured that I want encryption of AES 256 to be implemented, or rather, in the policy, I determined that we want encryption to be implemented on a data set because based on the classification of those data.

9570
16:33:03,248 --> 16:33:18,688
So in my policies, I would determine how the classification is going to work, how data is going to be classified on what parameters, and thus once the data gets classified, we are going to come back to standards where we say if it is classified as a confidential data, we want to use encryption to protect that data.

9571
16:33:18,798 --> 16:33:22,528
And we want AES 256 to be utilized for encryption.

9572
16:33:22,608 --> 16:33:23,728
So, how do we go about it?

9573
16:33:23,888 --> 16:33:25,248
How do we implement the encryption?

9574
16:33:25,328 --> 16:33:27,008
That's where your procedures come into the picture.

9575
16:33:27,408 --> 16:33:30,688
Your guidelines are troubleshooting mechanisms, optional documents.

9576
16:33:30,848 --> 16:33:37,648
So if somebody has trouble following the procedures, they might want to go into the guidelines and see how to troubleshoot the scenario that they are facing.

9577
16:33:37,888 --> 16:33:43,648
Baselines are basically the minimum achievable target that you want to go with with these policies.

9578
16:33:43,808 --> 16:33:54,208
So let's say when I say I want to have a baseline on a server where for the server to be published onto the internet or to be used in production environment, it has to meet a certain criteria.

9579
16:33:54,368 --> 16:33:59,648
Which means I can go back to the hardware and I can say the server needs a hardware configuration of X, Y, and Z.

9580
16:33:59,728 --> 16:34:03,968
Let's say a processor, a Xeon processor with 16 cores, 128 gigs of RAM.

9581
16:34:04,208 --> 16:34:09,648
So that's a baseline that I need for a particular server for it to be put into production environment for a particular use.

9582
16:34:09,808 --> 16:34:12,928
Anything above that is acceptable, but nothing should go below that.

9583
16:34:13,038 --> 16:34:18,368
Then comes the risk management while we are implementing these policies, procedures, we have those standards in place.

9584
16:34:18,862 --> 16:34:24,688
If we face any risk during that period, or if we face any threat or vulnerabilities identified during this period.

9585
16:34:24,848 --> 16:34:26,928
All these terms we are going to discuss in a little bit.

9586
16:34:27,088 --> 16:34:31,168
So when we identify vulnerabilities or threats, that's where our risks come into the picture.

9587
16:34:31,328 --> 16:34:37,648
The risk management comes in saying, okay, this is a risk that we have identified, and now how do we want to mitigate the risk?

9588
16:34:37,774 --> 16:34:43,248
Then the security organization comes into the picture, the day-to-day activity of how security is to be implemented.

9589
16:34:43,488 --> 16:34:52,368
And for all of these procedures, policies, baseline, standards to be implemented, to be ensured that they are or to be assured that they are working properly.

9590
16:34:52,528 --> 16:34:56,288
We need to make our employees aware that these policies exist.

9591
16:34:56,448 --> 16:34:57,968
This is something that they need to follow.

9592
16:34:58,128 --> 16:35:01,488
Thus, the awareness or the security education comes into the picture.

9593
16:35:01,678 --> 16:35:10,288
Here, the security education is more focused on helping employees adhere to the company policies and standards rather than educating employees about security.

9594
16:35:10,448 --> 16:35:22,128
We don't want to make everybody an ethical hacker, we just want to ensure them or we want to assure the organization that everybody has been warned about policies, procedures, security requirements, and they are going to follow those requirements.

9595
16:35:22,288 --> 16:35:27,888
For example, a password policy where you have to adhere to a specific password policy to ensure passwords are created.

9596
16:35:28,048 --> 16:35:34,288
At the same time, there is another password policy which says that you should not share passwords with your colleagues no matter what.

9597
16:35:34,448 --> 16:35:42,608
So for me, for my employees to follow that, I need to make them aware that these policies exist and there are repercussions if they do not follow these particular policies.

9598
16:35:42,768 --> 16:35:46,608
So coming back to the governance part, what is governance since we have been talking about it?

9599
16:35:46,798 --> 16:35:51,888
Governance ensures that the security strategies are aligned with business objectives and consistent with regulations.

9600
16:35:52,048 --> 16:35:53,328
So what does it guarantee?

9601
16:35:53,488 --> 16:35:57,168
Appropriate information security activities are being performed based on what?

9602
16:35:57,328 --> 16:36:02,688
Based on the policies that we have created, based on the standards that we have and the baselines that we would have created.

9603
16:36:02,848 --> 16:36:05,168
So security has to be comparable, right?

9604
16:36:05,248 --> 16:36:16,768
So if I say I have secured against particular attacks, the attacks have to be identified and then compared to those particular attacks, I can say I have mitigated these activities by having specific security controls.

9605
16:36:16,928 --> 16:36:18,528
Thus, I can say that I am secure.

9606
16:36:18,688 --> 16:36:29,316
The governance aspects keep a watch on all these security controls to see that those security activities are ensured, are being implemented, and are performing to the best of their abilities.

9607
16:36:29,550 --> 16:36:31,396
That's where your governance comes into the picture.

9608
16:36:31,568 --> 16:36:41,604
The risks being reduced, so the risk management also comes under the governance where you're looking at newly identified risks and you're then implementing security controls that would mitigate these risks.

9609
16:36:41,728 --> 16:36:45,568
Then you're looking at information security investments appropriately directed.

9610
16:36:45,648 --> 16:36:57,088
So when I say I want a security control, which would be a firewall, IDS, ITS, antiviruses, whatever is required, it needs to have a return on investment which is acceptable for the organization.

9611
16:36:57,248 --> 16:37:01,968
You don't want to spend up too much of money in security, thus creating losses for the organization.

9612
16:37:02,190 --> 16:37:06,768
When you say a business, a business objective is always to make money rather than lose money.

9613
16:37:06,862 --> 16:37:19,168
So security should be a supporting feature to the business objectives where the services are being provided by the business in a secure manner in such a way that they can still have a positive return on investment on the services that are being provided.

9614
16:37:19,278 --> 16:37:22,208
And the executive management can determine the program's effectiveness.

9615
16:37:22,288 --> 16:37:33,408
This is the major part because we have to, even in any compliances, audits, technical audits, we have to prove that whatever we have implemented actually works, is effective, thus mitigating the risk.

9616
16:37:33,488 --> 16:37:41,568
If your security controls are not effective, then you have just wasted a bucket load of money and have not achieved any security measures in your exercise.

9617
16:37:41,920 --> 16:37:43,520
Moving on to security controls.

9618
16:37:43,760 --> 16:37:52,800
Security controls are measures taken to safeguard an information system from an attack basically for the CIA triad, the confidentiality, integrity, and availability.

9619
16:37:52,960 --> 16:37:58,000
So security controls could be administrative controls, technical controls, or physical security controls.

9620
16:37:58,094 --> 16:38:00,000
What are administrative security controls?

9621
16:38:00,160 --> 16:38:02,880
They would be policies, procedures that we have in place.

9622
16:38:03,054 --> 16:38:14,960
So the password policy becomes an administrative control where the management has made everybody aware that the password needs to meet a particular complexity, should not be shared, and thus becomes an administrative security control.

9623
16:38:15,120 --> 16:38:32,480
A technical security control would be where we are implementing this policy, and thus, when I try to create a password, there is a software that maps the password to the complexity requirements, assures or ensures that the complexity requirements have been met, and thus allows the password to be accepted or rejects the password as the case may be.

9624
16:38:32,720 --> 16:38:34,320
That is a security control.

9625
16:38:34,560 --> 16:38:42,960
A physical security control, maybe let's say a CCTV camera which you're going to use to monitor people and ensure that nothing untoward happens.

9626
16:38:43,120 --> 16:38:48,868
It's not only against physical crime, but let's say access to a particular room where nobody is seen tailgating.

9627
16:38:49,360 --> 16:38:59,360
Maybe monitoring a server room which is very well secured and ensuring that unauthorized people do not get access or purposefully or intently do not access that area at all times.

9628
16:38:59,534 --> 16:39:05,040
So these are the three levels of security controls that can be implemented to enhance the security of an organization.

9629
16:39:05,294 --> 16:39:06,960
Then we come to security policies.

9630
16:39:07,120 --> 16:39:13,200
Policies is an overall broad statement produced by a senior management that dictates the role of security within the organization.

9631
16:39:13,390 --> 16:39:30,480
For example, the password policy that we talked about, it just said that it needs to meet a certain complexity, which is a broad statement that is being made by the management saying we want to adhere to this policy to ensure that we do not get hacked by brute force attacks or dictionary attacks or whatever it is.

9632
16:39:30,640 --> 16:39:34,800
The broad statement is trying to prevent certain attacks from happening on the organization.

9633
16:39:34,960 --> 16:39:39,280
How it is implemented, that's where the procedures and everything else comes into the picture.

9634
16:39:39,440 --> 16:39:42,800
It must integrate security into all business processes.

9635
16:39:42,960 --> 16:39:58,320
So, if I start creating a policy which ensures that the complexity should be so high that the password should be at least 20 characters strong, that would be detrimental to the organization's health, because people may not remember those passwords or resetting those passwords, or having technical mechanisms.

9636
16:39:58,480 --> 16:40:04,720
technical security controls in place to implement that kind of stringent security may be too expensive to have.

9637
16:40:04,880 --> 16:40:10,000
So, whatever policies are created, they need to be in line with the business processes as well.

9638
16:40:10,222 --> 16:40:13,840
The policy must be reviewed and modified periodically, or as company changes.

9639
16:40:14,000 --> 16:40:22,240
So as and when the company becomes more mature and can have more levels of security, they must look at their policies again and redefine them.

9640
16:40:22,400 --> 16:40:29,280
Or as a company grows or changes market scenarios or changes business processes, these policies may become redundant.

9641
16:40:29,360 --> 16:40:36,400
So you want to look at those policies and implement newer policies or modify the existing policies to suit the business processes in a better manner.

9642
16:40:36,560 --> 16:40:39,120
It must support vision and mission of the organization.

9643
16:40:39,280 --> 16:40:42,320
A business once established will always have a long-term vision.

9644
16:40:42,480 --> 16:40:45,120
For example, the e-tailer example that we are talking about.

9645
16:40:45,280 --> 16:40:52,000
For me to open an e-tailer, e-retailing website, I don't want it to be periodic, I don't want it to be for a month or something like that.

9646
16:40:52,080 --> 16:40:57,988
I want to achieve success in that factor and I want to create a plan of where I want to reach, let's say in the next five years.

9647
16:40:58,414 --> 16:41:00,080
So, how do I achieve that?

9648
16:41:00,240 --> 16:41:02,640
That's something that I need to plan as a business plan.

9649
16:41:02,800 --> 16:41:12,480
All my security mechanisms that are in place should be in alignment with the business plans, business visions across the number of years that we have envisaged the business to perform.

9650
16:41:12,686 --> 16:41:14,640
So that's where the policies come into the picture.

9651
16:41:14,720 --> 16:41:24,480
Policies are always long-term, not short-term documents, but need to be periodically reviewed and modified as the business grows or business changes its posture.

9652
16:41:24,718 --> 16:41:25,840
Then looking at compliance.

9653
16:41:26,080 --> 16:41:30,880
Compliance means confirming to a rule such as a specification, policy, standard, or law.

9654
16:41:31,086 --> 16:41:38,880
So we have looked at compliance from a perspective where we talked about ISO 27001 or PCI DSS or those frameworks.

9655
16:41:38,960 --> 16:41:45,988
Now these are not laws, but these are standards of frameworks that have been created over a period of time through experience.

9656
16:41:46,320 --> 16:41:50,320
And these are targeted towards organizations towards specific industries.

9657
16:41:50,542 --> 16:41:56,640
For example, PCI DSS is only towards finance organizations where they have payment gateways in place.

9658
16:41:56,878 --> 16:42:02,240
The ISO 27001 is a more generic policy which can be implemented by various organizations.

9659
16:42:02,638 --> 16:42:08,996
HIPAA Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act is an American act that talks about health related data of its citizens.

9660
16:42:09,280 --> 16:42:15,520
So these are compliances, these may not be laws, but you need to adhere to these to be effective and to be.

9661
16:42:15,952 --> 16:42:18,352
Allowed to conduct business in that particular industry.

9662
16:42:18,512 --> 16:42:19,952
Then we have code of ethics.

9663
16:42:20,238 --> 16:42:31,232
Code of ethics state safety of the commonwealth, duty to our principles and to each other requires that we adhere and we seem to adhere to the highest ethical standards of behavior.

9664
16:42:31,470 --> 16:42:34,832
Therefore, strict adherence to this code is a condition of certification.

9665
16:42:34,992 --> 16:42:35,872
What are the ethics?

9666
16:42:36,192 --> 16:42:48,420
Protect society, the commonwealth and the infrastructure, act honorably, honestly, justly, responsibly, responsibility and legally, prove diligent and competent service to the principles, advance and profession.

9667
16:42:48,592 --> 16:42:56,580
Now the code of ethics are codes created by IIC Square for the CISSP professional and you need to adhere to these codes.

9668
16:42:56,752 --> 16:43:09,392
If they identify violation of these codes from any member, they would debar the member, strip them of the certification, and even blacklist them based on whatever ethics they have violated.

9669
16:43:09,552 --> 16:43:14,352
So even before the exam, you have to go through the code of ethics, you have to accept the code of ethics.

9670
16:43:14,542 --> 16:43:19,140
In the exam, you may get get up to five to six questions just on code of ethics.

9671
16:43:19,438 --> 16:43:21,072
Then comes the legal systems.

9672
16:43:21,472 --> 16:43:23,472
That's where the law comes into the picture.

9673
16:43:23,726 --> 16:43:32,592
Law could differ from country to country or from region to region, and thus, depending on which country you are in, you may need to be aware of those laws of those countries.

9674
16:43:32,782 --> 16:43:43,952
As far as CISSP is concerned, ISC Square is concerned, they are an American organization, so they would talk about mostly American laws and a few European laws or directives as well.

9675
16:43:44,112 --> 16:43:50,272
So there are three different laws that we talk about civil, common, and religious laws.

9676
16:43:50,542 --> 16:43:58,052
Depends on the country, the culture of that particular country, the information security professional should understand the different legal systems followed internationally.

9677
16:43:58,272 --> 16:44:05,152
So you also may come across the classification as civil law, criminal law, and law of torts.

9678
16:44:05,422 --> 16:44:11,152
Civil law is basically a law where let's say a contract has been breached and it's not criminal in nature.

9679
16:44:11,392 --> 16:44:14,432
You just want to sue an organization for breach of contract.

9680
16:44:14,606 --> 16:44:26,672
Criminal law is where something criminal has happened under the particular act of that particular country, like let's say a murder or an attack that has happened, a physical attack, or something got stolen.

9681
16:44:26,862 --> 16:44:39,472
And the third one was the law of torts where it's more of a compensatory law where you can file a case, a civil suit, demand compensation in lieu of the wrongs that have happened to that particular organization or that individual.

9682
16:44:39,758 --> 16:44:41,312
Then comes the personal security.

9683
16:44:41,552 --> 16:44:48,580
The person, the people inside the organization need access to data to complete their assigned work and hence have the potential to misuse these privileges.

9684
16:44:48,912 --> 16:44:52,672
And thus you should have personal security as well.

9685
16:44:52,910 --> 16:45:01,312
Now, when we talk about hiring practices for people, most of the organizations perform background checks, and we get confused why these background checks are being done.

9686
16:45:01,518 --> 16:45:20,752
The background checks are essential to the health of the organization where you are ensuring that the person is not a malicious person, has the prerequisite certifications or the qualifications, have never been debarred or have never been prosecuted, do not have a criminal background, thus preventing physical attacks on the organization.

9687
16:45:20,912 --> 16:45:32,192
For example, a competitor may attempt to place in your organization a spy to spy to gather information on the secrets of that organization and report it back to the competitor.

9688
16:45:32,352 --> 16:45:47,952
A background check may reveal that this person was previously employed by a competitor, and thus you might want to verify that the how that employment got terminated, when did it get terminated, or is this person misusing anything or misrepresenting any aspect of his or their life?

9689
16:45:48,192 --> 16:46:02,912
Then you also need to get confidentiality agreements signed with them, for example, NDAs, non-disclosure agreements, data that is being shared with them, the ownership of that particular data, and what are the repercussions if that confidentiality is breached by that particular person.

9690
16:46:03,214 --> 16:46:16,912
Conflict of interest agreements for positions handling competitive information, as you go higher up in the organization, you might come across contracts where it prohibits a particular person in engaging and act into the same activity with a competitor for a particular period of time.

9691
16:46:17,072 --> 16:46:33,712
So if there is a conflict of interest when you're hiring a person where they have signed such an agreement with another competitor or another organization, you don't want to embroil yourself in a lawsuit where the other organization may sue you for hiring a person while they were still under that particular contract.

9692
16:46:33,872 --> 16:46:42,992
So it's not only to look at the background of these people but to safeguard the organization from any lawsuits or any violations of contract as well.

9693
16:46:43,342 --> 16:46:47,232
Get the non compete agreements for the positions in charge of unique corporate processes.

9694
16:46:47,342 --> 16:46:55,232
That again goes on to say if you're going to leave the organization, you cannot start a competing business similar to the organization's business and so on so forth.

9695
16:46:55,406 --> 16:47:00,112
Now let's come back to the technical aspect and start talking about vulnerability threats and risk.

9696
16:47:00,526 --> 16:47:01,952
Now, what is a vulnerability?

9697
16:47:02,192 --> 16:47:04,832
It is nothing but a weakness in a system or a process.

9698
16:47:05,006 --> 16:47:07,232
Implies the absence of a countermeasure.

9699
16:47:07,470 --> 16:47:09,924
Vulnerability is internal and is more easily managed.

9700
16:47:10,126 --> 16:47:29,952
So it's basically a flaw or a misconfiguration or a design flaw or something like using defaults, username, passwords, configurations, where if that particular flaw is misused, it will lead into a security event on that particular device for that particular organization.

9701
16:47:30,222 --> 16:47:33,872
So when we say a security event, it has to be detrimental in nature.

9702
16:47:33,952 --> 16:47:45,552
So if somebody is using a weak password that can easily be cracked, that is a vulnerability where you have identified a weak password, and then you want to prevent that vulnerability by imposing a security control or a countermeasure.

9703
16:47:45,712 --> 16:48:05,552
The security control here starts off with the policy of having a robust security policy where a password policy comes in that describes the complexity of a particular password, and then you talk about a technical countermeasure where you have implemented an identity and access management plan which would ensure that the person creating that password adheres to the complexity of that particular policy.

9704
16:48:05,742 --> 16:48:06,512
Then a threat.

9705
16:48:06,672 --> 16:48:11,232
The threat is the possibility that the vulnerability might be exploited, which will result in loss.

9706
16:48:11,406 --> 16:48:22,432
So if somebody has created a weak password and it has been allowed, that's a vulnerability which can be exploited by the usage of brute force attacks or dictionary-based attacks to crack passwords.

9707
16:48:22,592 --> 16:48:29,152
If the password is cracked, somebody unauthorized may get access to that data, thus resulting in a loss for that organization.

9708
16:48:29,358 --> 16:48:34,180
So the threat is the possibility of that particular vulnerability being exploited.

9709
16:48:34,542 --> 16:48:35,712
And then comes the risk.

9710
16:48:35,886 --> 16:48:38,820
The risk is basically the likelihood of that attack being happened.

9711
16:48:39,072 --> 16:48:44,752
So I have a weak password, it can be exploited by a root for as a dictionary attack.

9712
16:48:44,912 --> 16:48:48,832
The likelihood of that attack is what determines the risk level for me.

9713
16:48:49,070 --> 16:48:56,112
Now the likelihood will always be hypothetical, but we don't want to run helter skelter and we do not want to take the possibility to extreme.

9714
16:48:56,192 --> 16:48:58,532
So that's where your business processes come into the picture.

9715
16:48:58,862 --> 16:49:00,752
Your security policies come into the picture.

9716
16:49:00,942 --> 16:49:09,152
Based on the business processes and the policies implemented, if I have a weak password, what would be the loss that I would face for that particular person?

9717
16:49:09,358 --> 16:49:28,352
For example, a fresher coming into an organization having a weak password, but having limited access to data, would have a lesser risk because of the loss being lesser than a CEO of an organization using a weak password and thus getting exploited, because the CEO will have access to more proprietary data.

9718
16:49:28,366 --> 16:49:33,472
that, if gets into the competitor's hand, would lead in huge losses.

9719
16:49:33,712 --> 16:49:44,804
Thus, the same risk of having a password crack for a fresher will have a lesser loss, thus, a lesser risk compared to the same risk to a CEO where the loss would be higher.

9720
16:49:45,006 --> 16:49:47,952
So a threat could also be in multiple aspects.

9721
16:49:48,142 --> 16:49:56,832
Threats could be natural, that means to fires, flooding, tornadoes, anything that is an act of God that can happen with minimal human interaction.

9722
16:49:57,072 --> 16:50:14,992
Or the second threat would be man-made, where man is responsible for it, like theft, hacking, rioting, war, anything that is created by a man, or rather humans and technical in nature, could be software bugs where vulnerabilities create are created or server failures where something technical has gone wrong.

9723
16:50:15,152 --> 16:50:21,392
and you need a technical AS skilled person to come in and repair that particular aspect.

9724
16:50:21,614 --> 16:50:27,632
Then the fourth one would be a supply system failure or a chain supply failure where you have dependencies.

9725
16:50:27,792 --> 16:50:36,912
For example, any organization will have a dependency on an electricity service provider where they are providing electricity as a service which powers on the IT infrastructure.

9726
16:50:37,072 --> 16:50:43,152
If the service provider fails and there is an electricity or a power cut, your systems are not going to work.

9727
16:50:43,392 --> 16:50:48,420
Thus, you need a secondary mechanism to prevent that threat from being realized.

9728
16:50:48,672 --> 16:50:57,632
Once we have identified these threats, we have identified these vulnerabilities and we have identified the likelihood and the probability of that attack, that's where we come across the risk management exercise.

9729
16:50:57,838 --> 16:50:59,232
Now, what is risk management?

9730
16:50:59,502 --> 16:51:08,592
Risk management is a hypothetical exercise where we have identified a vulnerability, we have looked at the threat that it may have and the likelihood of that threat being realized.

9731
16:51:08,752 --> 16:51:13,792
Once we have identified the threat, the risk level, that's where the risk management comes into the picture.

9732
16:51:13,998 --> 16:51:17,312
Reading from the slides, a core component of enterprise security program.

9733
16:51:17,472 --> 16:51:17,792
Why?

9734
16:51:17,952 --> 16:51:21,312
Because we need a baseline and we compare security to a baseline.

9735
16:51:21,422 --> 16:51:24,832
For example, I've got a firewall that has been configured in a particular manner.

9736
16:51:25,134 --> 16:51:36,068
Now, when I do a vulnerability assessment on the firewall, it the vulnerability assessment gives me a report that states that some of the rules may now be redundant, thus allowing access to unauthorized users.

9737
16:51:36,272 --> 16:51:38,592
This becomes a risk or this becomes a threat.

9738
16:51:38,766 --> 16:51:49,472
If this threat is identified by unauthorized people, they would then try to attack this firewall to gain access to that data which they are unauthorized to access, thus leading to losses for an organization.

9739
16:51:49,632 --> 16:51:51,312
What is the likelihood of this happening?

9740
16:51:51,502 --> 16:51:56,944
If the likelihood is high, or no, that's what the risk management program is all about, right?

9741
16:51:57,104 --> 16:52:06,624
And based on this risks, we implement security controls, we manage the security controls, and then we verify the security controls for future risks to coming into the picture.

9742
16:52:06,784 --> 16:52:16,144
It must be defined and it is always defined as an ongoing project because risks will always change based on the businesses, based on the security controls that you have.

9743
16:52:16,398 --> 16:52:29,156
This will give a guidance to an organization of how security is being implemented, how it has enhanced over a period of time, and how security mature that organization has become because of the risk assessment program.

9744
16:52:29,344 --> 16:52:33,664
It also helps you to satisfy two aspects due diligence and due care.

9745
16:52:33,838 --> 16:52:35,024
What is due diligence?

9746
16:52:35,278 --> 16:52:41,264
Due diligence is basically the research that organization does in identifying those risks and mitigating them.

9747
16:52:41,486 --> 16:52:47,824
The due care is the actual part of mitigation where you have implemented a security control to mitigate the risk that you have identified.

9748
16:52:48,014 --> 16:52:58,624
So if company is negligent, that means if the company doesn't have a risk management program, it is not going to be that security oriented or the security is not going to be that great.

9749
16:52:58,784 --> 16:53:08,864
Thus, the company may not comply to laws, regulations, and standards, thus causing them penalties, lawsuits, and other repercussions in the industry based on whatever they are doing.

9750
16:53:09,102 --> 16:53:11,104
For risk management, we come to frameworks.

9751
16:53:11,344 --> 16:53:17,264
Frameworks are used to categorize information systems so that we can identify threats or risks based on the classification.

9752
16:53:17,518 --> 16:53:23,024
So this classification helps us determine the criticality and the sensitivity of information system, right?

9753
16:53:23,184 --> 16:53:25,664
So what is the sensitivity and criticality?

9754
16:53:25,838 --> 16:53:29,584
It basically gives us an understanding of the value of the asset that we have.

9755
16:53:29,744 --> 16:53:36,064
So if you go back to domain one, the asset value or the asset identification, asset security, that's where it comes into the picture.

9756
16:53:36,224 --> 16:53:42,064
Obviously, a database server is going to be more secure than an end user laptop based on the data that they have.

9757
16:53:42,224 --> 16:53:46,756
Thus, the criticality of the system for a database is much higher than an end user's laptop.

9758
16:53:46,958 --> 16:53:49,860
Then we look at the security controls that can be implemented.

9759
16:53:50,144 --> 16:53:54,660
Then for those, the frameworks like ISO 27001 comes into the picture.

9760
16:53:54,926 --> 16:53:59,128
There is COBIT, there is there are a lot of frameworks out there, NIST 800,-53.

9761
16:53:59,824 --> 16:54:03,024
Then we talk about accessing the security controls.

9762
16:54:03,118 --> 16:54:07,588
Once we have placed those security controls, we need to verify those controls are placed properly.

9763
16:54:07,824 --> 16:54:14,384
They actually mitigate the risk and are appropriate to correctly mitigate that particular risk that they are created.

9764
16:54:14,734 --> 16:54:20,544
Then looking at authorizing information systems, grant information systems operations based on risks determined.

9765
16:54:20,624 --> 16:54:31,556
So you have identified risk, you have authorizing some of them, some of the devices to function in a particular manner based on which some of those risks would be mitigated or would be reduced to an acceptable level.

9766
16:54:31,758 --> 16:54:35,664
So in the CISSP we talk about a lot of risk assessment strategies.

9767
16:54:35,744 --> 16:54:37,904
We look at how we can manage risks.

9768
16:54:38,190 --> 16:54:49,424
The four different types of managing risks would be one is to mitigate, other is to transfer, the third is to accept it, and the fourth one is to neglect the risk.

9769
16:54:49,584 --> 16:54:55,664
So in the CISSP it talks in great details about those risk assessment strategies.

9770
16:54:55,854 --> 16:54:58,884
Uh, we are talking about the four types of risk mitigating strategies.

9771
16:54:59,104 --> 16:55:06,464
They are risk avoiding or avoiding the risks, accepting the risk, transferring the risk, and eliminating the risk.

9772
16:55:06,544 --> 16:55:17,584
So in the course we basically talk about a lot of risk strategies and how to mitigate these risks and how they can be implemented to bring down to an acceptable level.

9773
16:55:17,744 --> 16:55:22,304
So again, risk acceptance, risk appetite of an organization comes into the picture.

9774
16:55:22,384 --> 16:55:29,764
These terms are then discussed in the training to understand how the policies would work around the risk management strategy.

9775
16:55:30,350 --> 16:55:33,088
Moving on, then looking at monitoring security control.

9776
16:55:33,184 --> 16:55:47,424
So once we have security controls in place, we have assist them, we also need to monitor them for the effectiveness and though they are going to create any alerts or any risks are being realized, or any new risk that may be created by the security control implementation.

9777
16:55:47,584 --> 16:55:55,264
So, in these risk assessment, these are the frameworks, these are the steps that we want to follow to identify, mitigate, and monitor these risks.

9778
16:55:55,344 --> 16:55:57,584
So the management begins with risk assessment.

9779
16:55:57,838 --> 16:55:59,264
What do you mean by risk assessment?

9780
16:55:59,504 --> 16:56:09,956
Identifying the impact of the risk that the risk would have on the organization, and there would be two different ways to assess the risk: a quantitative assessment or a qualitative assessment.

9781
16:56:10,144 --> 16:56:20,544
A quantitative assessment is more quantifiable or is numerical in its process where you're looking at assigning costs to particular assets and the threats.

9782
16:56:20,864 --> 16:56:33,664
So the losses that you may figure out in a risk assessment strategy would be more numerical, where would be in a currency format, where let's say a risk if realized would lead to a loss of an X amount of dollars.

9783
16:56:33,870 --> 16:56:36,144
That's where the quantitative analysis comes into the picture.

9784
16:56:36,592 --> 16:56:39,392
The qualitative analysis would be quality of a service.

9785
16:56:39,472 --> 16:56:44,112
So if a service is being affected because of a risk, what is the degradation of that service?

9786
16:56:44,192 --> 16:56:46,752
To what aspect is it getting degraded?

9787
16:56:46,992 --> 16:56:48,992
For example, internet that we use.

9788
16:56:49,262 --> 16:56:52,992
If there is an internet outage or if there is a reduction in internet speed, right?

9789
16:56:53,230 --> 16:57:04,192
So if I've got a 10 MVPS line and now because of some fault of the ISP, it gets reduced to 4 MVPS, I may not be able to conduct the business that I would I was doing on a 10 MVPS line.

9790
16:57:04,352 --> 16:57:06,992
Does the quality of that device has degraded?

9791
16:57:07,152 --> 16:57:11,632
Uh thus it is going that does it is a risk and it is causing me losses, right?

9792
16:57:11,712 --> 16:57:16,932
So qualitative risk analysis would be more descriptive in nature rather than be numerical in nature.

9793
16:57:17,774 --> 16:57:26,512
most of the companies or organizations would follow a hybrid policy because it is not possible to have a quantitative quantitative risk analysis in each and every scenario.

9794
16:57:26,752 --> 16:57:44,752
New organization, which doesn't have much data to rely on, historical data of how threats have affected that particular organization may go in for a qualitative one, and as they mature over time in the security practices, they may then turn over to a quantitative risk analysis or a hybrid risk analysis where they're using.

9795
16:57:44,752 --> 16:57:47,364
a combination of quantitative and qualitative, both.

9796
16:57:47,598 --> 16:57:53,072
Thus, the risk controls that we were talking about risk acceptance, risk reduction, and risk assignment.

9797
16:57:53,472 --> 16:57:55,152
there is risk avoidance as well.

9798
16:57:55,552 --> 16:57:56,912
So there are four controls.

9799
16:57:57,166 --> 16:58:04,912
The risk reduction is where you have implemented a security control based on which the risk has been reduced to a more acceptable level.

9800
16:58:05,152 --> 16:58:12,352
Risk assignment is where you're uh also known as risk avoidance, that's where you're avoiding risk by outsourcing it.

9801
16:58:12,512 --> 16:58:18,592
So talking about risk controls again, the four types being accept, avoid, transfer, and reduce.

9802
16:58:18,832 --> 16:58:34,032
Risk acceptance is where uh your the organization is willingly accepting the risk as the way it is because they don't want to deal with it right now, they might deal with uh with it in the next iteration, or is it as an it is at an acceptable level and the company just wants to move on?

9803
16:58:34,272 --> 16:58:40,592
Avoiding the risk is where you are going to try to not use the service where the risk exists.

9804
16:58:40,832 --> 16:58:48,900
Thus, avoiding is where you have identified a particular service where the risk exists, and now you're not using the service to avoid the risk altogether.

9805
16:58:49,152 --> 16:59:04,512
Risk limitation or risk reduction would be where you are implementing a security control to mitigate the risk, and then risk transference is where risk assignment comes into the picture, where you are transferring it to a third party, like outsourcing it by using insurance to limit the risk.

9806
16:59:04,592 --> 16:59:10,592
So, for example, the threat is of a fire, the risk is high, but you cannot do anything about it.

9807
16:59:10,798 --> 16:59:11,952
So you purchase insurance.

9808
16:59:12,032 --> 16:59:17,312
So if the fire actually happens, you would still be reimbursed for the losses that you have faced.

9809
16:59:17,518 --> 16:59:28,592
Coming back to countermeasures, we have discussed administrative countermeasures, these are policies, rules, regulations, laws, customs that a company needs to adhere to based on the industry that they are in.

9810
16:59:28,782 --> 16:59:38,432
Then there are technical controls, software controls, anything that is technical in nature, IDS, IPS, firewalls, encryption, anything that is going to help you implement the CIA.

9811
16:59:38,670 --> 16:59:47,792
And then physical, where you would look at locks on doors, security guards, at fences where they're preventing access to unauthorized people.

9812
16:59:48,014 --> 16:59:50,532
Let's talk about roles and responsibilities of management.

9813
16:59:50,832 --> 17:00:00,832
Now, as far as management is concerned or the hierarchy is concerned, you can see on the screen there's a senior management, the security professional, data owner, custodian, and user.

9814
17:00:00,912 --> 17:00:04,912
Now, these are from exam perspective, from the certification perspective.

9815
17:00:05,072 --> 17:00:08,832
Obviously, the hierarchy in an organization would be slightly different.

9816
17:00:08,974 --> 17:00:16,432
But here the senior management is the responsible party for the entire security mechanism, so they must drive the entire security program.

9817
17:00:16,878 --> 17:00:27,392
So CIS SSP talks about a top-down approach where the senior management has realized the importance of security and is implementing at all levels within the organization.

9818
17:00:27,632 --> 17:00:33,152
Thus, they are a part of it and they are supporting the security program.

9819
17:00:33,392 --> 17:00:47,872
They define the tolerance of risk of how much risk is to be tolerated by an organization from a monetary perspective or the qualitative perspective as well, depending on the laws, legislations, regulations, compliances, and the contracts the business has signed.

9820
17:00:48,046 --> 17:00:55,552
They will rely on security professionals to manage the risk, but the exercise and the management process has to be defined by the senior management.

9821
17:00:55,712 --> 17:01:05,124
Once the security team comes up with the countermeasures, the senior management either approves or disapproves them based on the return on investment of that countermeasure.

9822
17:01:05,326 --> 17:01:14,192
The security professional assists with the development of policy documents, gives their inputs because they are the people who are in the day to day process of dealing with these.

9823
17:01:14,352 --> 17:01:21,952
Thus, they can identify gaps and give good inputs to modify or to create the policy in a more robust manner.

9824
17:01:22,126 --> 17:01:29,472
The security professionals may be a part of the risk assessment and risk management team as well, especially the security technical aspects of it.

9825
17:01:29,888 --> 17:01:32,448
Assist with the implementation and maintenance of countermeasures.

9826
17:01:32,528 --> 17:01:39,648
So the administrative, technical, or physical measures that you have identified, you may be requested to implement that.

9827
17:01:39,808 --> 17:01:47,168
For example, the locks that we have on the door, the swipe locks, they are connected to a database where the database is maintained.

9828
17:01:47,328 --> 17:01:50,688
So as a security professional, you wouldn't be responsible to fit those locks.

9829
17:01:50,768 --> 17:01:59,236
But to create the database, manage and maintain the database where that information is kept upon, where people are authorized and authenticated.

9830
17:01:59,408 --> 17:02:03,808
And would be responsible for monitoring, auditing, and security assessments as well.

9831
17:02:04,048 --> 17:02:09,408
The data owner, the owner of that particular data part, would normally be the head of the department.

9832
17:02:09,568 --> 17:02:22,928
For example, the sales data is normally owned by the director of sales of that department, so that they would be ultimately responsible for identifying that data, classifying that data, and accepting the security controls that are implemented.

9833
17:02:23,088 --> 17:02:26,928
They may request for additional security controls if they deem fit.

9834
17:02:27,088 --> 17:02:29,968
The security professional implements these security controls.

9835
17:02:30,286 --> 17:02:39,568
Then the custodian, a person who is responsible for implementing the approved security controls or managing the day-to-day activity of the security controls.

9836
17:02:39,728 --> 17:02:46,768
For example, the security professional has identified an access control to be implemented on a data set that the data owner owns.

9837
17:02:46,990 --> 17:02:48,688
The data owner has approved of it.

9838
17:02:48,846 --> 17:02:54,368
It is the custodian who is going to implement that strategy or implement the control on it.

9839
17:02:54,608 --> 17:03:10,368
The user is basically the user who or the person who accesses the information and IT resources, does whatever access controls have been implemented, would identify the user, authenticate, authorize them, and held them accountable for whatever they are doing.

9840
17:03:10,574 --> 17:03:13,568
If the user is unable to authenticate, access would be denied.

9841
17:03:13,968 --> 17:03:16,996
This domain will account for 21% of the exam.

9842
17:03:17,408 --> 17:03:20,068
And it's really the guts of an audit.

9843
17:03:20,368 --> 17:03:23,556
This is where we talk about how you actually conduct an audit.

9844
17:03:23,854 --> 17:03:32,688
At this point, we're not as concerned with regulations and laws and technical issues, so much as we're concerned with actual auditing process.

9845
17:03:32,942 --> 17:03:35,008
And it is a formal process.

9846
17:03:35,168 --> 17:03:37,028
It's a science, it's not an art.

9847
17:03:37,838 --> 17:03:40,048
Now, for the test, again, this is 21%.

9848
17:03:40,910 --> 17:03:46,048
But I would say, as an actual auditor in practice, this is exactly the place to start.

9849
17:03:46,208 --> 17:03:48,928
Domain one should be the process.

9850
17:03:49,198 --> 17:03:56,164
We need to get in the habit early on of having a methodical, scientific approach to auditing.

9851
17:03:57,166 --> 17:04:01,604
In this domain, we're going to have several lessons that walk you through the process.

9852
17:04:02,030 --> 17:04:04,388
So let's go ahead and get started with domain one.

9853
17:04:05,168 --> 17:04:13,988
After completing this domain, domain one, you'll be able to understand basically what an audit is and how an IS audit function should be managed.

9854
17:04:14,478 --> 17:04:19,524
You'll be able to detail the ISAC IS audit and assurance guidelines and standards.

9855
17:04:20,110 --> 17:04:26,500
You'll be able to discuss risks and how to analyze them, and discuss and understand internal controls.

9856
17:04:26,958 --> 17:04:29,444
You'll also be able to explain the control assessment.

9857
17:04:30,208 --> 17:04:38,308
You'll be able to demonstrate how an information system audit should be performed and expound upon the details of the audit process.

9858
17:04:38,798 --> 17:04:44,528
Possibly most importantly, you'll be able to apply the ISAC audit principles to your audit.

9859
17:04:45,006 --> 17:04:50,208
The TISA divides up the task for the test into task and knowledge statements.

9860
17:04:50,510 --> 17:04:55,408
We focus the lessons on knowledge statements, but those include the tasks as well.

9861
17:04:55,662 --> 17:05:00,528
Task statements are what a CISA candidate is expected to know how to do or perform.

9862
17:05:00,814 --> 17:05:04,448
Knowledge statements are what a CISA student should know.

9863
17:05:04,750 --> 17:05:11,008
If you first know the items involved, then applying them is all that's required to perform a task.

9864
17:05:11,168 --> 17:05:17,248
So while we break the lessons down by knowledge statements, you will also learn to perform the tasks.

9865
17:05:17,646 --> 17:05:23,588
Tasks and knowledge statements establish and maintain the process of auditing information systems.

9866
17:05:24,110 --> 17:05:27,488
Tasks can be mapped to more than one knowledge statement.

9867
17:05:27,728 --> 17:05:33,728
Now, within this and the subsequent lessons, we're going to walk you through what you need to know to pass the CISA.

9868
17:05:34,190 --> 17:05:41,828
It's important that you take time to study and make sure you fully understand each of these lessons before proceeding to the next lesson.

9869
17:05:42,510 --> 17:05:47,204
Let's begin with CISA Domain 1 Knowledge Statement 1.1.

9870
17:05:48,448 --> 17:06:05,476
That knowledge statement tells us that knowledge of ISAC IT audit and assurance standards, guidelines, tools, and techniques, the code of professional ethics, and other applicable standards are all required for those hoping to become CISA certified.

9871
17:06:08,494 --> 17:06:10,788
There's an explanation for this knowledge statement.

9872
17:06:11,246 --> 17:06:18,564
First and foremost, the credibility of any audit is based at least in part on the use of commonly accepted standards.

9873
17:06:19,438 --> 17:06:30,212
It just so happens that ISAC is a global pioneer of IS assurance and audit guidelines, tools and techniques, standards, and has a comprehensive code of professional ethics.

9874
17:06:31,214 --> 17:06:35,716
ISACA standards provide you with a benchmark for any information systems audit.

9875
17:06:38,606 --> 17:06:45,316
Now the main areas that are covered under this knowledge statement include first and foremost the ISACA Code of Professional Ethics.

9876
17:06:45,742 --> 17:06:51,104
That's important to keep in mind because ISACA will emphasize this in the CISA exam.

9877
17:06:51,310 --> 17:06:56,612
You need to know the Professional Ethics Code and be able to apply it to scenarios that you're given.

9878
17:06:58,544 --> 17:07:11,184
ISAC Information Systems Assurance and Audit Standards Framework as well as information systems assurance and audit guidelines will be covered, but in less detail than the professional ethics.

9879
17:07:11,344 --> 17:07:16,004
In other words, for the framework and the guidelines, you need a general understanding.

9880
17:07:17,806 --> 17:07:24,384
The ISAC Information Systems Assurance Audit Tools and Techniques will permeate the CISA exam.

9881
17:07:24,464 --> 17:07:31,844
You'll see some under this knowledge statement, and other tools and techniques will show up in other knowledge statements and even other domains.

9882
17:07:33,678 --> 17:07:40,228
And finally you need to understand the relationship between guidelines, tools, techniques, and standards.

9883
17:07:48,944 --> 17:07:52,064
The code of professional ethics is critical to understand.

9884
17:07:52,398 --> 17:07:57,956
We're going to read it right now, pausing with certain portions to give a little extra commentary where needed.

9885
17:07:58,990 --> 17:08:07,556
ISACA set forth a code governing the professional conduct and ethics of all certified AS auditors and all members of ISACA.

9886
17:08:08,270 --> 17:08:19,940
Members and certification holders shall support the implementation of and encourage compliance with appropriate standards, procedures, and controls for information systems.

9887
17:08:21,006 --> 17:08:28,196
Perform their duties with due diligence and professional care in accordance with professional standards and best practices.

9888
17:08:29,166 --> 17:08:39,012
Serve in the interest of stakeholders in a lawful and honest manner while maintaining high standards of conduct and character and not engage in acts discreditable to the profession.

9889
17:08:40,224 --> 17:08:47,780
Maintain the privacy and confidentiality of information obtained in the course of their duties unless disclosure is required by legal authority.

9890
17:08:48,302 --> 17:08:53,584
Such information shall not be used for personal benefit or released to inappropriate parties.

9891
17:08:53,838 --> 17:08:56,548
Now this last one requires some extra commentary.

9892
17:08:57,584 --> 17:09:10,788
As an IS auditor, you're going to see all sorts of confidential information, perhaps be exposed to proprietary data, processes, plans, personnel information, that sort of thing.

9893
17:09:11,184 --> 17:09:15,908
You must make confidentiality a cornerstone of your professional ethics.

9894
17:09:17,024 --> 17:09:28,356
Members and certification holders shall also maintain competency in their respective fields, and agree to undertake only those activities which they can reasonably expect to complete with professional competence.

9895
17:09:29,294 --> 17:09:42,948
What this means is, first and foremost, you have to continue learning, make sure you increase your competency, you're aware of new techniques and strategies, and also make sure you don't take on projects you're not fully qualified for.

9896
17:09:43,758 --> 17:09:50,020
Inform appropriate parties of the results of work performed, revealing all significant facts known to them.

9897
17:09:50,926 --> 17:09:57,504
Support the professional education of stakeholders in enhancing their understanding of information systems security and control.

9898
17:09:57,902 --> 17:10:00,356
This last one also bears some commentary.

9899
17:10:00,974 --> 17:10:07,664
The entire purpose of an audit is to inform the stakeholders of the situation of their information systems.

9900
17:10:08,144 --> 17:10:11,344
How does it stand in a relationship to security?

9901
17:10:11,584 --> 17:10:15,652
Well, your job when the audit is over is to educate the stakeholders.

9902
17:10:16,464 --> 17:10:33,444
Failure to comply with the Code of Professional Ethics can result in an investigation into a member's andor certification holder's conduct, and ultimately in disciplinary measures, ISACA may choose to ban you from ISACA or to revoke your certification if the situation warrants it.

9903
17:10:35,566 --> 17:10:41,380
IT Audit and Assurance Standards Framework established by ISACA has specific objectives.

9904
17:10:42,030 --> 17:10:51,680
IS auditors should be informed of the bare minimum level of performance needed to meet the professional responsibilities set out in the professional code of ethics.

9905
17:10:51,840 --> 17:10:53,188
Now keep that in mind.

9906
17:10:53,998 --> 17:10:56,324
These standards are the minimum.

9907
17:10:56,910 --> 17:10:58,692
You should always strive to do better.

9908
17:11:00,174 --> 17:11:06,532
The standard should also inform the management of the profession's requirements concerning the work of audit practitioners.

9909
17:11:07,502 --> 17:11:20,836
And these standards should also inform holders of CISA certification that failure to meet with these standards may result in a review into the CESA holder's conduct by the ISACA Board of Directors, which may ultimately result in disciplinary action.

9910
17:11:22,350 --> 17:11:31,556
ISACA IS Assurance Audit Guidelines provide additional information on how to comply with ISACA Information Technology Assurance and Audit Standards.

9911
17:11:32,526 --> 17:11:42,276
The information systems auditor should use professional judgment, which means sometimes you may differ from the standards, but you need to be able to justify any difference.

9912
17:11:42,960 --> 17:11:50,436
The guidelines are documents that are all identified by a prefix G followed by a number, for example, G10, G12, G20.

9913
17:11:50,894 --> 17:11:53,476
There's actually 42 categories of guidelines.

9914
17:11:54,254 --> 17:11:56,740
In the next few screens, we'll briefly look at all of them.

9915
17:11:57,120 --> 17:12:01,440
You don't have to memorize them, and therefore we won't read every one to you.

9916
17:12:01,710 --> 17:12:06,468
I will point out a few of them that are of particular importance on the certification test.

9917
17:12:09,600 --> 17:12:13,040
What we see here, do professional care.

9918
17:12:13,120 --> 17:12:17,988
That ties directly in with the professional code of ethics, so G7 is particularly important.

9919
17:12:19,118 --> 17:12:26,276
There's an entire knowledge statement on G10 audit sampling, which discusses how to do proper sampling.

9920
17:12:26,800 --> 17:12:31,460
Audit evidence requirement G2 also is prominent on the certification test.

9921
17:12:33,120 --> 17:12:38,724
G15 planning, a properly planned audit, is the only way you're going to have a good audit.

9922
17:12:39,150 --> 17:12:46,180
And of course, G20 reporting, the way you report your audit, is essential to communicating your findings to the stakeholders.

9923
17:12:48,378 --> 17:12:51,680
G28 computer forensics may seem like an odd one to you.

9924
17:12:51,886 --> 17:13:04,196
It's not required that you be a forensics expert or professional, but there is a forensics component and you need to have at least a basic understanding of forensics and able to effectively conduct an audit.

9925
17:13:06,478 --> 17:13:09,028
Continuous Assurance G42.

9926
17:13:09,358 --> 17:13:19,268
Even if your audit shows that everything meets standards, are we sure it will still meet standards a week from now, a month from now, any time period between now and the next audit?

9927
17:13:19,920 --> 17:13:25,892
G38 access controls, those are always a common point to check during your audit.

9928
17:13:28,366 --> 17:13:33,188
ISAC also has standards and guidelines related to audit, the ITAF.

9929
17:13:33,710 --> 17:13:35,972
And you can see here a listing of those standards.

9930
17:13:36,160 --> 17:13:41,060
As with the G standards, you don't have to memorize each of these, and we won't read them to you.

9931
17:13:41,454 --> 17:13:43,044
But you should be familiar with them.

9932
17:13:43,950 --> 17:13:47,236
This concludes Knowledge Statement 1.1,

9933
17:13:51,588 --> 17:13:54,244
CISA knowledge statement 1.2.

9934
17:13:55,534 --> 17:14:02,960
Knowledge of the risk assessment concepts and tools and techniques used in planning, examination, reporting, and follow-up.

9935
17:14:05,454 --> 17:14:11,460
First of all, your overall audit plan has to focus on business risks related to the use of IT.

9936
17:14:12,080 --> 17:14:17,412
Now throughout this course, we're going to look at a lot of methodologies, standards, and techniques.

9937
17:14:17,838 --> 17:14:24,420
But if you think about it for just a moment, I think you'll agree that information system auditing comes down to one simple concept.

9938
17:14:24,974 --> 17:14:33,600
Identify the risks that business faces, look at the controls in place to mitigate those risks, and evaluate the efficacy of those controls.

9939
17:14:33,934 --> 17:14:35,876
That's really what auditing is all about.

9940
17:14:37,454 --> 17:14:40,676
The area under our audit represents the audit scope.

9941
17:14:41,230 --> 17:14:44,688
We're not going to audit everything, so let's find out what we are going to audit.

9942
17:14:44,768 --> 17:14:46,212
What is the scope of our audit?

9943
17:14:47,310 --> 17:14:54,212
Auditors should use risk analysis techniques to find out what are the critical areas to focus on within the audit scope.

9944
17:14:54,542 --> 17:14:58,340
In other words, you determine the audit scope by risk analysis.

9945
17:14:59,406 --> 17:15:01,968
You obviously have limited audit resources.

9946
17:15:02,222 --> 17:15:14,564
You don't have an infinite number of personnel, number of hours, or number of dollars to spend on an audit, so this requires a focus in drawing the audit plan, and the focus is on the risks that are important to this business.

9947
17:15:15,246 --> 17:15:18,404
It's also important to prepare a proper audit report.

9948
17:15:18,926 --> 17:15:20,356
Think about it for just a moment.

9949
17:15:20,750 --> 17:15:29,252
Once you're done with your audit, one of the big goals is to inform the auditee of the issues you found and the remediation steps you recommend.

9950
17:15:30,350 --> 17:15:37,124
The primary communication vehicle you have for this is your audit report, so writing a good audit report is very important.

9951
17:15:38,048 --> 17:15:40,688
Follow up on issues that are found in the audit.

9952
17:15:40,768 --> 17:15:42,048
That's also very critical.

9953
17:15:42,222 --> 17:15:44,196
It's not enough to identify an issue.

9954
17:15:44,608 --> 17:15:47,268
It's not even enough to recommend remediation.

9955
17:15:47,662 --> 17:15:52,708
There has to be some process to discover a follow-up if the remediation was done.

9956
17:15:55,470 --> 17:15:58,788
The main areas we'll look at in this lesson include risk analysis.

9957
17:15:59,150 --> 17:16:09,188
Now, throughout this course, you're going to see other looks at risk analysis, but we'll begin in this lesson looking at risk analysis methodologies, processes, and techniques.

9958
17:16:09,742 --> 17:16:11,748
This leads us to audit methodology.

9959
17:16:12,174 --> 17:16:16,688
Now that's another issue that will permeate the course, obviously, various methodologies.

9960
17:16:16,942 --> 17:16:18,244
We'll begin in this lesson.

9961
17:16:19,022 --> 17:16:25,316
Our whole focus is on risk-based auditing, using risk analysis to drive the entire audit process.

9962
17:16:26,318 --> 17:16:32,420
We also will be discussing inherent risks in the audit itself, what we call audit risk and materiality.

9963
17:16:33,408 --> 17:16:41,284
We'll begin looking at risk assessment, how do we determine what is a risk, and treatment, what are our steps to remediate a risk.

9964
17:16:42,254 --> 17:16:45,796
This will involve beginning your introduction to risk assessment techniques.

9965
17:16:46,382 --> 17:16:49,088
We'll also be discussing reporting techniques and follow-up.

9966
17:16:53,518 --> 17:16:55,684
Let's start with looking at risk analysis.

9967
17:16:55,968 --> 17:17:01,028
This is a process that helps an auditor recognize the vulnerabilities and risks.

9968
17:17:01,422 --> 17:17:02,468
That's the first part.

9969
17:17:02,894 --> 17:17:08,228
You have to be aware of what the risks and vulnerabilities to this specific organization are.

9970
17:17:08,608 --> 17:17:11,684
Now some risks and some vulnerabilities are common to everyone.

9971
17:17:12,048 --> 17:17:14,372
Everyone is at risk for a virus outbreak.

9972
17:17:14,702 --> 17:17:17,188
Everyone is at risk for a fire in the server room.

9973
17:17:17,870 --> 17:17:21,156
But we also have very specific risks to specific industries.

9974
17:17:21,888 --> 17:17:29,604
Then we need to look at how do we define controls that can be put in place or may already be in place to mitigate those risks.

9975
17:17:30,350 --> 17:17:35,568
Now throughout this lesson, you're going to see several different phrasings of the definition of risk.

9976
17:17:35,918 --> 17:17:41,732
They're all worded slightly differently because they come from different sources, but they all essentially mean the same thing.

9977
17:17:42,062 --> 17:17:43,652
Let's start with this first one.

9978
17:17:44,368 --> 17:17:49,476
Risk is defined as the mixture of the likelihood of an event and its magnitude.

9979
17:17:50,158 --> 17:17:53,828
First of all, an event we're defining as some negative incident.

9980
17:17:54,350 --> 17:17:55,780
How likely is it to happen?

9981
17:17:56,110 --> 17:17:58,116
Some events are more likely than others.

9982
17:17:58,414 --> 17:18:00,772
Not everything has an equal likelihood.

9983
17:18:01,390 --> 17:18:08,036
For example, for any organization, the likelihood of a hard drive crash in a server is relatively high.

9984
17:18:08,590 --> 17:18:18,528
But the likelihood of an intrusion by a state-sponsored cyber terrorist is only high for certain businesses, high-tech companies, defense contractors.

9985
17:18:18,702 --> 17:18:22,288
It's extremely low for a pizza delivery business or a bookseller.

9986
17:18:22,688 --> 17:18:24,804
So we have to look at the likelihood of an event.

9987
17:18:25,328 --> 17:18:27,172
Then let's look at the magnitude.

9988
17:18:27,534 --> 17:18:30,628
Not all events have the same magnitude for every industry.

9989
17:18:31,374 --> 17:18:32,900
Let's consider a hypothetical.

9990
17:18:33,390 --> 17:18:37,764
An event that causes your web server to crash and to be offline temporarily.

9991
17:18:38,368 --> 17:18:40,768
Let's assume your business is pizzas.

9992
17:18:41,008 --> 17:18:42,048
You sell pizzas.

9993
17:18:42,350 --> 17:18:43,972
You have a chain of pizza restaurants.

9994
17:18:44,526 --> 17:18:49,328
If your web server is down, some of your customers will not do business with you during that time.

9995
17:18:49,678 --> 17:18:52,288
They can't order online, so they will order from a competitor.

9996
17:18:52,368 --> 17:18:53,636
You will lose some business.

9997
17:18:53,968 --> 17:18:56,932
But some customers will come into your restaurants and eat there.

9998
17:18:57,358 --> 17:18:59,168
Some will come in and get takeout.

9999
17:18:59,568 --> 17:19:04,676
Some will phone in for their delivery orders, so you will lose some business, but by no means all.

10000
17:19:05,646 --> 17:19:10,688
Now let's assume the same web server crash, but your business is that of e-commerce.

10001
17:19:10,928 --> 17:19:13,252
You sell things online, it's what you do.

10002
17:19:13,934 --> 17:19:21,028
If your web server is down, you're making zero money, you're losing all dollars for every minute the web server is down.

10003
17:19:21,870 --> 17:19:25,488
Now in both scenarios, the web server going down is negative.

10004
17:19:25,934 --> 17:19:26,916
It's a risk.

10005
17:19:27,182 --> 17:19:30,756
But the magnitude is different in the second scenario than in the first.

10006
17:19:31,918 --> 17:19:34,212
Let's look at another definition that's very similar.

10007
17:19:35,278 --> 17:19:46,272
IT risk is specifically the enterprise risk associated with the ownership, use, operation, influence, involvement, and adoption of information technology within a business.

10008
17:19:46,638 --> 17:19:48,260
Now let's think about that for just a moment.

10009
17:19:49,070 --> 17:19:52,292
We all like new technologies, myself more than most.

10010
17:19:53,070 --> 17:19:57,988
The convenience, the productivity, all these things that are afforded us by new technologies.

10011
17:19:58,542 --> 17:20:02,276
But every technology also involves a risk simply by owning and using it.

10012
17:20:02,862 --> 17:20:13,540
For example, we already mentioned websites that expand your customer base, let you sell to customers more conveniently, and in some cases to customers in other areas that you normally wouldn't get to reach.

10013
17:20:13,872 --> 17:20:22,724
However, every website is vulnerable to denial of service attacks, SQL injection, cross-site scripting, and a variety of other attacks.

10014
17:20:23,630 --> 17:20:25,872
Smartphones, these are now ubiquitous.

10015
17:20:25,952 --> 17:20:29,092
Everyone has one, and I personally depend on mine all the time.

10016
17:20:29,552 --> 17:20:35,872
And many people now bring these phones into their workplace and connect them to the organization's Wi-Fi.

10017
17:20:36,032 --> 17:20:38,884
This is referred to as BYOD, bring your own device.

10018
17:20:39,712 --> 17:20:42,244
Well, that poses a lot of risks.

10019
17:20:42,510 --> 17:20:49,152
Yes, it's incredibly convenient, and it allows employees to blend work with personal time.

10020
17:20:49,390 --> 17:20:59,748
They may on their own time address a work issue through their phone, and they may be able to take a critical personal issue, such as a sick relative, and still come to work because of the access they have with the phone.

10021
17:21:00,206 --> 17:21:01,552
So it blends our worlds.

10022
17:21:01,632 --> 17:21:11,748
However, you now have attached to your Wi-Fi a diverse number of phones with a wide range of operating systems, software, and hardware.

10023
17:21:12,112 --> 17:21:13,316
This is a risk.

10024
17:21:13,872 --> 17:21:19,012
So every technology, just owning it and using it, gives us some risk to the enterprise.

10025
17:21:23,470 --> 17:21:32,036
Let's consider two other definitions of risks, and these are slightly worded differently, but very similar and almost identical in meaning to what we've already seen.

10026
17:21:33,390 --> 17:21:36,912
The probable frequency and probable magnitude of a future loss.

10027
17:21:37,152 --> 17:21:41,540
Now, what I really like about this definition is the use of the word probable.

10028
17:21:43,246 --> 17:21:50,404
It's unlikely that you'll be able to know exactly how frequent an event may occur, or the exact magnitude.

10029
17:21:50,670 --> 17:21:52,356
You have to perform an estimate.

10030
17:21:53,552 --> 17:22:01,312
The second definition, the potential that a given threat will exploit vulnerabilities of an asset or group of assets and cause harm to the organization.

10031
17:22:01,472 --> 17:22:08,516
Now this comes from the standard ISO 27005, which you're definitely going to see on the CISA exam.

10032
17:22:08,974 --> 17:22:14,752
Not just this definition, but you'll see more about the standard and we'll revisit again in future lessons.

10033
17:22:14,912 --> 17:22:18,052
Definitely be familiar with ISO 27005.

10034
17:22:18,512 --> 17:22:21,792
But I like this definition because it emphasizes two things.

10035
17:22:22,126 --> 17:22:25,540
First and foremost, we're concerned about harm to the organization.

10036
17:22:26,032 --> 17:22:32,100
If a particular IT system is offline, but it doesn't harm the organization, it's not that big a concern.

10037
17:22:32,686 --> 17:22:36,420
If you have 20 printers and one of them is offline, it's an inconvenience.

10038
17:22:36,832 --> 17:22:39,300
It doesn't have great harm to the organization.

10039
17:22:39,952 --> 17:22:48,260
I also like this definition because it's talking about the exploiting of vulnerabilities, and that's what we're really concerned about in risk mitigation.

10040
17:22:52,430 --> 17:23:02,532
Now the process of risk analysis is complex and involved, and it's applied in a variety of areas, such as disaster recovery planning and business continuity planning.

10041
17:23:03,376 --> 17:23:07,140
But let's look at it specifically from the auditor's point of view.

10042
17:23:08,654 --> 17:23:14,096
From the information system audit point of view, risk analysis is used for the following purposes.

10043
17:23:14,478 --> 17:23:18,852
First, it helps the auditor identify threats and risks within the IS environment.

10044
17:23:19,182 --> 17:23:22,496
We've already stated that your audit has to be risk driven.

10045
17:23:22,798 --> 17:23:28,804
But that begins by identifying the threats and risks that are of most concern in this specific environment.

10046
17:23:29,710 --> 17:23:33,456
It also lets you plan the audit by looking at the controls in place.

10047
17:23:33,616 --> 17:23:38,756
And we look at those controls in light of the specific risks and threats we've already identified.

10048
17:23:39,374 --> 17:23:42,340
Now you're in a position to know the audit objectives.

10049
17:23:42,990 --> 17:23:50,884
You're basically testing to see if the controls in place appropriately and adequately mitigate the threats and risks you've identified.

10050
17:23:51,278 --> 17:23:57,348
This makes decision making a much easier process when you're using risk-based methodology.

10051
17:23:57,856 --> 17:24:01,056
Now on the right hand side of the screen here, you see several steps.

10052
17:24:01,358 --> 17:24:07,908
These are actually occurring initially in a linear fashion for our initial audit, but then in an ongoing basis.

10053
17:24:08,238 --> 17:24:10,692
Let's start with identifying business objectives.

10054
17:24:11,150 --> 17:24:14,468
This literally means what is the business trying to do?

10055
17:24:14,830 --> 17:24:20,068
Now you can think about this in a very broad scope, but we're being more specific.

10056
17:24:20,334 --> 17:24:27,812
For example, if you have a pizza business, their objective is to sell pizzas, but we need to break this down much more fine-tuned.

10057
17:24:28,238 --> 17:24:32,496
For example, the objective of the website is to allow people to order online.

10058
17:24:33,390 --> 17:24:35,696
Underneath that objective, we have sub-objectives.

10059
17:24:35,856 --> 17:24:38,948
We want to be able to effectively and securely process credit cards.

10060
17:24:39,342 --> 17:24:43,684
We want it to be a user-friendly experience, and there may be a host of other objectives.

10061
17:24:43,982 --> 17:24:49,604
But before you can even begin looking at the audit, you have to know what the business is trying to do.

10062
17:24:50,126 --> 17:24:57,060
Now that flows very naturally into identifying those information assets that support the business objective.

10063
17:24:57,678 --> 17:25:05,776
It may sound odd, but normally an organization has a number of information systems, some of which are not absolutely critical to the business objective.

10064
17:25:06,126 --> 17:25:07,780
We're concerned about those that are.

10065
17:25:08,256 --> 17:25:14,656
Again, if you have 20 printers and most of your business is online anyway, it doesn't require printing for each transaction.

10066
17:25:15,022 --> 17:25:17,956
Printing is used for things like monthly reports and invoicing.

10067
17:25:18,478 --> 17:25:21,124
Well then one printer being down is not a big concern.

10068
17:25:21,614 --> 17:25:23,908
The web server being down is a huge concern.

10069
17:25:24,816 --> 17:25:34,500
Now that you've identified the information assets that support those business objectives, now we flow straight to doing a risk assessment on those assets.

10070
17:25:35,246 --> 17:25:37,124
What are the threats to those assets?

10071
17:25:37,486 --> 17:25:42,084
What vulnerabilities are in those assets that would allow a threat to be realized?

10072
17:25:43,022 --> 17:25:44,416
And what would be the impact?

10073
17:25:44,656 --> 17:25:49,828
Now impact often involves something we haven't discussed yet called a criticality analysis.

10074
17:25:50,542 --> 17:25:57,252
That's just a nice way of saying we look at each particular asset and evaluate how critical it is to the organization.

10075
17:25:57,710 --> 17:26:03,556
And that's often based simply on how much damage would it cause if that particular asset wasn't available.

10076
17:26:04,256 --> 17:26:08,388
Now that you've done a risk assessment, now we can do risk management.

10077
17:26:08,736 --> 17:26:12,388
Let's look at the risks and map them to existing controls.

10078
17:26:14,766 --> 17:26:18,820
Although that can be an involved process, it really comes down to two questions.

10079
17:26:19,534 --> 17:26:22,896
Are there controls in place that address each and every risk you have?

10080
17:26:23,136 --> 17:26:29,060
Any place you have a risk that does not have a control, that's an obvious place that needs to be addressed.

10081
17:26:29,936 --> 17:26:36,176
The second question is: assuming there is a control in place for that risk, does it adequately mitigate the risk?

10082
17:26:36,336 --> 17:26:39,172
Does it bring the risk down to a level that's tolerable?

10083
17:26:40,016 --> 17:26:43,748
Once you've done that, now we can do risk treatment.

10084
17:26:44,270 --> 17:26:51,332
Let's look at those risks that are not mitigated by existing controls, or at least the existing controls don't adequately mitigate it.

10085
17:26:51,918 --> 17:26:55,268
Now, as I mentioned, your initial audit, this is a linear process.

10086
17:26:55,776 --> 17:26:57,668
But I think you'll agree things change.

10087
17:26:58,126 --> 17:27:00,324
Business objectives change all the time.

10088
17:27:00,656 --> 17:27:03,428
Sometimes a business may add on new objectives.

10089
17:27:03,726 --> 17:27:08,772
They may leave old objectives, they may add on a new line of business or abandon a line of business.

10090
17:27:09,376 --> 17:27:11,620
Information assets definitely change.

10091
17:27:12,078 --> 17:27:17,188
There are new operating systems, there are changes to hardware, all of these change things.

10092
17:27:17,614 --> 17:27:18,896
Certain threats change.

10093
17:27:19,056 --> 17:27:26,212
For example, SQL injection is still a threat, but it's less of a threat today than it was five years ago.

10094
17:27:26,670 --> 17:27:37,604
Denial of service attacks are still a big threat, but a specific old one called a SIN flood, SYN flood, is not near as much a danger today as it once was due to modern firewalls.

10095
17:27:37,966 --> 17:27:39,012
Threats have changed.

10096
17:27:39,470 --> 17:27:42,852
On the other hand, there are new threats that didn't exist five years ago.

10097
17:27:43,278 --> 17:27:47,012
All of these changes cause this process to now become cyclical.

10098
17:27:47,630 --> 17:27:52,516
We will reevaluate the risk and repeat the process.

10099
17:27:57,390 --> 17:28:04,900
There are some basic risk assessment terms, and these are borrowed from business continuity and disaster recovery planning that you need to be familiar with.

10100
17:28:05,582 --> 17:28:08,100
Assets are the resources you're trying to protect.

10101
17:28:08,416 --> 17:28:13,252
Now your natural inclination may be to think about servers, and those are certainly assets.

10102
17:28:13,774 --> 17:28:21,892
But I think if you'll reflect on this for a moment you'll agree that in most cases the data on the server is worth a lot more than the server itself.

10103
17:28:23,086 --> 17:28:34,308
Risk, and this is yet another definition that may seem to be worded differently, but means essentially the same that we've seen so far, is the potential that a chosen action or activity will lead to a loss.

10104
17:28:35,214 --> 17:28:38,404
Threats, any negative action that could harm a system.

10105
17:28:39,438 --> 17:28:43,140
Vulnerabilities, any weakness that allows a threat to cause harm.

10106
17:28:43,950 --> 17:28:45,856
Impact, the severity of damage.

10107
17:28:46,094 --> 17:28:48,900
Whenever possible we like to express this in dollars.

10108
17:28:53,390 --> 17:28:57,092
Now to express that in dollars, we have some very specific formulas.

10109
17:28:57,742 --> 17:29:01,860
These formulas are also borrowed from disaster recovery and business continuity planning.

10110
17:29:02,510 --> 17:29:03,716
Exposure factor.

10111
17:29:04,016 --> 17:29:07,396
This is the percentage value of an asset lost due to an incident.

10112
17:29:07,616 --> 17:29:11,332
Now what we mean is in many cases you won't completely lose an asset.

10113
17:29:11,854 --> 17:29:15,236
Let's assume you have a database and you do a full backup every hour.

10114
17:29:15,726 --> 17:29:18,980
Then the worst you can lose is 59 minutes worth of data.

10115
17:29:19,662 --> 17:29:25,796
Because if the database crashes one minute before your next backup, you will have lost those 59 minutes.

10116
17:29:26,254 --> 17:29:28,496
You can recover all the other data.

10117
17:29:28,942 --> 17:29:35,776
Now if we look at a single loss expectancy, that means what do we lose in a single negative incident?

10118
17:29:36,174 --> 17:29:41,028
We start with the asset value, how much is the asset worth, times that exposure factor.

10119
17:29:41,936 --> 17:29:44,356
That will give us a single loss expectancy.

10120
17:29:45,262 --> 17:29:47,844
Next, we have to consider the annual rate of occurrence.

10121
17:29:48,206 --> 17:29:51,076
That's the number of losses you might expect to have in a year.

10122
17:29:51,630 --> 17:29:55,908
Now this can be effectively estimated through a number of different methods.

10123
17:29:56,206 --> 17:29:58,020
First, look at previous years.

10124
17:29:58,576 --> 17:30:02,016
What was the number of losses last year and the year before?

10125
17:30:02,350 --> 17:30:14,336
Then there are a variety of security firms that every year publish reports that tell you the preceding years, various losses, various attacks, various threats broken down by industry and size of company.

10126
17:30:14,576 --> 17:30:23,696
For example, insurance companies that do over $20 million a year in business, but less than $100 million, how many virus outbreaks do they have on average?

10127
17:30:23,950 --> 17:30:26,308
That sort of information is publicly available.

10128
17:30:26,656 --> 17:30:28,772
Now I want to stop and point out something here.

10129
17:30:29,296 --> 17:30:32,580
There are a number of things throughout this process that are estimates.

10130
17:30:33,134 --> 17:30:34,820
Annual rate of occurrence is one.

10131
17:30:35,374 --> 17:30:38,596
But as I was just indicating, an estimate doesn't mean a wild guess.

10132
17:30:38,958 --> 17:30:42,016
You shouldn't just think and come up with some random number.

10133
17:30:42,318 --> 17:30:44,580
Annual rate of occurrence is 15 times.

10134
17:30:44,976 --> 17:30:48,132
That's very ineffective and in fact counterproductive.

10135
17:30:48,576 --> 17:30:55,776
But if you look at your prior year's annual rate of occurrence, how many virus outbreaks did you have a year ago, two years ago, three years ago?

10136
17:30:56,142 --> 17:30:57,604
Is there an upward trend in them?

10137
17:30:57,998 --> 17:31:07,776
If you look at industry reports that give you the norm for your industry and size of business, and you use all of this to estimate the annual rate of occurrence, then yes, it's an estimate.

10138
17:31:07,936 --> 17:31:10,276
No, it's not exact, but it's a good estimate.

10139
17:31:10,862 --> 17:31:20,862
You should do the same thing when looking at asset value, exposure factor, or any number of factors that we've already discussed or will discuss, where we say it's an estimate.

10140
17:31:21,230 --> 17:31:22,896
estimates should never be guess.

10141
17:31:23,630 --> 17:31:29,536
I would also recommend that in your final audit report you actually include the basis for your estimates.

10142
17:31:29,646 --> 17:31:30,788
Where'd you get this number?

10143
17:31:31,950 --> 17:31:35,556
Now with that said, we're ready to compute annualized loss expectancy.

10144
17:31:36,270 --> 17:31:38,244
That's the yearly cost due to a risk.

10145
17:31:38,478 --> 17:31:41,956
You take the single loss expectancy times the annual rate of occurrence.

10146
17:31:42,414 --> 17:31:45,056
And normally this is done per asset per risk.

10147
17:31:45,136 --> 17:31:48,896
In other words, the web server and denial of service attacks.

10148
17:31:49,102 --> 17:31:51,012
What's the annualized rate of occurrence?

10149
17:31:51,406 --> 17:31:53,316
What's the annualized loss expectancy?

10150
17:31:53,936 --> 17:32:00,292
Now the reason we do this is we can now calculate the cost effectiveness of mitigating controls.

10151
17:32:00,686 --> 17:32:04,644
Let's say you've identified denial of service attacks as a risk to your website.

10152
17:32:05,456 --> 17:32:09,856
Let's say you have 10 occurrences a year, each one costs roughly $1,000.

10153
17:32:10,478 --> 17:32:12,772
So $10,000 a year will be lost.

10154
17:32:13,550 --> 17:32:16,676
Well now we have to ask a question: what will it cost to mitigate that?

10155
17:32:17,582 --> 17:32:25,936
If there's a control that will reduce that to two times a year, so our loss goes from $10,000 a year to $2,000 a year, should we implement that control?

10156
17:32:26,176 --> 17:32:27,696
Well, it's a very simple formula.

10157
17:32:28,014 --> 17:32:29,456
How much does the control cost?

10158
17:32:29,934 --> 17:32:35,108
If it costs $5,000 but saves us $8,000, then it's well worth it.

10159
17:32:35,662 --> 17:32:41,124
If it costs $15,000 and saves us $8,000, we're actually better off without it.

10160
17:32:46,352 --> 17:32:50,148
Now let's look at the three formulas at the bottom that use these terms.

10161
17:32:50,478 --> 17:32:54,980
Again, some of these items like annual rate of occurrence will be estimates.

10162
17:32:55,246 --> 17:32:57,312
Exposure factor will be estimates.

10163
17:32:57,486 --> 17:33:02,948
But please use as much data as possible to make these good estimates, not just wild guesses.

10164
17:33:04,174 --> 17:33:21,232
If you take the value of the asset, however much it was worth, that can include purchase cost, maintenance cost, development cost, whatever the complete value of the asset is, multiply that by the exposure factor, the result is the single loss expectancy.

10165
17:33:21,454 --> 17:33:24,836
How much you expect to lose if there's a single loss.

10166
17:33:26,158 --> 17:33:27,072
What is risk?

10167
17:33:27,552 --> 17:33:32,832
You take the probability of something happening times the cost if that happened.

10168
17:33:33,072 --> 17:33:37,552
For example, what would it cost you for your server to be down for a week?

10169
17:33:37,774 --> 17:33:38,980
Let's say your web server.

10170
17:33:39,854 --> 17:33:40,832
Calculate that cost.

10171
17:33:40,912 --> 17:33:45,412
Now that should be something you can do with concrete numbers with very little guessing.

10172
17:33:45,998 --> 17:33:53,060
Now you try to estimate the probability of a risk, multiply that times the cost, and you have your risk value.

10173
17:33:54,606 --> 17:33:57,472
Next, let's look at annualized loss expectancy.

10174
17:33:57,742 --> 17:34:13,348
You take that single loss expectancy you previously computed, multiply it times that annual rate of occurrence, and remember this is an estimate, but it's an estimate hopefully based on statistical averages, past performance, and reports from industry.

10175
17:34:13,646 --> 17:34:20,420
But in any case, once you multiply ARO times SLE, you have the annualized loss expectancy.

10176
17:34:21,006 --> 17:34:30,752
These three formulas and these terms are central to impact analysis, thus they're central to business continuity planning and disaster recovery planning.

10177
17:34:30,958 --> 17:34:33,632
In other words, this is how you calculate risk.

10178
17:34:33,934 --> 17:34:36,804
Risk should have as little guessing as possible.

10179
17:34:37,518 --> 17:34:44,420
The risk-based audit approach is simply based on the concept of determining which areas should be audited based on the level of risk.

10180
17:34:44,686 --> 17:34:51,940
The things we've already talked about, including the formulas we just looked at, are how you decide the level of risk.

10181
17:34:53,230 --> 17:35:02,272
Now, once you've looked at a risk and you've looked at controls, even added or enhanced controls, you have not totally eliminated risks.

10182
17:35:02,478 --> 17:35:05,476
There is some risk left over, and that's called residual risk.

10183
17:35:05,966 --> 17:35:08,484
Now, how much residual risk is okay?

10184
17:35:09,230 --> 17:35:11,748
Well, that depends on the management's risk appetite.

10185
17:35:12,174 --> 17:35:21,060
So the goal of an audit is to make sure mitigating controls, reduce risk, take that residual risk down to a level that's acceptable to management.

10186
17:35:22,112 --> 17:35:24,832
We have another risk, risks in the audit itself.

10187
17:35:24,992 --> 17:35:26,992
These aren't risks from outside threats.

10188
17:35:27,246 --> 17:35:32,432
These are risks, for example, your report might contain an error, an error that's material.

10189
17:35:32,838 --> 17:35:36,592
A Simple typo isn't what we're concerned about, although those certainly look bad.

10190
17:35:36,910 --> 17:35:39,140
We're concerned about an error that's material.

10191
17:35:39,792 --> 17:35:43,652
This can be through some mistake in your reporting or auditing process.

10192
17:35:44,334 --> 17:35:50,404
Or it's possible that an audit might not detect a specific threat, risk, or vulnerability.

10193
17:35:50,862 --> 17:35:54,980
That audit risk that something might be undetected is very important.

10194
17:35:58,542 --> 17:36:00,004
Now, risk assessment.

10195
17:36:00,750 --> 17:36:03,152
We've already looked at that in in some depth.

10196
17:36:03,342 --> 17:36:04,868
Let's summarize it now.

10197
17:36:08,526 --> 17:36:17,072
Risk assessment involves identifying risks, prioritizing them, which are more important, and as much as possible quantifying the risk.

10198
17:36:17,326 --> 17:36:21,252
That involves some of those formulas like ALE and SLE that we already looked at.

10199
17:36:21,774 --> 17:36:25,764
Now we evaluate those against some criteria for risk tolerance.

10200
17:36:25,952 --> 17:36:29,316
What are the objectives and risk tolerance for this organization?

10201
17:36:30,112 --> 17:36:34,352
Risk assessments have to be carried out regularly because things change.

10202
17:36:34,734 --> 17:36:42,340
The risk environment change, regulatory requirements change, legal requirements change, the risk appetite of a business may change.

10203
17:36:42,672 --> 17:36:47,152
So it's not adequate to base your audit on a really old risk assessment.

10204
17:36:47,374 --> 17:36:52,452
The risk assessment should either immediately precede your audit or very soon before your audit.

10205
17:36:56,494 --> 17:37:05,252
Once you've identified a risk, while we may have dozens and hundreds of different technological answers, all risk treatment comes down to four categories.

10206
17:37:06,158 --> 17:37:09,924
The first is risk mitigation, and this is by far the most common.

10207
17:37:10,286 --> 17:37:17,924
It's a control that lowers the risk, hopefully lowers the risk to the point that the residual risk is acceptable to management.

10208
17:37:18,576 --> 17:37:22,084
Now, one you might not have thought of is risk acceptance.

10209
17:37:22,576 --> 17:37:25,776
You objectively and knowingly choose not to take action.

10210
17:37:25,936 --> 17:37:28,816
Now this is not lack of action due to ignorance.

10211
17:37:29,102 --> 17:37:30,276
You weren't aware of the risk.

10212
17:37:30,496 --> 17:37:45,252
What happens is you did the risk, you looked at mitigating controls, and using formulas like the ALE and SLE formula you've recently seen, you determined that the cost of mitigation was much, much more expensive than the cost of loss.

10213
17:37:45,776 --> 17:37:53,252
Or you determined that the likelihood of a particular threat was so low as to be almost impossible.

10214
17:37:53,710 --> 17:37:59,780
Any of these criteria may lead you to simply accept the risk and not implement mitigating controls.

10215
17:38:00,238 --> 17:38:07,616
Now before you take that course of action, this absolutely must have management buy-in and should absolutely be documented thoroughly.

10216
17:38:07,776 --> 17:38:09,860
Why are you choosing to accept the risk?

10217
17:38:10,816 --> 17:38:14,148
Risk avoidance is something we prefer but rarely can do.

10218
17:38:14,446 --> 17:38:15,936
You literally evade the risk.

10219
17:38:16,016 --> 17:38:18,308
There is zero chance of risk occurring.

10220
17:38:18,496 --> 17:38:19,376
That's hard to do.

10221
17:38:19,536 --> 17:38:22,692
In most cases, the best you can do is effectively mitigate.

10222
17:38:23,694 --> 17:38:30,532
Now risk transfer or sharing, that used to mean simply you have vendors, partners, suppliers that take on part of the risk.

10223
17:38:30,894 --> 17:38:36,176
But more and more we're seeing insurance companies issue IT breach insurance.

10224
17:38:36,256 --> 17:38:37,536
So they share the risk.

10225
17:38:37,776 --> 17:38:42,116
They take on the cost of some issue should a threat occur.

10226
17:38:46,542 --> 17:38:57,536
Whereas we will look at many, many different methods throughout this course, they really come down to a few different criteria, a few different properties that all risk assessment methods have.

10227
17:38:57,696 --> 17:39:01,732
Now there are some formal methods like the scoring system method and judgmental method.

10228
17:39:02,158 --> 17:39:05,696
First, remember that a combination of methods can be used.

10229
17:39:05,856 --> 17:39:09,636
You don't have to pick a single risk assessment method and stay with it.

10230
17:39:10,702 --> 17:39:12,896
Methods can develop and change over time.

10231
17:39:13,230 --> 17:39:16,484
They get updated, there are new methods, that sort of thing.

10232
17:39:17,518 --> 17:39:21,860
Unfortunately, all methods to some degree depend on your subjective judgment.

10233
17:39:22,816 --> 17:39:29,936
Now you should always evaluate the appropriateness of any chosen risk methodology method for your environment.

10234
17:39:30,126 --> 17:39:32,656
Don't just pick one because it's the one you always use.

10235
17:39:32,942 --> 17:39:36,740
What's appropriate for this environment and these specific goals?

10236
17:39:38,094 --> 17:39:40,708
That concludes Knowledge Statement 1.2.

10237
17:39:46,830 --> 17:39:51,780
Knowledge statement 1.3 of domain one of the CISA exam.

10238
17:39:54,862 --> 17:40:05,700
Knowledge of fundamental business processes, things like purchasing, payroll, accounts payable, accounts receivable, and the role of information systems in these processes.

10239
17:40:09,966 --> 17:40:21,028
The purpose of this knowledge statement is to emphasize that before you can do a successful IS audit, you need to understand the underlying business process that's being audited.

10240
17:40:21,614 --> 17:40:24,256
As we already mentioned, is this an accounts payable system?

10241
17:40:24,416 --> 17:40:26,416
Is this a human resources system?

10242
17:40:26,862 --> 17:40:28,576
What's the purpose of the process?

10243
17:40:28,736 --> 17:40:29,776
What are its goals?

10244
17:40:29,936 --> 17:40:31,056
How does it function?

10245
17:40:31,470 --> 17:40:32,836
What are its constraints?

10246
17:40:33,616 --> 17:40:39,364
Now you need to also understand the role that information systems play in these processes.

10247
17:40:39,616 --> 17:40:44,576
Clearly, some business processes are more IS-centric than others.

10248
17:40:44,736 --> 17:40:50,244
You need to understand the specific part that IS plays in any given business process.

10249
17:40:50,830 --> 17:41:04,164
Information system auditing involves assessment of all the information system related controls, but also understanding those control objectives, which is why you need to understand the fundamental business processes.

10250
17:41:04,816 --> 17:41:13,616
It also involves identifying key controls that help achieve a well controlled environment as per particular standards.

10251
17:41:13,776 --> 17:41:21,828
Now that may also involve the knowledge of certain industry standards and laws, and we'll visit that topic in later knowledge statements.

10252
17:41:26,830 --> 17:41:32,964
Here are just some examples of fundamental business processes using transactions as an example.

10253
17:41:33,550 --> 17:41:44,388
A bank can have various transactions, mobile banking, ATM, over-the-counter, deposits, withdrawals, wire transfers, all sorts of accounts.

10254
17:41:45,390 --> 17:41:59,424
A chain store might have point of sale transactions with credit card, extranet cash transactions with suppliers using electronic data interchange, ordering from vendors, those sorts of processes.

10255
17:41:59,584 --> 17:42:05,188
Whatever the particular business you're auditing, you need to understand what it is that business does.

10256
17:42:05,710 --> 17:42:09,796
And what are the particular processes that are most critical to that business?

10257
17:42:10,414 --> 17:42:27,716
what information systems, those processes use, and then apply things that we've looked at in earlier lessons, such as risk assessment, a risk assessment, that's particular to the specific fundamental business processes for the organization that you're about to audit.

10258
17:42:29,262 --> 17:42:33,348
Now there are a few different ways of looking at and understanding a business better.

10259
17:42:34,190 --> 17:42:38,064
The following few screens will show you a few of these methodologies.

10260
17:42:38,286 --> 17:42:41,828
None of these do you have to memorize for the CISA exam.

10261
17:42:43,374 --> 17:42:46,468
And we won't cover them in any significant depth.

10262
17:42:46,798 --> 17:42:52,036
You just need to basically identify what these frameworks or processes are.

10263
17:42:56,814 --> 17:43:00,772
The Zachman framework is a common one used for defining an enterprise.

10264
17:43:01,358 --> 17:43:06,784
There are two ways to classify an enterprise that are combined together in the Zachman framework.

10265
17:43:07,054 --> 17:43:08,356
The first is very simple.

10266
17:43:08,782 --> 17:43:10,116
What is the enterprise?

10267
17:43:10,510 --> 17:43:11,424
How does it work?

10268
17:43:11,664 --> 17:43:12,708
When does it work?

10269
17:43:13,326 --> 17:43:14,532
Who's involved?

10270
17:43:14,926 --> 17:43:15,940
Where do they work?

10271
17:43:16,224 --> 17:43:18,244
Why do they do these tasks?

10272
17:43:19,182 --> 17:43:21,024
The second is more specific.

10273
17:43:21,358 --> 17:43:32,304
You identify the specific business elements in that enterprise, define them, look at their representation, specification, configuration, and instantiation.

10274
17:43:32,544 --> 17:43:36,612
Now again, it's not important that you go into more depth in the Zachman framework.

10275
17:43:36,942 --> 17:43:40,324
It's not important that you memorize it, at least not for the CISA exam.

10276
17:43:41,646 --> 17:43:58,500
The goal here is to simply make sure that you're aware that this is one framework that you could utilize in your audit practice to help you better understand a specific enterprise so you will know their business processes in enough detail to be able to effectively audit their information systems.

10277
17:44:02,064 --> 17:44:07,264
Something similar to the Zachman framework is the Sherwood Applied Business Security Architecture.

10278
17:44:07,664 --> 17:44:12,064
as you might guess, this is more specific to security in the business.

10279
17:44:12,224 --> 17:44:21,744
And it looks at all the enterprise from a risk-driven perspective, looking at the architecture as it relates to information security.

10280
17:44:21,984 --> 17:44:36,004
So this deviates a bit from Zachman in that it's not so focused on general business processes and an enterprise-wide description, but more focused on the risk associated with specific security architectures.

10281
17:44:36,304 --> 17:44:46,464
The primary characteristic of this Sherwood applied business security architecture is that everything must be derived from an analysis of the business requirements for security.

10282
17:44:46,638 --> 17:44:52,516
So you look at what that business needs for security and see if that's being met.

10283
17:44:53,344 --> 17:44:58,144
It also involves an ongoing what they call manage and measure phases of the life cycle.

10284
17:44:58,414 --> 17:45:09,572
In other words, you're continually measuring how close we are to meeting those business requirements, and we manage so that we make sure we're constantly meeting those measurements.

10285
17:45:10,318 --> 17:45:19,492
I personally think that Sherwood Applied Business Security Architecture should be used in combination with Zachman to give you a more comprehensive understanding of the business.

10286
17:45:23,950 --> 17:45:31,296
A third model you might consider devised by Michael Bell is the service oriented modeling framework, often simply called SOMF.

10287
17:45:31,616 --> 17:45:37,456
It allows you to model business and software systems to specify service orientation.

10288
17:45:37,696 --> 17:45:48,932
In other words, we look at what services the business provides, what services specific systems within the business provide, and that perspective allows us to better understand the business itself.

10289
17:45:49,422 --> 17:45:52,256
It can be used with any number of architectural approaches.

10290
17:45:52,558 --> 17:45:56,036
In other words, you can combine it with the Sherwood model we just looked at.

10291
17:45:56,576 --> 17:46:03,936
It can also be used, in addition to prepping an audit, to design any application, business environment, local or distributed.

10292
17:46:04,016 --> 17:46:21,668
In other words, if you first consider what the business does, what service it provides, and then consider what software systems are necessary to provide that service, that will drive not only audits, but the designing of applications, technologies, and systems.

10293
17:46:21,934 --> 17:46:32,656
And again, any of these particular options, service-oriented modeling framework, Sherwood or Zachman, can be used in combination with others.

10294
17:46:32,816 --> 17:46:41,056
The whole idea is not that you memorize each of these three, but that you be aware that there are tools to help you better understand the underlying business.

10295
17:46:41,216 --> 17:46:46,496
You don't simply have to approach in an ad hoc fashion trying to understand the business.

10296
17:46:46,798 --> 17:46:53,604
You can use one of these modeling tools to give yourself a deeper comprehension of the business that you're preparing to audit.

10297
17:46:54,190 --> 17:46:55,936
That concludes Knowledge Statement

10298
17:46:55,936 --> 17:47:03,972
1.3.4.

10299
17:47:08,334 --> 17:47:12,420
Knowledge of control principles related to controls and information systems.

10300
17:47:18,222 --> 17:47:31,108
What we're going to attempt to do under this knowledge statement is to ensure that you, the CISA candidate, understand the different types of controls and how they function, and you can explain how those control principles relate to information systems.

10301
17:47:36,016 --> 17:47:38,656
Primarily we're concerned with internal controls.

10302
17:47:38,976 --> 17:47:49,188
External controls would be laws and regulations that are external to a particular enterprise but do impact how they handle information security in their information systems.

10303
17:47:49,550 --> 17:47:58,628
But internal controls are the enterprise's own internal processes that have been implemented to achieve specific objectives while minimizing risk.

10304
17:47:59,216 --> 17:48:07,684
They comprise the enterprise structures, procedures, policies, and practices that have been implemented to lower the level of risk in an enterprise.

10305
17:48:07,936 --> 17:48:26,816
Now that includes everything from information technology projects such as intrusion detection system, anti-malware, to policies, to training, to procedures, everything within the enterprise that's meant to minimize risk and achieve specific objectives.

10306
17:48:26,976 --> 17:48:29,056
And they can be manual or automated.

10307
17:48:29,296 --> 17:48:38,096
Now in the information system world, we often think of automated things such as anti-malware, intrusion detection systems, and that sort of thing.

10308
17:48:38,256 --> 17:48:46,416
But manual things such as having a second party confirm a payment over a certain level before that payment is processed.

10309
17:48:46,576 --> 17:48:50,256
Well, that can be manual or automated, but it's still a control.

10310
17:48:55,118 --> 17:48:57,376
Internal controls really consider two things.

10311
17:48:57,710 --> 17:49:01,856
By implementing this control, what can be attained or what can be evaded?

10312
17:49:02,350 --> 17:49:03,296
What can be attained?

10313
17:49:03,456 --> 17:49:08,176
Let's take, for example, manual spot checks of source code.

10314
17:49:08,336 --> 17:49:14,176
Someone reviews source code before the project is compiled and distributed throughout the organization.

10315
17:49:14,336 --> 17:49:15,696
What can be attained by that?

10316
17:49:15,856 --> 17:49:18,416
Well, first and foremost, better quality of software.

10317
17:49:18,576 --> 17:49:23,524
We can look and see that all the basic software procedures were implemented correctly.

10318
17:49:24,174 --> 17:49:25,136
What can be evaded?

10319
17:49:25,422 --> 17:49:29,092
Well, at least some of the more obvious bugs will be evaded through this process.

10320
17:49:29,616 --> 17:49:32,932
Internal controls and procedures have two categories.

10321
17:49:33,262 --> 17:49:35,296
The general control procedures.

10322
17:49:35,598 --> 17:49:41,456
Previously we mentioned having a second party authorize payments over a certain level.

10323
17:49:41,616 --> 17:49:42,976
Well, that's a business control.

10324
17:49:43,056 --> 17:49:46,176
That's a general control for the entire enterprise.

10325
17:49:46,416 --> 17:49:49,892
It may or may not be implemented through technology.

10326
17:49:50,976 --> 17:49:57,776
Information system control procedures regard control procedures directly related to your information systems.

10327
17:49:58,062 --> 17:50:02,404
How do we secure databases, web servers, and that sort of thing?

10328
17:50:06,016 --> 17:50:14,016
All internal controls fall into one of three categories preventative controls, corrective controls, and detective controls.

10329
17:50:18,112 --> 17:50:20,900
Preventatives stop something before it occurs.

10330
17:50:21,392 --> 17:50:24,352
Locking an office to prevent unauthorized access.

10331
17:50:24,592 --> 17:50:26,500
Using RSA tokens.

10332
17:50:26,894 --> 17:50:30,112
Encrypting a hard drive to prevent someone from viewing files.

10333
17:50:30,382 --> 17:50:36,132
Using a virtual private network to prevent eavesdroppers from monitoring your communication.

10334
17:50:41,166 --> 17:50:47,232
Corrective controls are meant to either minimize or actually correct when a problem occurs.

10335
17:50:47,534 --> 17:50:48,992
For example, data backup.

10336
17:50:49,152 --> 17:50:53,412
You can put the data back to where it was and correct the situation.

10337
17:50:58,032 --> 17:51:02,272
Detective controls help you learn that something negative has occurred.

10338
17:51:02,432 --> 17:51:05,952
Now these can be things that are physical or information security.

10339
17:51:06,192 --> 17:51:14,672
In the information security realm, you're probably thinking of things like automated systems, intrusion detection systems, and things of that nature.

10340
17:51:14,990 --> 17:51:23,152
But something as simple as a physical access log, an audit trail, access control list to server room.

10341
17:51:23,312 --> 17:51:28,452
All of these are detective controls that help you detect what occurred and when.

10342
17:51:29,038 --> 17:51:34,660
Now it's not the case that any one of these three types of controls is more important than the other.

10343
17:51:34,992 --> 17:51:45,444
A good system has to have preventative, corrective, and detective controls, and during your audit you need to ensure that all three are in place to support the enterprise's objectives.

10344
17:51:51,152 --> 17:51:59,460
Now IS control objectives are top-level requirements that management sets for adequate control of each IT process.

10345
17:51:59,792 --> 17:52:08,752
IS control objectives are first of all a statement of the preferred purpose or result to be attained by applying controls to particular information systems.

10346
17:52:08,912 --> 17:52:20,512
In other words, by implementing this control, whether it be a physical login sheet, an anti-malware system, or whatever it might be, what is it you want to attain by doing this?

10347
17:52:20,672 --> 17:52:23,012
What do you hope will be gained?

10348
17:52:24,654 --> 17:52:30,032
IS control objectives are also procedures, policies, organizational structures, and practices.

10349
17:52:30,272 --> 17:52:40,612
Anything that's intended to reasonably assure that enterprise objectives will be achieved while undesired events are detected, corrected, or prevented.

10350
17:52:41,072 --> 17:52:43,152
So we're looking at the entire process.

10351
17:52:43,312 --> 17:52:45,348
We're looking at your company's policies.

10352
17:52:45,902 --> 17:52:48,592
We're looking at the inherent organizational structures.

10353
17:52:48,846 --> 17:52:58,308
That can include everything from something as simple as an organizational chart, who do you report a particular issue to, to something far more complex and intricate.

10354
17:52:58,606 --> 17:53:06,980
Any procedures that you might have in place that might help assure enterprise objectives, this falls under the heading of IS controls.

10355
17:53:11,118 --> 17:53:12,032
Here are some examples.

10356
17:53:12,192 --> 17:53:15,152
Now I note that these are very general.

10357
17:53:15,374 --> 17:53:16,352
They're meant to be.

10358
17:53:16,526 --> 17:53:20,592
This isn't telling you how to implement them, this is telling you the objectives.

10359
17:53:20,832 --> 17:53:25,604
For example, ensure the integrity of the system, for example, an operating system.

10360
17:53:26,272 --> 17:53:32,228
Ensure the integrity of sensitive and critical application systems, your financial data, your customer data.

10361
17:53:33,232 --> 17:53:38,180
Safeguard your assets, that includes physical assets as well as technology assets.

10362
17:53:38,912 --> 17:53:42,192
Ensure the effectiveness and efficiency of operations.

10363
17:53:42,432 --> 17:53:45,124
It's not enough that things work, they have to work well.

10364
17:53:45,792 --> 17:53:48,992
Ensure proper authentication processes for users.

10365
17:53:49,232 --> 17:53:52,260
This is a critical part of information system security.

10366
17:53:53,232 --> 17:54:00,516
Ensure the availability of any given service, and this is accomplished through disaster recovery planning and business continuity planning.

10367
17:54:00,832 --> 17:54:08,260
There are other knowledge objectives later on in this course which will cover disaster recovery and business continuity in much more detail.

10368
17:54:11,232 --> 17:54:16,740
Information system control procedures include all of the following, and let's talk briefly about each of these.

10369
17:54:17,152 --> 17:54:19,872
Strategy and direction of the IT function.

10370
17:54:20,112 --> 17:54:23,392
What are we trying to accomplish with this specific function?

10371
17:54:23,552 --> 17:54:26,628
How does it integrate with the organizational goals?

10372
17:54:27,022 --> 17:54:37,892
It sometimes occurs that enterprise goals change and technology changes, and a particular IS control or particular IT function may no longer fit with the strategy.

10373
17:54:38,832 --> 17:54:40,512
System development procedures.

10374
17:54:40,672 --> 17:54:46,352
We have lessons later on specifically on that topic, but that's one of the things you will check in an audit.

10375
17:54:46,592 --> 17:54:49,124
Are systems being developed in a proper manner?

10376
17:54:49,392 --> 17:54:56,932
And that ties in closely with do we have quality assurance processes that are appropriate and that are being adhered to?

10377
17:54:57,486 --> 17:54:59,552
What about the communications in the network?

10378
17:54:59,632 --> 17:55:01,632
Do they meet security needs?

10379
17:55:01,872 --> 17:55:05,472
Do they mesh with the enterprise objectives?

10380
17:55:05,632 --> 17:55:07,940
Do they support the goals of the business?

10381
17:55:09,070 --> 17:55:12,272
General organization and management of the IT function.

10382
17:55:12,432 --> 17:55:16,992
How is IT, this particular function we're auditing, how is it managed?

10383
17:55:17,072 --> 17:55:18,148
How is it organized?

10384
17:55:19,022 --> 17:55:23,332
Are there operation procedures in place that support enterprise objectives?

10385
17:55:24,032 --> 17:55:26,788
Are there appropriate physical access controls?

10386
17:55:27,630 --> 17:55:37,188
If a database is involved, and it usually is with an information system, is database administration done in a way that's consistent with enterprise objectives?

10387
17:55:37,472 --> 17:55:40,992
What about access to IT programs data and resources?

10388
17:55:41,152 --> 17:55:43,012
Is it controlled appropriately?

10389
17:55:43,552 --> 17:55:50,116
Are there appropriate system programming and system support departments to support the enterprise objectives?

10390
17:55:50,958 --> 17:56:00,672
Of course, do we have business continuity planning in place that's appropriate and sufficient to support this particular business process?

10391
17:56:01,232 --> 17:56:04,112
Do we have detective and protection mechanisms?

10392
17:56:04,302 --> 17:56:11,312
All of these questions need to be addressed when looking at each and every IS control during the course of your audit.

10393
17:56:16,192 --> 17:56:19,872
An audit work program represents your audit plan and strategy.

10394
17:56:20,046 --> 17:56:22,212
It has procedure, scope, and objectives.

10395
17:56:22,432 --> 17:56:30,468
It's basically a guide for documenting the various steps you take during the audit, the type and extent of evidentiary matters reviewed.

10396
17:56:30,752 --> 17:56:33,392
It gives a trail for the entire process used.

10397
17:56:33,552 --> 17:56:41,412
At some point you'll want to do quality assurance of your audit process, and only through an audit work program do you have the trail necessary to do that.

10398
17:56:41,712 --> 17:56:44,992
And it provides accountability for performance.

10399
17:56:45,232 --> 17:56:49,892
Whatever approach you use to auditing, it always comes down to four basic steps.

10400
17:56:50,414 --> 17:56:51,492
First is planning.

10401
17:56:51,822 --> 17:56:57,668
You always assess risks first and you develop your audit program in light of those risks.

10402
17:56:58,670 --> 17:57:00,832
You also have objectives and procedures.

10403
17:57:00,992 --> 17:57:04,164
Recall guidance five that we looked at in an earlier lesson.

10404
17:57:05,312 --> 17:57:09,392
Once you have your plan, you have to obtain and evaluate evidence.

10405
17:57:09,678 --> 17:57:11,152
It's all about evidence.

10406
17:57:11,342 --> 17:57:17,232
You don't have any preconceived notions about whether or not a control is meeting the objectives.

10407
17:57:17,392 --> 17:57:22,820
When you're auditing, you seek evidence to determine the strengths and weaknesses of controls.

10408
17:57:23,552 --> 17:57:27,952
Once you've done all of that, you have to prepare and present a report.

10409
17:57:28,112 --> 17:57:30,752
There's usually a draft version and a final report.

10410
17:57:31,086 --> 17:57:35,312
Just as important and covered in Guidance 35 is the follow-up.

10411
17:57:35,758 --> 17:57:39,312
Were corrective actions taken to the issues you found during the audit?

10412
17:57:39,598 --> 17:57:40,512
Were they fixed?

10413
17:57:40,846 --> 17:57:43,072
Or do those negative conditions still exist?

10414
17:57:43,374 --> 17:57:44,512
That's very important.

10415
17:57:48,832 --> 17:57:54,672
Audit methodology are the standard audit procedures that are used to attain the objectives of the audit.

10416
17:57:54,832 --> 17:58:02,512
This is a documented approach for performing the audit in a continuous and recurring manner in order to achieve the planned audit objectives.

10417
17:58:02,752 --> 17:58:09,892
Audit methodology always has a scope of the audit, the audit objectives, and the work programs we previously mentioned.

10418
17:58:10,766 --> 17:58:13,552
That concludes Knowledge Statement 1.4.

10419
17:58:20,224 --> 17:58:23,108
CISA knowledge statement one five

10420
17:58:27,310 --> 17:58:32,464
Knowledge of risk-based audit planning and audit project management techniques including follow-up.

10421
17:58:37,198 --> 17:58:43,172
What this means is we're going to look at an approach to audit that's based on the enterprise's risks.

10422
17:58:43,566 --> 17:58:53,984
As you already realize, it's usually not possible, or at least not practical, to audit every single function of every single IS that you have in the organization.

10423
17:58:54,144 --> 17:59:03,300
All of your information systems are very complex, and checking each and every control in each and every possible scenario is usually impractical.

10424
17:59:03,824 --> 17:59:08,676
Risk-based audit planning starts with identifying the key enterprises' risks.

10425
17:59:09,038 --> 17:59:12,704
What are the risks that are particularly important to this enterprise?

10426
17:59:12,944 --> 17:59:15,588
In other words, a risk analysis has been conducted.

10427
17:59:16,144 --> 17:59:19,984
Now for this to work, you have to have an understanding of the organization.

10428
17:59:20,334 --> 17:59:21,984
What sort of business are they in?

10429
17:59:22,286 --> 17:59:24,452
What sort of transactions are they conducting?

10430
17:59:25,024 --> 17:59:26,624
What is the business environment?

10431
17:59:26,784 --> 17:59:33,824
Now by environment, we're talking about the information systems environment, the technology environment, and perhaps a regulatory environment.

10432
17:59:35,054 --> 17:59:38,704
You also need to know what the business's control objectives are.

10433
17:59:38,864 --> 17:59:42,980
What are they trying to accomplish by implementing certain controls?

10434
17:59:43,694 --> 17:59:48,944
It's important to understand the type and nature of transactions that organization engages in.

10435
17:59:49,166 --> 17:59:55,940
For example, B2B wire transfers are very different than consumer-initiated credit card transactions.

10436
17:59:56,304 --> 18:00:02,404
That changes the entire scenario, what risks are present, and how one goes about auditing.

10437
18:00:03,104 --> 18:00:09,344
You also need to understand the flow of these transactions and how they're captured in information systems.

10438
18:00:09,678 --> 18:00:17,860
Remember, our focus is information system auditing, so it's not just the transactions, but how are they processed within computer systems?

10439
18:00:22,318 --> 18:00:24,944
There are four different risks we're concerned about.

10440
18:00:25,326 --> 18:00:27,076
Let's begin with inherent risk.

10441
18:00:27,598 --> 18:00:35,824
Stated formally, the probability of an error existing that might be material, assuming compensating controls do not exist.

10442
18:00:36,334 --> 18:00:41,252
This exists irrespective of an audit and is contributed to by the nature of a business.

10443
18:00:41,504 --> 18:00:47,904
Put another way, certain businesses have certain risks that are just part of how they do business.

10444
18:00:48,144 --> 18:00:55,812
If your business routinely takes in paper checks, then there's always the chance of fraudulent checks or insufficient funds.

10445
18:00:56,110 --> 18:01:04,868
If your business is engaged in e-commerce, then you must have a website, and that website is susceptible to attacks such as SQL injection.

10446
18:01:05,742 --> 18:01:06,544
Control risk.

10447
18:01:06,704 --> 18:01:15,904
Now formally that is a probability that a material error exists which will not be prevented or detected in a timely basis by the system of internal controls.

10448
18:01:16,238 --> 18:01:31,332
Put another way, you either lack the appropriate controls to detect an issue, or the controls won't detect it in time, or there is some issue, some difference between what you would like the control to do and what it actually accomplishes.

10449
18:01:32,046 --> 18:01:33,344
Then we have detection risk.

10450
18:01:33,424 --> 18:01:35,824
Now this is very important to the auditor.

10451
18:01:36,064 --> 18:01:46,276
Put formally, the probability that the information system auditor used inadequate checks and surmises that material errors are absent when in fact they are present.

10452
18:01:46,830 --> 18:01:51,620
Put much more succinctly and simply, the chance that you, the auditor, miss something.

10453
18:01:52,224 --> 18:02:03,204
This is terribly important to you as an auditor, and it's really combated by simply using all the appropriate standards, tools, and techniques that you'll learn throughout your study for the CISA exam.

10454
18:02:04,142 --> 18:02:15,568
Now, if you take all three of these, inherent risk, control risk, and detection risk, and bring together into a cumulative risk, that is described as the overall audit risk.

10455
18:02:15,822 --> 18:02:20,196
It's the summation of all the audit risk groups for each control objective.

10456
18:02:21,006 --> 18:02:22,372
That last part is critical.

10457
18:02:23,248 --> 18:02:29,968
You usually identify risks per control or per specific business activity.

10458
18:02:30,208 --> 18:02:33,328
A specific business activity has an inherent risk.

10459
18:02:33,550 --> 18:02:38,048
The specific controls for that specific activity have a control risk.

10460
18:02:38,288 --> 18:02:42,048
The overall audit risk is broken down for each control.

10461
18:02:42,638 --> 18:02:44,420
Let's use an e-commerce example.

10462
18:02:45,134 --> 18:02:47,076
Let's say you have an e-commerce website.

10463
18:02:47,662 --> 18:02:51,268
The inherent risk is web attacks such as SQL injection.

10464
18:02:51,726 --> 18:02:57,732
You've put in place a specialized application firewall that's designed to prevent those attacks.

10465
18:02:58,094 --> 18:03:04,420
The control risk is that that control may or may not adequately prevent all of those attacks.

10466
18:03:04,910 --> 18:03:12,260
The detection risk is that in the process of doing an audit, you may or may not have detected any gaps in the control.

10467
18:03:12,848 --> 18:03:27,428
Now if you put all that together, we have an overall audit risk for that specific control, being the application firewall, that's there to help prevent attacks on a very specific business process, the e-commerce transactions.

10468
18:03:32,288 --> 18:03:33,488
Gap analysis.

10469
18:03:33,568 --> 18:03:39,300
Now this term has been used in marketing and other areas to mean something a little different than what we mean here.

10470
18:03:39,726 --> 18:03:41,408
Here we really have two issues.

10471
18:03:41,678 --> 18:03:43,780
We have a product gap and a usage gap.

10472
18:03:44,174 --> 18:03:45,668
Let's start with usage gap.

10473
18:03:46,254 --> 18:03:55,008
In a usage gap issue, you have a control that if used totally properly would be an adequate control.

10474
18:03:55,374 --> 18:04:02,608
But either the control is not implemented or the control is not properly configured or is not being properly used.

10475
18:04:02,688 --> 18:04:10,208
There is some gap between the potential that control has to mitigate risk and the actual use of the control.

10476
18:04:10,768 --> 18:04:15,968
Now a product gap is when there's some issue, the product itself is missing something.

10477
18:04:16,128 --> 18:04:19,088
It's unable to fully meet your control needs.

10478
18:04:19,328 --> 18:04:27,780
That's actually fairly common, which is why most security situations require multiple controls to address specific issues.

10479
18:04:32,238 --> 18:04:36,516
Now when doing your risk-based audit, there's some definitions you need to have in mind.

10480
18:04:37,038 --> 18:04:38,596
Target of evaluation.

10481
18:04:39,008 --> 18:04:44,688
This is the particular information security deliverable, the object for which assurances are made.

10482
18:04:44,878 --> 18:04:46,244
What is it you're testing?

10483
18:04:47,088 --> 18:04:52,448
Assurance activities are the things you use to test, the methods of testing.

10484
18:04:52,608 --> 18:04:54,756
We'll discuss those at length later on.

10485
18:04:55,328 --> 18:04:56,804
The security target.

10486
18:04:57,166 --> 18:05:03,008
These are the security specifications and requirements that you use to test the target of evaluation.

10487
18:05:03,406 --> 18:05:13,860
Put another way, assurance activities check a target of evaluation to discover whether or not that target of evaluation has met the security targets.

10488
18:05:14,862 --> 18:05:20,208
A security protection profile is similar to a security target, but it's broader in scope.

10489
18:05:20,462 --> 18:05:27,568
It's not about a specific deliverable, but it's more about general security needs of a given business or group.

10490
18:05:32,270 --> 18:05:35,908
There are some risk-based audit definitions you need to be familiar with.

10491
18:05:36,128 --> 18:05:41,316
We've used some of these terms already, and I believe you probably know what they mean, but let's just make sure.

10492
18:05:41,888 --> 18:05:43,168
What is a control?

10493
18:05:43,648 --> 18:05:47,268
I think we addressed this in earlier lessons, but let's be clear again.

10494
18:05:47,918 --> 18:05:51,168
A control is anything meant to mitigate a risk.

10495
18:05:51,328 --> 18:05:53,328
Now that can be technological things.

10496
18:05:53,614 --> 18:05:57,220
We mentioned previously an application firewall, that's a control.

10497
18:05:57,870 --> 18:05:59,876
Antivirus software is a control.

10498
18:06:00,814 --> 18:06:03,088
Intrusion detection systems, that's a control.

10499
18:06:03,328 --> 18:06:05,728
But controls also include processes.

10500
18:06:05,968 --> 18:06:10,848
Any sort of educational program to train people in security, that's a control.

10501
18:06:11,168 --> 18:06:15,748
Any sort of process whereby supervisors double check something, that's a control.

10502
18:06:16,448 --> 18:06:22,688
Now an IT control objective is a statement of what you want that control to do exactly.

10503
18:06:22,990 --> 18:06:28,196
General statements like make things more secure, stop attacks, those aren't useful.

10504
18:06:28,528 --> 18:06:31,140
A control objective should be very specific.

10505
18:06:32,368 --> 18:06:35,408
Risk is the chance that something bad will happen.

10506
18:06:35,854 --> 18:06:42,368
Now we've looked at this definition in multiple different ways throughout the preceding lessons, and you'll see it again throughout the course.

10507
18:06:42,638 --> 18:06:50,404
The idea of risk is that there is a probability that something negative will occur and will have some level of damage.

10508
18:06:51,648 --> 18:06:53,636
Evidence we've not yet talked about.

10509
18:06:54,208 --> 18:06:56,612
Evidence is all about data.

10510
18:06:57,166 --> 18:07:00,368
Auditing is not an art, it is a science.

10511
18:07:00,528 --> 18:07:02,948
And like any science, it's based on evidence.

10512
18:07:03,248 --> 18:07:06,128
You collect data to make determinations.

10513
18:07:06,382 --> 18:07:13,008
At the end of the day, what an audit really comes down to is looking at the risks of an organization.

10514
18:07:13,248 --> 18:07:15,524
That's why we're talking about risk-based auditing.

10515
18:07:16,688 --> 18:07:29,380
Examining the controls that are put in place to mitigate those risks, and then gathering evidence that will determine factually whether or not those controls meet their control objectives or not.

10516
18:07:29,774 --> 18:07:31,008
It's as simple as that.

10517
18:07:31,248 --> 18:07:33,028
It all comes down to evidence.

10518
18:07:33,934 --> 18:07:38,208
IT governance is the entire process of managing your information systems.

10519
18:07:38,448 --> 18:07:44,932
Now this is very important to audit because specific issues in governance can affect security.

10520
18:07:45,488 --> 18:07:51,408
For example, what party is responsible for which aspect of security, who approves changes.

10521
18:07:51,648 --> 18:07:58,116
All of these things affect your IT security and therefore they have to be addressed in your risk-based audit.

10522
18:07:58,368 --> 18:08:01,568
That concludes knowledge statement 1.5.

10523
18:08:04,846 --> 18:08:07,568
CISA knowledge statement 1.6.

10524
18:08:09,248 --> 18:08:18,052
Knowledge of the applicable laws and regulations that affect the scope, evidence collection and preservation, and frequency of an audit.

10525
18:08:20,208 --> 18:08:29,968
Certainly that won't occur in every audit, but if you routinely conduct information systems audits, it seems a guarantee that you will eventually uncover fraud.

10526
18:08:30,254 --> 18:08:38,688
For this reason, fraud investigations or legal proceedings require the integrity of evidence be maintained throughout its life cycle.

10527
18:08:38,848 --> 18:08:41,808
This is called chain of custody and forensic evidence.

10528
18:08:42,048 --> 18:08:52,420
So for this reason, your audit needs to be conducted assuming there might be evidence of fraud or something criminal that will have to be presented in a court proceeding.

10529
18:08:53,326 --> 18:09:01,328
Put another way, you need to handle your audit as if you were gathering evidence for court and make sure you've maintained things like chain of custody.

10530
18:09:01,488 --> 18:09:04,048
Now don't be too concerned about that at this point.

10531
18:09:04,208 --> 18:09:08,868
There are later lessons and knowledge statements that will give you some general knowledge of forensics.

10532
18:09:09,678 --> 18:09:18,180
The legal requirements include laws, regulations, contractual agreements, all these things can be placed on your audit or the auditee.

10533
18:09:18,862 --> 18:09:29,408
Management and audit personnel in any organization have to be aware of these external requirements for computer system practices and controls, how your data is processed, transmitted, or stored.

10534
18:09:29,806 --> 18:09:37,124
There is a need to comply with lots of different laws and lots of different legal requirements, and that has an impact on your audit.

10535
18:09:37,614 --> 18:09:41,988
Now, what we mean by all this is every industry is affected by some laws.

10536
18:09:43,374 --> 18:09:47,088
And in this lesson, we will look at a few laws from the United States.

10537
18:09:47,248 --> 18:09:53,728
Now, depending on where you are when you take the CESA, usually the CISA exam will focus primarily on U.S.

10538
18:09:53,888 --> 18:09:57,408
laws because that's where the largest number of CISA testers are at.

10539
18:09:57,648 --> 18:10:03,728
However, they may throw in a few local laws for European Union, Canada, and other regions.

10540
18:10:03,888 --> 18:10:07,812
So make sure you take the time to familiarize yourself with your local laws.

10541
18:10:08,430 --> 18:10:09,924
But back to the actual audit.

10542
18:10:10,208 --> 18:10:14,564
No matter what organization you're auditing, there are some legal requirements.

10543
18:10:14,768 --> 18:10:20,996
There may be industry regulations that aren't laws but are really important for that particular organization.

10544
18:10:21,248 --> 18:10:30,948
There may be contractual agreements, for example, that entity has a contractual obligation with one of their clients to reach a certain level of information assurance.

10545
18:10:31,534 --> 18:10:41,764
You have to be aware of all of these requirements, legal, regulatory, and contractual, so that you can put those thoughts into your audit and make sure your audit addresses those issues.

10546
18:10:45,264 --> 18:10:49,264
There are a few essential areas that are covered under this knowledge statement.

10547
18:10:49,424 --> 18:10:50,692
Let's begin with evidence.

10548
18:10:50,944 --> 18:10:54,464
Now in previous lessons we've mentioned evidence is important.

10549
18:10:54,784 --> 18:10:57,104
Evidence is factual data.

10550
18:10:57,486 --> 18:11:01,664
Speculation really is not important in an audit.

10551
18:11:01,824 --> 18:11:10,784
We're not interested in guessing, in supposing, we need evidence that shows certain things are or are not true.

10552
18:11:11,182 --> 18:11:16,996
In respect to legal, regulatory contractual requirements, those are usually relatively clear.

10553
18:11:17,550 --> 18:11:25,796
You need evidence that the information systems you're auditing, the controls you're auditing, either do or do not meet those requirements.

10554
18:11:26,222 --> 18:11:31,844
Now, of course, that requires you to have in-depth familiarization with that particular requirement.

10555
18:11:32,784 --> 18:11:36,260
Evidence goes hand in hand with audit documentation.

10556
18:11:36,622 --> 18:11:46,084
You need to have very thorough documentation of exactly what you audited, how you audited, and what your conclusions were based on evidence.

10557
18:11:46,944 --> 18:11:51,104
To some degree, some level of continuous auditing is necessary.

10558
18:11:51,264 --> 18:11:59,024
That doesn't necessarily mean that a professional CISA auditor comes out and audits the organization continuously.

10559
18:11:59,184 --> 18:12:18,276
It may mean such a simple thing as internal spot checks, it may mean log examination, it may mean automated systems, but something to ensure that not only did the organization meet its legal, regulatory, and contractual requirements today when you did the audit, but that they're still meeting them next month.

10560
18:12:19,950 --> 18:12:22,543
Legal requirements are perhaps the most important.

10561
18:12:22,990 --> 18:12:29,504
It is not the goal of the CISA to make you an attorney, and not all the major laws will be reviewed on the CISA.

10562
18:12:29,933 --> 18:12:34,564
It's important for you to spend some time familiarizing yourself with appropriate laws.

10563
18:12:35,822 --> 18:12:42,051
And that will be based on your legal jurisdiction, your industry, and facts of that nature.

10564
18:12:42,478 --> 18:12:46,724
But you do need to be aware there are legal requirements that do impact your audit.

10565
18:12:53,038 --> 18:12:55,812
Here's a few laws that are very important in the United States.

10566
18:12:56,270 --> 18:13:01,823
HIPAA and HITECH, the Health Insurance Portability and Accountability Act of 1996.

10567
18:13:02,144 --> 18:13:11,331
Now that's a very lengthy law, but most important for auditing is it identifies what is considered personal health information and how it has to be handled.

10568
18:13:12,334 --> 18:13:24,464
This was augmented by the HITECH, or Health Information Technology for Economic and Clinical Health Act, which redefined what a breach is and gave stricter standards for notifying people in case of a breach.

10569
18:13:24,717 --> 18:13:38,020
If you're auditing not just medical clinics, not just hospitals, but medical billing companies, health insurance companies, anything of that nature, then these two laws become very, very critical.

10570
18:13:38,318 --> 18:13:43,971
And again, our goal is not to go in depth into every law, but to briefly introduce you to a few of the most important ones.

10571
18:13:53,516 --> 18:13:56,356
Sarbanes-Oxley is strictly a United States issue.

10572
18:13:56,436 --> 18:13:57,916
It does not affect other countries.

10573
18:13:58,464 --> 18:14:01,024
It is all about publicly traded companies.

10574
18:14:01,183 --> 18:14:05,060
So a privately held company does not have to adhere to Sarbane's Oxley.

10575
18:14:05,518 --> 18:14:14,467
Sarbane's Oxley was a rather complex piece of legislation and it was meant to address some financial fraud that had taken place in the early 2000s.

10576
18:14:14,784 --> 18:14:21,183
Most important for IT is that publicly traded companies must keep electronic records for five years.

10577
18:14:21,424 --> 18:14:36,224
The reason I point out this specific one is not so much that you have to memorize this for the test, but it gives us a great example of how we take a legal requirement and that gives us a very clear control objective, and it's very easy to audit.

10578
18:14:36,543 --> 18:14:45,988
If there are electronic records that don't go back five years, there needs to be an explanation or an understanding because you're legally required to keep them.

10579
18:14:47,216 --> 18:14:58,436
Now, the PCI DSS, or Payment Card Industry Data Security Standards, that's an extensive set of documents that could take an entire course in and of themselves.

10580
18:14:59,149 --> 18:15:03,376
The CISA does not ask you to be a PCI-DSS expert.

10581
18:15:03,615 --> 18:15:11,776
But basically, any organization that processes credit cards will to some level have to comply with PCI DSS.

10582
18:15:12,255 --> 18:15:14,975
Now notice these are standards in the industry.

10583
18:15:15,136 --> 18:15:16,255
It's not a law.

10584
18:15:16,416 --> 18:15:26,576
It's the Visa, MasterCard, Discover, American Express companies saying, look, if you're going to process and handle credit card data, you have to do these things.

10585
18:15:26,736 --> 18:15:29,216
And that applies in many, many countries.

10586
18:15:29,296 --> 18:15:34,656
In fact, anywhere that you're processing credit cards, PCI DSS comes into play.

10587
18:15:34,816 --> 18:15:44,868
So anytime you're auditing a company that processes credit card information, you need to be familiar with PCI DSS and incorporate that in your audit.

10588
18:15:45,136 --> 18:15:48,736
Now these are just examples of legal and regulatory requirements.

10589
18:15:48,975 --> 18:15:51,683
An exhaustive list would be humongous.

10590
18:15:51,950 --> 18:15:57,056
Depending on where you live and your industry, there can be any number of legal and regulatory requirements.

10591
18:15:57,216 --> 18:16:01,056
You don't have to memorize them all, but be generally familiar with them.

10592
18:16:07,216 --> 18:16:11,056
Here's another set of standards that the test will not ask you to memorize.

10593
18:16:11,216 --> 18:16:14,979
I'm just giving you an example of all the industry standards out there.

10594
18:16:15,598 --> 18:16:19,535
For cryptography, there are a number of ISO standards used around the world.

10595
18:16:19,776 --> 18:16:22,426
You see a brief description of each here.

10596
18:16:23,085 --> 18:16:30,255
digital signatures, how to handle cipher algorithms, hash functions, how to manage keys, data processing.

10597
18:16:30,606 --> 18:16:37,956
The point is very clearly it can become overwhelming the number of regulations, the number of standards that you need to be familiar with.

10598
18:16:40,238 --> 18:16:43,376
The good news is you don't have to be an expert in these areas.

10599
18:16:43,630 --> 18:16:47,780
CISA does not assume that you're an expert in cryptography by any means.

10600
18:16:48,335 --> 18:17:03,428
It just assumes that you know which standards to refer to when conducting an audit if cryptography, for example, is a part of the business activity and you need to refer to some standards to see if they meet control objectives.

10601
18:17:03,918 --> 18:17:07,364
And these are just here exemplary, you don't have to commit these to memory.

10602
18:17:13,285 --> 18:17:15,886
When you're doing a report, there are some basics.

10603
18:17:16,176 --> 18:17:20,656
First of all, obviously, who are you auditing, the organization?

10604
18:17:21,454 --> 18:17:23,376
Who should receive a copy of this audit?

10605
18:17:23,535 --> 18:17:24,736
And are there restrictions?

10606
18:17:24,895 --> 18:17:26,816
Should this audit not be forwarded?

10607
18:17:27,149 --> 18:17:28,816
Can it be forwarded to certain people?

10608
18:17:29,070 --> 18:17:32,016
Basically, these are demographic issues.

10609
18:17:32,301 --> 18:17:33,376
Who's being audited?

10610
18:17:33,535 --> 18:17:35,107
Who gets to know about the audit?

10611
18:17:35,856 --> 18:17:36,816
Then the scope.

10612
18:17:37,038 --> 18:17:38,255
What was the scope of your audit?

10613
18:17:38,416 --> 18:17:39,296
What did you audit?

10614
18:17:39,456 --> 18:17:43,936
Now I personally, this is not on the CISA, this is just my personal practice.

10615
18:17:44,176 --> 18:17:46,724
I also like to identify anything that was omitted.

10616
18:17:47,056 --> 18:17:51,216
If I conduct an audit and certain items were not audited, I want to point that out.

10617
18:17:51,376 --> 18:17:53,187
These were not audited, and here's why.

10618
18:17:53,856 --> 18:18:01,376
But for the test, you need to know your scope, your objectives, what were you trying to accomplish, the period of coverage.

10619
18:18:01,535 --> 18:18:08,580
I audited for a certain period of time, and that should be sufficient for six months or twelve months or however long you think.

10620
18:18:09,056 --> 18:18:10,496
The nature of the audit.

10621
18:18:10,736 --> 18:18:11,936
Was it automated?

10622
18:18:12,096 --> 18:18:13,216
Was it a team?

10623
18:18:13,696 --> 18:18:18,096
Did it involve penetration testing as well as auditing or vulnerability scanning?

10624
18:18:18,335 --> 18:18:19,268
What happened?

10625
18:18:20,462 --> 18:18:23,364
The timing and extent of the audit, how in depth was it?

10626
18:18:24,174 --> 18:18:28,335
Timing is important because auditing at different times may give different results.

10627
18:18:28,590 --> 18:18:37,539
For example, if you're auditing retail credit card transactions in a heavy holiday season, you may get different results than you would at a different time.

10628
18:18:38,496 --> 18:18:44,416
My favorite part is findings, conclusions, recommendations, follow up, reservations, or qualifications.

10629
18:18:44,576 --> 18:18:47,056
What this means is first of all, what did you find?

10630
18:18:47,342 --> 18:18:48,964
What did your audit discover?

10631
18:18:49,615 --> 18:18:51,812
Next, what do you conclude from that?

10632
18:18:53,088 --> 18:18:56,100
Probably the most important thing is what are your recommendations?

10633
18:18:56,653 --> 18:19:01,488
If a particular control you found to be inadequate, how do you recommend they fix it?

10634
18:19:01,678 --> 18:19:09,648
It's not enough for an auditor to look at an organization and say, this specific IT control does not meet control objectives.

10635
18:19:09,933 --> 18:19:12,708
You need to tell them how they can rectify that situation.

10636
18:19:13,568 --> 18:19:16,835
What if you found something did meet control objectives?

10637
18:19:17,088 --> 18:19:19,488
Do you have any reservations or qualifications?

10638
18:19:19,728 --> 18:19:31,876
For example, this might be a place to state that yes, this particular control does meet the control objectives, but that in your opinion, those control objectives may not adequately account for certain risks.

10639
18:19:33,008 --> 18:19:45,539
Now you should put this in some sort of organization, grouping them by materiality or by the intended recipient or by the business group or by the particular control, those sorts of things, some sort of organization.

10640
18:19:46,047 --> 18:19:50,403
You also have to mention any faults you found and any constructive corrections.

10641
18:19:51,407 --> 18:20:03,888
Normally your report will either be very large, starting with an executive summary, or the report will essentially tell your findings, and then there will be appendices that have the evidence to support your results.

10642
18:20:04,128 --> 18:20:10,340
Either way, there has to be some way for the audit recipient to refer to your evidence.

10643
18:20:10,687 --> 18:20:14,128
It's not appropriate for you to say because I said so.

10644
18:20:14,448 --> 18:20:16,047
You have to show the evidence.

10645
18:20:16,365 --> 18:20:20,835
Now, oftentimes executives are not going to take the time to read all your evidence.

10646
18:20:21,168 --> 18:20:27,780
Executives may simply read your conclusions, but the evidence needs to be available should they desire to look at it.

10647
18:20:28,528 --> 18:20:33,827
Of course, your overall findings, conclusion, and your opinion, and always signed and dated.

10648
18:20:39,694 --> 18:20:44,687
One tool that the CISA occasionally will bring up is the balance scorecard.

10649
18:20:45,008 --> 18:20:47,808
This is a way of looking at an organization.

10650
18:20:47,876 --> 18:20:51,808
that was originally designed as a performance management tool.

10651
18:20:51,967 --> 18:20:54,047
So it wasn't really designed for audits.

10652
18:20:54,221 --> 18:20:57,967
It was used to track execution of activities.

10653
18:20:58,288 --> 18:21:04,128
Basically, it's looking at here is an objective, how is that objective meant?

10654
18:21:04,448 --> 18:21:05,808
Sort of a scorecard.

10655
18:21:05,888 --> 18:21:06,724
That's the name.

10656
18:21:07,168 --> 18:21:13,008
Now you can use this to measure controls, their performance against an expected value.

10657
18:21:13,248 --> 18:21:15,808
And you can look at things from four perspectives.

10658
18:21:16,128 --> 18:21:17,284
Financial perspective.

10659
18:21:17,838 --> 18:21:18,928
How much did it cost?

10660
18:21:19,278 --> 18:21:20,768
Was there a return on investment?

10661
18:21:21,008 --> 18:21:22,179
Was money lost?

10662
18:21:22,797 --> 18:21:24,128
Customer perspective.

10663
18:21:24,429 --> 18:21:29,407
Sometimes security controls can be so onerous that they negatively impact customer experience.

10664
18:21:29,568 --> 18:21:32,848
Well that should be involved in your audit report.

10665
18:21:33,808 --> 18:21:35,168
Internal processes.

10666
18:21:35,501 --> 18:21:39,492
Did the controls affect internal processes in a positive or negative way?

10667
18:21:39,808 --> 18:21:41,728
And then finally, innovation or learning.

10668
18:21:41,888 --> 18:21:44,687
Have you learned something from measuring this control?

10669
18:21:45,005 --> 18:21:51,780
Now again, the balance scorecard was not originally meant for auditing, but it can be applied to the auditing situation.

10670
