18:52:46,670 --> 18:52:49,156
The customer is the beneficiary of the attack.

11020
18:52:49,774 --> 18:52:57,776
If there's an attack going on on your company and somebody wants it to happen, that customer might be somebody who's not even technical.

11021
18:52:57,936 --> 18:53:01,220
They might employ a gang of people to attack your company.

11022
18:53:02,094 --> 18:53:03,776
So the adversary is the operator.

11023
18:53:03,936 --> 18:53:07,936
That person could be an internal worker, a disgruntled, unhappy internal worker.

11024
18:53:08,096 --> 18:53:10,308
It could be a gang of outside workers.

11025
18:53:11,054 --> 18:53:20,228
And so what they're going to do is they're going to use some form of infrastructure, maybe your own company infrastructure, they're going to use their own to wage the attack.

11026
18:53:20,462 --> 18:53:26,016
Then they are going to use a capability, some tools to wage the attack.

11027
18:53:26,414 --> 18:53:28,452
And then there's a victim that would be you.

11028
18:53:28,782 --> 18:53:31,376
So that customer is the beneficiary of the attack.

11029
18:53:31,456 --> 18:53:34,500
It might be the same as the adversary, but oftentimes they're separate.

11030
18:53:34,830 --> 18:53:38,628
These are the kinds of terms that are used in the concept of threat hunting.

11031
18:53:39,246 --> 18:53:44,096
There are companies that are more mature than others in threat hunting.

11032
18:53:45,056 --> 18:53:49,456
this is something I I stole from uh I use from uh SANS.

11033
18:53:49,696 --> 18:53:52,452
They have some very good articles about what threat hunting is.

11034
18:53:52,816 --> 18:53:55,216
SANS is a security organization.

11035
18:53:55,616 --> 18:54:00,644
And they basically put together levels from zero all the way to four.

11036
18:54:01,296 --> 18:54:04,996
I would argue that most companies are at the zero or one level.

11037
18:54:05,296 --> 18:54:09,156
If you're really lucky, you might be at the HMM2 procedural level.

11038
18:54:09,806 --> 18:54:10,096
Okay?

11039
18:54:10,656 --> 18:54:17,412
Um most companies are happy if they have automated alerting, uh, and most companies really don't have much of a routine.

11040
18:54:17,550 --> 18:54:21,732
And I I would argue that more and more companies are getting up to HMM1, right?

11041
18:54:21,936 --> 18:54:26,212
Where they incorporate a little bit of threat intelligence and they have a little bit of routine.

11042
18:54:26,896 --> 18:54:29,892
the goal is to get as high as possible, but it's very costly.

11043
18:54:30,606 --> 18:54:30,896
Okay?

11044
18:54:32,590 --> 18:54:34,244
so it's the hunting maturity model.

11045
18:54:34,510 --> 18:54:48,868
And the whole idea, folks, is that you basically move To create an architecture and move from a passive defense to an active defense, and the ability to gain intelligence and take on an offensive capability against the hackers.

11046
18:54:49,536 --> 18:54:52,256
There are various types of threat hunting out there, folks.

11047
18:54:52,416 --> 18:55:04,964
User-centered, which is where you look at the job responsibility of people, then you see, well, the more important the responsibility, the more likely that there's an attack, or the more likely that a successful attack could bring the company down.

11048
18:55:05,230 --> 18:55:11,300
So you focus on problem users or problem processes, focused on users, and then you try to solve the problem.

11049
18:55:11,536 --> 18:55:14,052
The other way to do it is you focus on capability.

11050
18:55:14,574 --> 18:55:16,176
What's the business function of a system?

11051
18:55:16,336 --> 18:55:18,496
What's the business use and then you try to solve it?

11052
18:55:18,670 --> 18:55:21,348
There's infrastructure where you focus on the equipment.

11053
18:55:21,696 --> 18:55:24,420
I find capability is very useful as well as user.

11054
18:55:25,136 --> 18:55:35,856
Uh and there are various reasons why you would approach any one of these three, user capability or infrastructure, but I find that the user-centered is the most useful at many times.

11055
18:55:35,950 --> 18:55:38,980
I'm running out of time, so I'm gonna move on here a little bit.

11056
18:55:39,438 --> 18:55:40,656
What are the hackers after?

11057
18:55:40,816 --> 18:55:42,020
Well, it depends on your company.

11058
18:55:42,736 --> 18:55:43,056
Right?

11059
18:55:43,216 --> 18:55:50,016
If your company is focusing on a certain thing of value, they're gonna go after that, or at least the processes that support that thing of value.

11060
18:55:50,350 --> 18:55:57,092
Oftentimes they're after personally identifiable information, so they can sell it on the dark web, or they can further manipulate the company.

11061
18:55:57,536 --> 18:55:58,656
Accounts information.

11062
18:55:58,958 --> 18:56:09,540
They want to control SCADA systems to either wreak havoc or to engage in ransomware to make your company pay a lot of money to get their systems back, or they're after a company's reputation.

11063
18:56:10,030 --> 18:56:22,564
They collect information, a good threat hunter collects information from multiple resources In terms of what I was talking to, one person who says, Well, there's so much information, it's like a sinkhole, you just kind of get lost in it.

11064
18:56:22,830 --> 18:56:29,060
You turn that sinkhole of information into actually very useful, actionable data.

11065
18:56:30,830 --> 18:56:34,212
So you manage risk through deep packet inspection.

11066
18:56:36,176 --> 18:56:47,428
that means you get into the packet and then you arc it you investigate architectures, you gather intelligence, and you view behavior analytics and vulnerability data and look for indicators of compromise.

11067
18:56:47,536 --> 18:56:51,856
And then you go into this loop here, it's a meaningful loop, it's not an infinite loop.

11068
18:56:53,076 --> 18:56:57,540
So it's a loop that basically allows you to engage in proper threat hunting.

11069
18:56:57,936 --> 18:57:02,496
So the software that you might use helps you look at trends.

11070
18:57:02,638 --> 18:57:10,020
It looks inside of IP sessions, but it does more than just show you a bunch of data, it then turns that data into trends and information.

11071
18:57:10,734 --> 18:57:12,324
So it gives you a dashboard of the network.

11072
18:57:12,782 --> 18:57:19,620
It reflects the heartbeat of the network and you find out who is talking to whom and where and it creates context.

