22:55:32,782 --> 22:55:34,820
What is black box and white box testing?

13263
22:55:35,118 --> 22:55:41,380
So when you are testing a software or you're testing your infrastructure, there are two different tests that you can conduct.

13264
22:55:41,744 --> 22:55:45,220
The first one is a black box, the second one is a white box.

13265
22:55:45,486 --> 22:55:49,744
In a black box test, there is no knowledge that is shared with the tester.

13266
22:55:49,824 --> 22:55:58,596
So let's say you're an ethical hacker and you have been awarded a contract by an organization to test their current application that they have developed.

13267
22:55:58,944 --> 22:56:03,264
Now they are not going to give you any information, they are not going to tell you what the application is.

13268
22:56:03,344 --> 22:56:13,104
They just probably give you an IP address and a port number where the application is hosted, and now you have to fire in your own queries and try to figure out what the application is, try to gather.

13269
22:56:13,104 --> 22:56:17,024
information, see what, uh, what information can be gathered in the first place?

13270
22:56:17,134 --> 22:56:26,148
and based on that, you're going to figure out your way, identify vulnerabilities, and see if any of those vulnerabilities can lead to a security incident.

13271
22:56:26,384 --> 22:56:33,988
So without any knowledge, zero knowledge of the IT infrastructure or the source code, that's a black box attack or a black box test.

13272
22:56:34,510 --> 22:56:40,100
A white box test, on the other hand, is where full knowledge of the IT infrastructure or the source code is shared.

13273
22:56:40,686 --> 22:56:49,636
So the ethical hacker has complete knowledge, and based on the knowledge, they are then going to test out the system to see if there are any flaws that they can identify.

13274
22:56:49,838 --> 22:56:50,144
Right?

13275
22:56:50,384 --> 22:56:53,424
So, why would these two audits be important?

13276
22:56:54,158 --> 22:57:04,544
Because the first one, a black box audit, emulates the attack of an outsider, an external hacker sitting outside the organization trying to figure their way in.

13277
22:57:04,846 --> 22:57:17,984
Whereas a white box attack can emulate the attack of an insider, so a disgruntled employee within that organization misusing their access controls or their access rights to make unvalidated profits, right?

13278
22:57:18,446 --> 22:57:30,304
So somebody is corrupt, who has been bribed, who wants to sell out company secrets based, uh so they're going to try to find out vulnerabilities, try to steal data, and try to sell it on the uh gray market, right?

13279
22:57:30,384 --> 22:57:35,972
So a white box would emulate uh internal attack, a black box would emulate an external attack.

13280
22:57:36,238 --> 22:57:39,396
Moving on to question 17 what is use case testing?

13281
22:57:40,078 --> 22:57:45,316
Now, use case testing is a functional test and it is also a black box test.

13282
22:57:45,904 --> 22:57:46,224
Right?

13283
22:57:46,478 --> 22:57:47,716
What is a functional test?

13284
22:57:47,982 --> 22:57:51,664
It tests the functionality of a particular software once it has been created.

13285
22:57:52,846 --> 22:57:54,144
Why is it a black box test?

13286
22:57:54,304 --> 22:58:03,104
Because the user doesn't know what the functionality is, they just want to find out each and every scenario and try to see what that scenario generates as a response.

13287
22:58:03,264 --> 22:58:07,748
They are not sure whether that is the appropriate response that should be generated or not.

13288
22:58:08,622 --> 22:58:14,340
They're just trying to find out the response that is going to be generated after they fire off a query.

13289
22:58:14,624 --> 22:58:20,580
So this technique is used by testers to get the test scenarios to exercise the whole system from start to finish.

13290
22:58:21,166 --> 22:58:23,876
So let's say it's a login mechanism for an application.

13291
22:58:24,216 --> 22:58:24,336
right?

13292
22:58:24,704 --> 22:58:31,344
Now, a user at this point in time, the tester, since it's a black box testing, will know that it is a login mechanism.

13293
22:58:31,504 --> 22:58:35,024
They will not know the details of what login mechanisms are being utilized.

13294
22:58:35,184 --> 22:58:51,024
So they wouldn't know whether input validation is done, they wouldn't know whether output encoding is done, they wouldn't know how the CGI calls are being made, they will not know how the queries are handled at the server side and how the database is going to treat that particular query.

13295
22:58:51,246 --> 22:58:54,852
So they have no idea whether SQL injection attacks are possible and so on so forth.

13296
22:58:55,214 --> 22:59:02,944
So for them, with whatever input they are going to try to insert for that login mechanism, that's a functional black box test.

13297
22:59:03,182 --> 22:59:19,524
The functionality being whether the login mechanism works and based on the type of inputs that are going to put in, whether it creates an unwarranted output, whether they can bypass the mechanism or they can hack into the system because of some of the flaws that were left behind.

13298
22:59:20,270 --> 22:59:20,544
Right?

13299
