In the next video, we'll be looking at operating systems and applications.

13375
23:07:55,374 --> 23:07:57,344
The first question is on virtual memory.

13376
23:07:57,424 --> 23:07:58,980
What exactly is virtual memory?

13377
23:08:00,142 --> 23:08:02,144
For a computer, we have two types of memory.

13378
23:08:02,624 --> 23:08:07,780
first is the primary memory, which is your random access memory, which is also known as a volatile memory.

13379
23:08:08,142 --> 23:08:11,972
And the secondary memory is your hard disk where your data is stored permanently.

13380
23:08:12,464 --> 23:08:24,452
But for a computer, when it has let's say a 4GB memory or a RAM, as in this scenario on your screen, it is going to replicate that and is going to create another 4GB of virtual memory on the hard disk.

13381
23:08:24,784 --> 23:08:27,908
And it is going to use it in tandem along with the RAM.

13382
23:08:29,070 --> 23:08:40,228
So if the RAM is insufficient, the processor is going to utilize the 4GB of the virtual memory that is created on the hard disk and it is going to swap data from the RAM to the hard disk.

13383
23:08:41,070 --> 23:08:44,228
This can also be known as a page file or a swap file.

13384
23:08:44,622 --> 23:08:47,664
The next question is what are different scheduling algorithms?

13385
23:08:47,984 --> 23:08:58,464
Now the context for this question is you're talking about a processor and you are talking about how processes are going to be fed to the processor and how the processor is going to treat these processes.

13386
23:08:58,734 --> 23:09:01,344
So the first is first come first serve.

13387
23:09:01,424 --> 23:09:05,984
So the process which requests the CPU first gets the CPU allocation first.

13388
23:09:06,254 --> 23:09:11,716
Now, whenever there are processes that are being run by different applications, they make requests for some CPU time.

13389
23:09:12,144 --> 23:09:21,344
Now in first come first serve, the first service or the first process that is going to request some processing time will get that much allocated to them.

13390
23:09:21,582 --> 23:09:25,508
They will run through the process first and then the next and the next and so on so forth.

13391
23:09:26,704 --> 23:09:28,996
The second one is the shortest job first.

13392
23:09:29,344 --> 23:09:37,732
This is the process where the shortest execution time for that process is calculated, and that process is selected first for the CPU.

13393
23:09:38,862 --> 23:09:40,612
Then there is priority scheduling.

13394
23:09:40,910 --> 23:09:43,908
This scheduler selects the tasks to work as per priority.

13395
23:09:44,238 --> 23:09:49,264
So there would be some tasks that are marked with high priority, some would be normal, and some would be low.

13396
23:09:49,424 --> 23:09:55,184
So based on this high, normal or low priority, all the processes will be classified.

13397
23:09:55,471 --> 23:10:00,804
higher priority will be dealt with first, then the normal and then the least priority.

13398
23:10:02,542 --> 23:10:08,452
The fourth option is multiple level queues, where processes are assigned to a queue based on the specific property.

13399
23:10:08,864 --> 23:10:11,904
like process priority, the size of memory, etc.

13400
23:10:12,064 --> 23:10:12,224
So.

13401
23:10:12,304 --> 23:10:26,020
it will be classified based on the attributes given to that particular process, and multiple queues will be created, and then based on the attributes, the processes will be processed by the CPU.

13402
23:10:27,502 --> 23:10:29,184
Then shortest remaining time.

13403
23:10:29,264 --> 23:10:32,676
The process will be allocated to the task which is closest to its completion.

13404
23:10:33,184 --> 23:10:41,476
So you look at it this way the process that will take the least time to complete its processing would be chosen first.

13405
23:10:42,894 --> 23:10:47,984
And then the round robin method where each process comes in turn gets an equal share of time.

13406
23:10:48,302 --> 23:11:01,956
So if there are 10 processes, each process will be allocated a certain amount of time, after which the next process will be processed, and so on so forth, and it will continue in a round robin fashion till all the processes get executed.

13407
23:11:04,014 --> 23:11:11,748
So, in short, six different scheduling algorithms depending on how you uh how the operating system deals with it.

