The next question is What are the steps involved in hacking a server or a network?

13409
23:11:19,440 --> 23:11:21,360
So, this is more of an ethical hacking question.

13410
23:11:21,582 --> 23:11:32,480
You're looking at devices, and for uh and the interviewer asks you what kind of steps are involved, what are the activities that you would do in hacking a server or a network?

13411
23:11:32,720 --> 23:11:44,480
Now, there are no specific steps that you would define because every hack is going to be unique, but it has a hack can be classified in five different steps, which are quite generic, right?

13412
23:11:44,782 --> 23:11:55,492
So the first step will always always be the recognizance step, also known as information gathering phase, also known as footprinting or fingerprinting, depending on what exactly you're doing.

13413
23:11:55,760 --> 23:12:04,868
But in this phase, the attacker gathers all the evidence, all the information that is possible about the targets that they want to attack.

13414
23:12:05,360 --> 23:12:10,320
So here you're trying to get to know the victim so you can launch specific attacks towards them.

13415
23:12:10,670 --> 23:12:19,040
You want to identify what operating system, they are utilizing, what IP addresses, MAC, addresses, the versions of the operating systems, and applications, the patch levels.

13416
23:12:19,246 --> 23:12:27,520
find out vulnerabilities, find out whatever information is possible, find out the information about the person who is using those computers.

13417
23:12:27,694 --> 23:12:31,040
so you can launch social engineering attacks and so on and so forth.

13418
23:12:31,200 --> 23:12:37,796
So the first step is all about gathering enough information based on which you can launch further attacks.

13419
23:12:38,286 --> 23:12:42,052
Once you have that information comes the second phase, which is known as the scanning phase.

13420
23:12:42,560 --> 23:12:44,480
This is more of a technical phase.

13421
23:12:44,590 --> 23:12:53,200
So you have uh in the first step, you have got your IP addresses, domain names, maybe even network maps, and you have identified which devices are available.

13422
23:12:53,280 --> 23:13:02,400
Now, in the scanning phase, you're going to identify live devices and then you're going to scan them for open ports, processes, protocols, services.

13423
23:13:02,638 --> 23:13:08,068
You're going to identify vulnerabilities, you're going to enumerate them to identify more information from them.

13424
23:13:08,814 --> 23:13:17,924
Thus, at this point in time, you will have identified a certain set of vulnerabilities or a certain set of security loopholes that you can misuse.

13425
23:13:18,606 --> 23:13:24,228
Once you have identified those, you are going to the next step, which is the gaining access tape.

13426
23:13:24,720 --> 23:13:39,652
In this, you are actually going to execute your attacks based on the vulnerabilities that you have found, and you are either going to gain access to that particular system by installing a Trojan or destroy the system by installing a virus or install a spyware or a keylogger, whatever you wanted to achieve.

13427
23:13:40,046 --> 23:13:50,116
So in the gaining access phase, you would have based on the knowledge that you have gained in the first and the second phase, you're going to launch your attacks and you're going to try to gain access to that particular device.

13428
23:13:51,566 --> 23:13:53,920
Then the next step is where you're going to maintain that access.

13429
23:13:54,000 --> 23:14:00,420
Now that you have hacked into that device, it is not necessary that you will always be able to get access to that device.

13430
23:14:00,960 --> 23:14:07,760
Suppose you have cracked the password of that particular user and the user changes that password after a few days, your attack is worthless.

13431
23:14:07,840 --> 23:14:10,788
So what you're going to do here is you're going to maintain your access.

13432
23:14:11,438 --> 23:14:26,692
So this is where it is assumed that you want repeated access to that device, and thus you're going to install a keylogger or a Trojan or some mechanism which will still allow you to get access to that device without the knowledge or the authorization of that particular user.

13433
23:14:26,990 --> 23:14:30,532
And finally, the last step is where you're going to cover your tracks.

13434
23:14:30,926 --> 23:14:44,580
So whatever activity that you have done so far will have created logs and will have created information based on which the victim will come to know that they have been compromised and may be able to trace that activity back to you.

13435
23:14:45,040 --> 23:14:58,800
So to prevent the user or the victim from realizing that they have been hacked and to prevent them to discover who has hacked them, you want to cover your tracks by deleting logs and any references that point to that particular activity.

13436
23:14:59,054 --> 23:15:00,800
You're going to hide the files that you have created.

13437
23:15:00,880 --> 23:15:05,280
So you have installed a Trojan or a Keylogger, these will create files and directories.

13438
23:15:05,360 --> 23:15:07,840
You're going to hide them so that they are not discovered.

13439
23:15:08,142 --> 23:15:10,160
You're going to hide processes that have been created.

13440
23:15:10,240 --> 23:15:20,644
You're going to try to hide all the activity that you have done so that to conceal the actual attack and preventing the user from realizing that they have been compromised.

13441
23:15:21,454 --> 23:15:28,516
So these are the five steps that will be involved in hacking a server network application or any computing device you'll come across.

