What are batched operating systems?

13497
23:22:19,120 --> 23:22:23,204
The computer operator places the jobs coming from input devices into batches.

13498
23:22:23,760 --> 23:22:38,788
So consider this not from a desktop perspective but from a server perspective where these devices are used by organizations to compute and to crunch some processes that is going to make some business sense out of it.

13499
23:22:39,246 --> 23:22:52,640
So when there are multiple processes coming in, multiple jobs that are going to be scheduled, a batched OS is going to place these jobs in batches and they're going to crunch those based on the inputs that have been given by these operators.

13500
23:22:53,006 --> 23:22:59,364
Distributed OS is where there are multiple computers which are interconnected and are communicating through networks.

13501
23:22:59,680 --> 23:23:04,000
So in a corporate environment, you don't use one single computer to do everything.

13502
23:23:04,080 --> 23:23:12,004
You've got a data center, and the data center will have a cluster of servers where they are going to share some resources to crunch one particular task.

13503
23:23:12,430 --> 23:23:12,640
Right?

13504
23:23:12,942 --> 23:23:15,748
So that's where your distributed OS come into the picture.

13505
23:23:16,622 --> 23:23:19,600
Then you have time sharing OSS where you are renting some time.

13506
23:23:19,760 --> 23:23:22,480
So time-sharing OSS minimizes the response time.

13507
23:23:22,640 --> 23:23:25,520
Example in today's world, cloud, right?

13508
23:23:25,920 --> 23:23:42,660
You go onto the cloud, you have a virtual service over there, you schedule something, you schedule a job over there, it is uh it is executed, and for that time being, that operating system services your particular request and provides you that particular job.

13509
23:23:43,694 --> 23:23:52,804
Any application that you see online that is executed, for example, Facebook from a consumer's perspective, could be a time-shared experience.

13510
23:23:53,966 --> 23:23:59,360
Then multi-programmed OS, the operating system uses CPU scheduling to separate jobs.

13511
23:23:59,662 --> 23:24:13,760
So, you are scheduling the CPU to complete certain jobs in this particular manner, and in real-time OS, the operating system gives maximum time to critical operations, so it identifies the priority of these operations.

13512
23:24:13,840 --> 23:24:22,756
It knows the high priority items, the medium, low priority items, and based on that, it is going to execute these critical operations and get the job done.

13513
23:24:23,182 --> 23:24:27,876
Moving on to the next question: what is the difference between logical address space and physical address space?

13514
23:24:28,240 --> 23:24:33,060
Now, when you're looking at address spaces, this is where applications come into the picture.

13515
23:24:33,440 --> 23:24:48,420
and when you execute an application, it is going to create a particular address in the memory where it is going to create a buffer to store its own information, so that it can be provided to the processor, processed, and then can be returned back to the application as an output.

13516
23:24:48,782 --> 23:24:49,111
right.

13517
23:24:49,391 --> 23:24:57,311
So, as far as definitions are concerned, logical address is generated during running of an application or a program.

13518
23:24:57,742 --> 23:25:04,068
A physical address is a physical address or a physical location on the memory module itself.

13519
23:25:05,102 --> 23:25:11,184
Right, visibility you can view a logical address because it is programmed into a computer.

13520
23:25:11,264 --> 23:25:25,764
So if I'm looking at C C and I'm using malloc or memory allocation, that's where the logical address is going to be created, where a buffer is going to be created for that program, and whatever the user input is going to be, it's going to be stored in that buffer.

13521
23:25:26,094 --> 23:25:37,504
But whereas physical address is concerned, this logical address will be created on a physical store or a physical memory module, which will have its own addressing mechanism.

13522
23:25:37,744 --> 23:25:43,780
Thus, you you can see the memory module, but you cannot see the specific address on that particular memory module.

13523
23:25:44,334 --> 23:25:58,624
But as far as a logical address is concerned, while you're programming or you're debugging the application, it will show you the logical address that has been created, the start point and the end point of the logical address that has been created for that particular program.

13524
23:25:58,830 --> 23:26:01,348
It can be shown in a debugging environment.

13525
23:26:02,382 --> 23:26:02,944
Right?

13526
23:26:03,630 --> 23:26:08,144
Address space logical and physical address is physical, like I discussed.

13527
23:26:08,238 --> 23:26:09,664
It's the memory module itself.

13528
23:26:09,838 --> 23:26:17,504
You can access only the physical address on that particular memory because logical addresses can be viewed, but you cannot access them physically.

13529
23:26:17,904 --> 23:26:30,500
Generation, the logical addresses are generated by the CPU during the processing time, whereas physical addresses are generated or computed by the memory management unit or the MMU that you have on your computers.

13530
23:26:31,342 --> 23:26:37,764
And as far as logical addresses, they will always be they are variable, whereas the physical address is always going to be constant.

13531
23:26:38,158 --> 23:26:42,944
Looking at the next question, what is the difference between logical address space and physical address space?

13532
23:26:43,024 --> 23:26:56,420
So moving on from the previous question to this, the logical address is an address created by the CPU for the processes that need to be addressed and that need to be stored as a buffer in the physical memory.

13533
23:26:56,686 --> 23:27:06,596
Whereas the physical memory itself is going to be an address that is going to be there on the physical part of that memory, which is going to be assigned to it by the MMU.

