Interview questions based on cyber attacks.

13568
23:30:11,648 --> 23:30:13,088
Let's start off with the first one.

13569
23:30:13,248 --> 23:30:16,048
The first question is what is SQL injection?

13570
23:30:16,288 --> 23:30:24,688
SQL stands for structured query language, which is a language that is used by most of your databases or your relational databases.

13571
23:30:25,486 --> 23:30:31,071
The variations of your database would be MySQL, Microsoft SQL, Oracle SQL.

13572
23:30:32,451 --> 23:30:33,891
You'll have IBM databases.

13573
23:30:34,131 --> 23:30:39,391
All of these databases utilize the structured query languages to interact with the applications.

13574
23:30:39,822 --> 23:30:50,436
Now all of these databases have their own syntax, so you'll have to study most of these databases based on which applications and which databases you want to provide security for.

13575
23:30:51,088 --> 23:31:11,172
But as the name suggests, SQL injection vulnerability, or a structured query language injection vulnerability, is where a user can maliciously inject a SQL input or a SQL statement in a query and send it to the database and evoke a response out of it.

13576
23:31:11,808 --> 23:31:20,292
So this vulnerability is not specifically to the database, the vulnerability lies more in the application and the coding of that application.

13577
23:31:20,622 --> 23:31:32,128
So when the application receives a query which it needs to be forwarded to the SQL database, we need to configure at the application level of what queries are allowed and what queries are not allowed.

13578
23:31:32,208 --> 23:31:55,460
So, there are different various aspects of how to manage a SQL injection vulnerability, but the basic flaw lies in the application where invalidated input is accepted and sent forward to the database, where the database might confuse it into an executable statement and thus create a response that was not warranted.

13579
23:31:55,888 --> 23:31:59,328
There are various types of SQL injections as shown on the screen.

13580
23:31:59,488 --> 23:32:11,524
In band SQL injection, where you can look at an error-based or a union-based injection, a blind SQL injection where it is either Boolean-based or a time-based attack, and then an out-of-bound SQL injection.

13581
23:32:11,950 --> 23:32:21,444
Essentially, you're looking at databases and you're looking at application security where you want to encourage secure coding practices so in unvalidated input is mitigated.

13582
23:32:23,168 --> 23:32:25,540
The next question is what is spoofing?

13583
23:32:25,968 --> 23:32:30,448
Now, in spoofing, you're basically assuming the identity of another person.

13584
23:32:30,798 --> 23:32:37,648
So here the attacker pretends to be some other person or an organization and sends you an email that appears to be a legitimate email.

13585
23:32:37,808 --> 23:32:47,908
It looks almost genuine, it has been constructed to replicate what a genuine email would have been, and it is very difficult to spot a fake one.

13586
23:32:48,462 --> 23:32:54,404
There are different ways to identify whether an email is genuine or not, but that's for a different video.

13587
23:32:54,862 --> 23:33:01,248
Moving on to the next question: what is a distributed denial of service attack or a DDoS attack?

13588
23:33:01,422 --> 23:33:11,248
Now, generally, a denial of service attack is an attack where legitimate users are prevented access to the resources that they legitimately can access, right?

13589
23:33:11,502 --> 23:33:24,260
So, for example, if it is a bandwidth-based attack, the attacker consumes the bandwidth of the network in such a way that there is no more bandwidth left for legitimate users to access the network.

13590
23:33:24,846 --> 23:33:32,420
Now, a single device may not be able to generate that much amount of traffic to consume the bandwidth of a huge server.

13591
23:33:32,688 --> 23:33:42,500
Thus, the attacker will construct a botnet, and through that botnet, they will launch a distributed denial of service attack to the target victim.

13592
23:33:43,408 --> 23:33:47,808
So a botnet there are two terms that we want to look at over here.

13593
23:33:47,888 --> 23:33:51,968
The first term is a bot, and the second one being the botnet itself.

13594
23:33:52,238 --> 23:34:04,196
Bot is a software that once installed on a victim's machine, allows the hacker to send remote commands to that machine that will make it do uh generate some activity.

13595
23:34:05,072 --> 23:34:13,952
Once we have enough machines on which such bots have been implemented, the collection of these machines would be known as a botnet.

13596
23:34:14,352 --> 23:34:30,912
So an attacker would then instruct this entire botnet to start generating data traffic to be target to be sent to the targeted network or to the targeted server, which will then bog down the server, thus crashing it and preventing users from accessing that particular resource.

13597
23:34:31,374 --> 23:34:35,552
The next question is how to avoid ARP poisoning or ARP.

13598
23:34:35,790 --> 23:34:38,112
Now, first let's understand what ARP is.

13599
23:34:38,446 --> 23:34:44,516
ARP stands for address resolution protocol, which is a protocol used by computers to communicate over the network.

13600
23:34:44,912 --> 23:34:50,992
Once your computer boots up, it starts a discovery process of identifying its neighbors.

13601
23:34:51,214 --> 23:35:03,620
So if I'm in a particular subnet, my machine will proactively send out ARP requests and address resolution protocol to find out which other machines are within the same network and which are live.

13602
23:35:03,792 --> 23:35:10,596
Once it sends out a query, a live machine will respond to that query along with its MAC address.

13603
23:35:12,078 --> 23:35:19,941
This information is then stored in what is known as an ARP table or an ARP table on the machine's cache.

13604
23:35:20,750 --> 23:35:29,472
So whenever my machine now wants to send out a packet to this particular machine, it will go to the ARP table, it will identify the IP address and the associated MAC address.

13605
23:35:29,552 --> 23:35:38,112
It will print that onto the data packet as a destination IP and destination MAC and send that packet across to the switch.

13606
23:35:38,192 --> 23:35:45,188
The switch will then identify the MAC address and send the packet to the relevant machine that is connected to that particular switch.

13607
23:35:45,712 --> 23:35:52,832
Now to confuse the switch into sending it to a different machine, the R poisoning attack is created.

13608
23:35:53,152 --> 23:35:58,980
This attack is generally launched to create a man in the middle attack.

13609
23:35:59,312 --> 23:36:05,952
Now to prevent this R poisoning from happening in the first place, there are three different aspects that we can utilize.

13610
23:36:06,158 --> 23:36:12,752
First, we can use packet filtering, which will filter out and block packets that are the same source address data.

13611
23:36:12,832 --> 23:36:17,552
So you have identified some malicious IP addresses and you want to block out some IP addresses.

13612
23:36:17,632 --> 23:36:27,472
So you're using a packet filter firewall where you have constructed the firewall to filter out certain packets originating from particular range of IP addresses.

13613
23:36:27,662 --> 23:36:32,228
This firewall and this technique will then block those kind of packets coming in.

13614
23:36:33,072 --> 23:36:36,100
Second, keeping away from trust relationships.

13615
23:36:36,366 --> 23:36:44,192
Organizations will develop protocols that do not depend on trust relationships and thus you want to keep this protocol away from that.

13616
23:36:44,352 --> 23:36:52,432
Once you have created a trust relationship, these machines should not be sending out ARP requests to other machines in the first place.

13617
23:36:52,592 --> 23:37:00,612
Since the trust relationship has been defined and these machines know whom to communicate with, such kind of protocols should then be disabled.

13618
23:37:01,294 --> 23:37:03,472
Or you can use an ARP spoofing software.

13619
23:37:03,552 --> 23:37:10,592
So there are some softwares out there that will look for ARP spoofing and prevent that from happening in the first place.

13620
23:37:10,672 --> 23:37:18,832
So if somebody has sent out a spoofed ARP packet, that packet will be picked up by this software and it will be mitigated.

13621
23:37:19,886 --> 23:37:31,072
Network visualizers like glasswire, antiviruses like Sophos, they have inbuilt capabilities of identifying ARP spoofing attacks and mitigate them in the first place.

