13716
23:48:04,846 --> 23:48:09,028
The first question: define cryptography, encryption, and decryption.

13717
23:48:09,392 --> 23:48:19,716
Now, cryptography is used by security professionals to scramble data into non-readable format, which is used in securing that information.

13718
23:48:21,038 --> 23:48:27,812
So it involves converting data from a readable format into a non-readable format and then reversing it back to readable format again.

13719
23:48:28,462 --> 23:48:34,832
For example, the word computer is now scrambled into looking like an unreadable format.

13720
23:48:34,912 --> 23:48:44,260
Now, if you look at this word that it has been scrambled into, it would be very difficult for a human to figure out what the actual word was.

13721
23:48:44,622 --> 23:48:54,592
Now in this scenario, we have taken an algorithm where we have made a shift of the alphabet where we have added two alphabets to the current one.

13722
23:48:54,672 --> 23:49:01,632
So C plus 2 becomes E, O plus 2 becomes Q, M plus 2 becomes O.

13723
23:49:01,902 --> 23:49:03,780
So we have done a shift of 2.

13724
23:49:04,142 --> 23:49:09,872
And thus the key over here for this algorithm is the alphabet plus 2.

13725
23:49:09,952 --> 23:49:16,132
So any person who figures that out will be able to unscramble this and convert this back into readable text.

13726
23:49:16,672 --> 23:49:25,732
The fact of scrambling a readable text data into something that is unreadable by using a particular key is what cryptography is all about.

13727
23:49:26,766 --> 23:49:34,272
Now as we discussed the decryption again is replacing the alphabet and taking it further back by two characters.

13728
23:49:34,510 --> 23:49:41,472
So E minus 2 becomes C, Q minus 2 becomes O, O minus 2 becomes M, and so on so forth.

13729
23:49:41,552 --> 23:49:49,632
So anybody who knows this key, the shift key, anybody will able to decrypt this particular character.

13730
23:49:49,792 --> 23:49:51,392
So this depends on the user.

13731
23:49:51,472 --> 23:50:00,964
If I want to utilize alphabet plus five, then the spacing, the shifting of that character will be the fifth character from that particular character, and so on so forth.

13732
23:50:02,766 --> 23:50:06,436
The next question: what is the difference between ciphertext and clear text?

13733
23:50:07,118 --> 23:50:10,521
Ciphertext refers to the text which is encrypted and totally undesirable.

13734
23:50:12,718 --> 23:50:15,392
The message received after decryption is known as clear text.

13735
23:50:15,598 --> 23:50:17,072
This text is comprehensible.

13736
23:50:17,312 --> 23:50:20,032
So the word computer is clear text.

13737
23:50:20,112 --> 23:50:23,312
That means that it has not been treated to any cryptographic measures.

13738
23:50:23,472 --> 23:50:25,636
It does what it is intended to be.

13739
23:50:26,094 --> 23:50:34,180
However, if the moment we encrypt it, that means we scramble it into unreadable text by using any of the algorithms that we'll be looking at.

13740
23:50:34,990 --> 23:50:36,932
That text is known as a ciphertext.

13741
23:50:37,646 --> 23:50:40,324
And without the key, this becomes unreadable.

13742
23:50:41,198 --> 23:50:45,472
The clear text, as discussed, is the plain word that we have utilized.

13743
23:50:45,552 --> 23:50:50,084
We are using the English language in this instance, so the plain word computer is the clear text.

13744
23:50:50,286 --> 23:50:55,392
Once we add the encryption layer to it, we get the ciphertext to it.

13745
23:50:57,440 --> 23:51:00,708
Moving on to the next question: What is a block cipher?

13746
23:51:01,422 --> 23:51:05,200
This refers to the method of encrypting the plain message block by block.

13747
23:51:05,360 --> 23:51:08,720
The plain message is broken down into fixed size blocks and then encrypted.

13748
23:51:08,800 --> 23:51:12,720
Now a block cipher is normally used for data that is stored.

13749
23:51:12,878 --> 23:51:18,880
So a data that is stored on a hard disk, and we want to encrypt that data that is known as block encryption or a block cipher.

13750
23:51:19,040 --> 23:51:26,160
So a block cipher is an algorithm that will allow you to encrypt data that is stored onto a hard disk.

13751
23:51:26,638 --> 23:51:34,720
So in this example, we've got plain text which is 64 bits in size, and we have added a layer of encryption to it.

13752
23:51:34,894 --> 23:51:42,724
So plain text plus the key that we have studied in the previous questions, and then the scrambled data out of it, which is unreadable and thus encrypted.

13753
23:51:43,086 --> 23:51:45,732
Then the next question: what is public key infrastructure?

13754
23:51:46,190 --> 23:51:51,360
Now the public key infrastructure is a set of policies which secures the communication between a server and a client.

13755
23:51:51,440 --> 23:51:54,400
It uses two cryptographic keys, public and private.

13756
23:51:54,720 --> 23:52:13,796
So the infrastructure itself is a set of policies, people, procedures, and techniques which are standardized in nature and are globally accepted, which allow us to use digital certificates to encrypt data and decrypt the data at the other end.

13757
23:52:14,222 --> 23:52:22,052
We use asymmetric encryption over here, which means that we use two keys: one is a public key to encrypt and the private key to decrypt.

13758
23:52:22,958 --> 23:52:29,124
The other part of encryption is a symmetric encryption where the same key is used to encrypt and the same key is used to decrypt.

13759
23:52:30,080 --> 23:52:35,120
Now, in a public key infrastructure, like I said, we have standardized that.

13760
23:52:35,200 --> 23:52:40,836
So in the standardizing part of it, these are the various players that have been defined in the public key infrastructure.

13761
23:52:41,166 --> 23:53:07,332
The first is the user or the sender in this scenario, the one who requires this digital signature to digitally sign a particular transaction or a communication, a registration authority with whom they're going to register for that particular key, the certification authority, who issues that key, the verification authority, who validates the key itself, and the recipient who's going to be the other party of that particular transaction.

13762
23:53:07,680 --> 23:53:09,252
So, how is this utilized?

13763
23:53:10,350 --> 23:53:18,080
A sender or the user who requires this digital signature will request or apply for a digital signature with the registration authority.

13764
23:53:18,240 --> 23:53:23,280
The registration authority would validate the genuine of the user.

13765
23:53:23,566 --> 23:53:30,000
So they might do some identity verification or proof of residence or something like that.

13766
23:53:30,080 --> 23:53:46,308
Once they've identified the person and they have validated the information, they will then send the request to the certification authority stating that the sender has been validated and we can it and the certification authority can issue the digital certificate to the particular user.

13767
23:53:47,278 --> 23:53:53,956
They will send the public key to the sender, which will be utilized by the sender for further transactions.

13768
23:53:54,254 --> 23:54:02,884
So when the sender is going to sign some data and wants to send it across to the recipient, they will use the public key to sign it and send it across.

13769
23:54:03,342 --> 23:54:10,788
The recipient will then validate with the verification authority to see if the data, the signed data, is correct or not.

13770
23:54:11,120 --> 23:54:20,160
Now, while the certification authority sends the public key to the sender, the certification authority updates the private key with the verification authority.

13771
23:54:20,366 --> 23:54:27,920
So whatever is signed by the sender received by the recipient and they want to validate it, they will send it back to the verification authority.

13772
23:54:28,206 --> 23:54:31,332
The verification authority will validate using the private key.

13773
23:54:31,600 --> 23:54:41,316
Once the private key is validated, it will then send the okay signal back to the recipient, thus allowing the validation of that particular transaction.

13774
23:54:42,190 --> 23:54:54,660
If the signature is tampered with or is not the verification authority is not able to validate the signature, it will then send a denial message back to the recipient and the transaction will not go through.

13775
23:54:55,086 --> 23:54:58,480
So the PKI enables trusted digital identities for people.

13776
23:54:58,734 --> 23:55:11,652
So the PKI grants secure access to digital resources based on the infrastructure that has been created, and the core of the PKI is a certification authority which ensures that the trustworthiness of the digital data is ensured.

13777
23:55:12,240 --> 23:55:20,000
So going back to the previous slide, these are the key players that have been standardized in the public key infrastructure.

13778
23:55:20,238 --> 23:55:27,844
The certification authority is the authority that issues the digital certificates, the validation authority is the one who validates that digital certificate.

13779
23:55:28,366 --> 23:55:30,320
Moving on, what is RSA?

13780
23:55:31,566 --> 23:55:37,088
RSA is one of the first public key cryptosystems that is used for secure data transmission.

13781
23:55:37,168 --> 23:55:39,808
It stands for Revest, Shamir, and Edelman.

13782
23:55:40,206 --> 23:55:48,804
Now, these are the three people who have created this algorithm: Ron Rivest, Adi Shamir, and Leonard Edelman, who are the inventors of this technique.

13783
23:55:49,198 --> 23:55:53,328
It is an asymmetric cryptography algorithm which works on both public and private keys.

13784
23:55:53,408 --> 23:55:57,764
Hence, the encryption key is public and the decryption key is kept private.

13785
23:55:57,968 --> 23:56:01,568
Now, as we have discussed earlier, symmetric and asymmetric cryptography.

13786
23:56:01,728 --> 23:56:06,372
Symmetric cryptography is where the same key is used to encrypt and decrypt.

13787
23:56:06,638 --> 23:56:13,252
Whereas asymmetric cryptography is where there are two keys to encrypt and decrypt the algorithm.

13788
23:56:13,646 --> 23:56:15,728
What are the few alternatives to RSA?

13789
23:56:15,968 --> 23:56:19,088
Now RSA is an algorithm that is used for encryption.

13790
23:56:19,168 --> 23:56:25,808
There are a lot of other algorithms that can be utilized to alter or to scramble data depending on your requirements.

13791
23:56:25,968 --> 23:56:29,968
So in the previous question, we have studied and we have talked about what RSA is.

13792
23:56:30,048 --> 23:56:35,140
It stands for reverse, shamir, and edelma, the three creators of that particular algorithm.

13793
23:56:35,502 --> 23:56:40,484
But there are a lot of alternatives to this algorithm depending on how secure you want that data to be.

13794
23:56:41,088 --> 23:56:48,448
And some of them are listed here on your screen Duo Security, Okta, Google Authenticator, and LastPass.

13795
23:56:48,750 --> 23:56:52,848
LastPass is a password manager, so is Duo Security.

13796
23:56:53,008 --> 23:56:55,328
Google Authenticator is something that we all utilize.

13797
23:56:55,408 --> 23:57:04,484
It is an application that we can download and store on our mobile devices, and we can set that up to authenticate ourselves with certain portals.

13798
23:57:04,688 --> 23:57:13,088
So it issues a unique ID to us, which once utilized will allow us access to those particular portals.

13799
23:57:13,390 --> 23:57:30,608
Okta is an identity manager where you have you have created different digital identities and you have assigned them certain permissions, and based on your authentication mechanisms, Okta will allow or disallow access to those different applications or different portals as you have configured it.

13800
23:57:30,688 --> 23:57:37,328
So all four are authorization and authentication mechanisms which can be used as alternatives to RSA.

