13804
23:57:47,648 --> 23:57:51,008
Next question: what are the prime objectives of modern cryptography?

13805
23:57:51,118 --> 23:57:57,968
And this is a very important question because we've we've so far looked at what cryptography is and what public key infrastructure is.

13806
23:57:58,128 --> 23:57:59,808
But what is the achievement out of it?

13807
23:57:59,968 --> 23:58:01,328
Why are we utilizing it?

13808
23:58:01,454 --> 23:58:04,208
And what do we want to gain out of it?

13809
23:58:04,462 --> 23:58:10,688
So the main and the prime objectives of modern cryptography are as follows mentioned on your screen.

13810
23:58:10,848 --> 23:58:18,916
The first one is confidentiality, the second one is non-repudiation, third one is authenticity, and the fourth one is integrity.

13811
23:58:19,328 --> 23:58:26,048
Now, if I go back to the first one, confidentiality, that is where I want to keep data confidential.

13812
23:58:26,222 --> 23:58:29,728
That means it will only be visible to the authorized users.

13813
23:58:40,078 --> 23:58:43,236
Now that is how confidentiality is ensured.

13814
23:58:43,408 --> 23:58:55,088
So, when we want to keep data confidential, we create a list of users who we are going to allow access to certain resources, and we are going to define what access controls are to be utilized.

13815
23:58:55,168 --> 23:59:05,008
what access are allowed, whether they got administrative access or user-level access, and only those authorized users are going to be able to access these resources.

13816
23:59:05,168 --> 23:59:07,300
That is how we maintain confidentiality.

13817
23:59:08,334 --> 23:59:10,208
The next one is non-repudiation.

13818
23:59:10,368 --> 23:59:16,612
Non-reputation is the prevention of denial of having been a part of a particular transaction.

13819
23:59:17,038 --> 23:59:34,532
So in the public key infrastructure that we discussed, where a digital signature was utilized to sign a particular transaction and then sent to the recipient, the sender would not be able to deny of having originated that transaction because it was using their digital certificate.

13820
23:59:34,958 --> 23:59:36,848
Thus, non-reputation comes into the picture.

13821
23:59:37,168 --> 23:59:47,888
One more example that we can have here is on our mobile phones when we use SMS short messaging service and we send a message to to another person.

13822
23:59:48,142 --> 23:59:57,568
The person, when they receive a message, the number is validated by the service operator, and thus the sender cannot deny having sent that message.

13823
23:59:57,870 --> 24:00:12,836
The sender at the same time can have a delivery report sent to them from that the message was delivered to the inbox of the recipient, and thus, if the recipient denies having received that message, the delivery report becomes proof of having that message being delivered to their inbox.

13824
24:00:13,008 --> 24:00:18,788
Thus, both the parties cannot deny of being a part of that particular transaction.

13825
24:00:21,070 --> 24:00:22,724
Then comes the part of authenticity.

13826
24:00:24,046 --> 24:00:34,928
Now in confidentiality, we have created a digital identity, assigned it to a particular person, and we have given them digital signatures where they cannot deny having been a part of that transaction.

13827
24:00:35,088 --> 24:00:39,488
But authenticity is the part where they try to prove that they are who they claim to be.

13828
24:00:39,568 --> 24:00:46,768
So if I am claiming a digital identity, I have to prove that I am that person who I'm who I'm trying to claim to be.

13829
24:00:46,862 --> 24:00:53,728
And an example to that is when we go to our gmail.com websites, it first asks us what is our username.

13830
24:00:53,888 --> 24:00:59,204
Our username is normally our email address, which identifies the account that we are trying to access.

13831
24:00:59,648 --> 24:00:59,968
Right?

13832
24:01:00,128 --> 24:01:04,804
So this account is confidential because it is only authorized for a particular person.

13833
24:01:05,742 --> 24:01:12,964
And once they identify themselves by identifying the email address, that's when the authentication part comes into the picture where it asks for the password.

13834
24:01:13,902 --> 24:01:20,484
Now it has never ever happened that we just go on to the gmail.com type in a password, and then it figures out which account we are talking about.

13835
24:01:20,910 --> 24:01:32,932
So the first step is always called the confidentiality part where we identify which account we are talking about, and then we try to authenticate as the owner of that particular account by providing the appropriate password to that account.

13836
24:01:33,168 --> 24:01:41,808
If both of these match, only then do we get access to that account and we are able to make whatever transactions we want to make.

13837
24:01:42,094 --> 24:01:48,228
Now, when we are making those transactions, non-reputation comes into the place where all our activity is also being logged.

13838
24:01:48,448 --> 24:01:53,008
So we have identified our account, we have authenticated ourselves by providing the password.

13839
24:01:53,166 --> 24:01:56,036
So the proof is there that it is us who are trying to access it.

13840
24:01:56,622 --> 24:02:07,620
And then whatever activity we do, send an email, receive an email, delete something, attach something, all of those activities are logged and stored as proof of what actions have been done.

13841
24:02:08,110 --> 24:02:16,484
So tomorrow, if we deny having sent that email, Gmail can still prove to us through those logs that that activity was done by us.

13842
24:02:17,966 --> 24:02:30,116
And the fourth part is integrity, which ensures that the data received and sent and sent by the sender and received by the recipient has not been modified while in motion.

13843
24:02:30,638 --> 24:02:39,524
So the integrity part is the trustworthiness of that data, that the data has not been modified by any hacker or any other entity and is still trustworthy.

13844
24:02:40,174 --> 24:02:42,928
So these are the four prime objectives of modern cryptography.

13845
24:02:43,088 --> 24:02:51,748
Once I have scrambled that data using my public signature, it is only my private signature that is going to decrypt it.

13846
24:02:53,358 --> 24:03:00,208
Using these mechanisms, I will be able to achieve all these four aspects of cryptography and security.

13847
24:03:00,494 --> 24:03:02,448
Next question: What is Safer?

13848
24:03:03,054 --> 24:03:07,408
Now, Safer stands for secure and fast encryption routine, which is also a block cipher.

13849
24:03:07,662 --> 24:03:13,924
As we have discussed previously, block cipher is a cipher that is used to encrypt data that is stored.

13850
24:03:14,606 --> 24:03:18,116
So it has a 64-bit block size and byte-oriented algorithm.

13851
24:03:18,688 --> 24:03:21,488
Safer's encryption and decryption procedures are highly secure.

13852
24:03:21,742 --> 24:03:24,768
This technology is widely used in applications like digital payment cards.

13853
24:03:24,848 --> 24:03:51,248
So, when you're using a digital payment gateway to make transactions, so you have gone onto an online portal, you want to purchase a particular item, and then it takes you to another payment gateway where you have to fill in your credit card, information, sensitive information like your expiry date, CVV information, and then the OTP or the password that you have created for your particular account.

13854
24:03:51,328 --> 24:03:59,044
Now, all of these need to be secured or highly secured based on PCI DSS, which is the payment card industry data security standard.

13855
24:03:59,278 --> 24:04:04,928
So these standards ensure that certain protocols are utilized to attain that level of security.

13856
24:04:05,088 --> 24:04:11,492
Safer is one of those block ciphers that is used under the digital payment gateway infrastructure.

13857
24:04:11,854 --> 24:04:15,940
Next question How does the public infrastructure public key infrastructure work?

13858
24:04:16,750 --> 24:04:19,552
Now we have already discussed this in the previous diagrams.

13859
24:04:19,872 --> 24:04:36,832
We have identified the key players: the certification authority, the registration authority, the end user who requires the digital certificate, the validation authority who's going to validate it, and then the recipient, the end user with whom the transaction is going to be conducted.

13860
24:04:37,198 --> 24:04:51,012
So the first point here is the request for the digital certificate is sent to the registration authority, they validate it, and then they okay it to the certification authority who then process the request and the digital certificate is issued to the person who has requested it.

13861
24:04:51,566 --> 24:04:59,312
So when the person wants to conduct that transaction, they use that digital certificate to sign that transaction with the end user.

13862
24:04:59,598 --> 24:05:05,348
The end user validates that with the validation authority, and once validated, the transaction goes through.

13863
24:05:05,806 --> 24:05:09,028
And now the last question: what is the blowfish algorithm?

13864
24:05:09,518 --> 24:05:11,952
It is a 64-bit symmetric encryption algorithm.

13865
24:05:12,032 --> 24:05:17,392
So this is an algorithm that uses the same key to encrypt and the same key to decrypt.

13866
24:05:17,550 --> 24:05:20,112
The same secret keys used to encrypt and decrypt the messages.

13867
24:05:20,272 --> 24:05:25,764
Here the operations are based on exclusive ORs and additions to the 32-bit words.

13868
24:05:26,222 --> 24:05:28,432
The key has a maximum length of 448 bits.

13869
24:05:28,526 --> 24:05:29,952
Now, this is a little bit technical.

13870
24:05:30,272 --> 24:05:33,472
You might not want to go this technical in an interview question.

13871
24:05:33,552 --> 24:05:36,352
You just need to identify what the algorithm is used for.

13872
24:05:36,494 --> 24:05:47,972
So whether it is a symmetric algorithm, which means it uses the same key or a symmetric algorithm where it uses a public key to encrypt and a private key to decrypt.

13873
24:05:48,352 --> 24:05:54,672
Thus, the blowfish algorithm is just one more algorithm which uses symmetric encryption to encrypt and decrypt data.

13874
24:05:54,926 --> 24:06:00,592
Algorithms that we have seen, RSA and others that we have discussed, as far as the interview questions are concerned.

13875
24:06:00,672 --> 24:06:10,672
What we need to remember is which algorithms are symmetric, which algorithms are asymmetric, what do symmetric algorithms do, and what do asymmetric algorithms do.

13876
24:06:10,862 --> 24:06:14,112
And we also look at block ciphers and stream ciphers.

13877
24:06:14,446 --> 24:06:24,512
Block ciphers are utilized to encrypt data that is stored, stationary data, data at rest, and stream ciphers are utilized for data in motion while they are being streamed.

13878
24:06:24,752 --> 24:06:27,844
CSRF stands for cross-site request forgery.

13879
24:06:28,174 --> 24:06:30,432
It is a type victim is authenticated.

13880
24:06:30,606 --> 24:06:35,844
The attack takes advantage of the trust a website has in the authenticated user's browser.

13881
24:06:36,112 --> 24:06:38,020
Now we'll see how it works.

13882
24:06:38,352 --> 24:06:54,724
So security vulnerability that occurs when an attacker tricks a victim into performing an unwanted action on a website or web application in which the attacker creates a malicious website or injects malicious code into a legitimate website that the victim visits.

13883
24:06:54,990 --> 24:07:02,832
And when the victim visits the attacker's website or the compromised legitimate website, the malicious code can execute without the victim's knowledge.

13884
24:07:03,054 --> 24:07:08,032
A malicious code sends a request to the targeted website or application on behalf of the victim.

13885
24:07:08,272 --> 24:07:15,952
This request may include sensitive actions like changing the victim's password, making financial transactions or modifying personal information.

13886
24:07:16,270 --> 24:07:24,388
Since the request originates from the victim's browser, the targeted website or application considers it legitimate and processes the request.

13887
24:07:24,846 --> 24:07:30,820
The victim's account is compromised and the attacker gains unauthorized access or performs unwanted actions.

13888
24:07:31,118 --> 24:07:39,364
To protect against CSRF attacks, web developers can implement several countermeasures, including using anti-CSRF tokens.

13889
24:07:39,790 --> 24:07:41,636
Using anti-CSRF tokens.

13890
24:07:42,094 --> 24:07:46,660
Web applications can generate and include unique tokens in each form or request.

13891
24:07:46,832 --> 24:07:53,860
These tokens are verified by the server before processing the action, ensuring that the request originated from the same website.

13892
24:07:54,094 --> 24:07:56,432
The next is same site cookies attribute.

13893
24:07:56,654 --> 24:08:05,348
Web developers can set the same site attribute on cookies, which prevents them from being sent with cross-site requests, thus reducing the risk of CSRF attacks.

13894
24:08:05,632 --> 24:08:07,152
Verifying the origin header.

13895
24:08:07,374 --> 24:08:11,876
Servers can check the origin header to ensure the request originate from the same domain.

13896
24:08:12,112 --> 24:08:14,672
The next is implementing strict access control.

13897
24:08:14,912 --> 24:08:22,500
Web applications should enforce strict access controls and authorization mechanisms to ensure that only authorized actions can be performed.

13898
24:08:22,766 --> 24:08:33,284
And the next measure can be by implementing these countermeasures, organizations can significantly reduce the risk of CSRF attacks and protect their users' sensitive information and accounts.

13899
24:08:33,646 --> 24:08:40,932
And the next question is that is the most important question, which interviewer asks that is difference between XSS and CSRF.

13900
24:08:41,198 --> 24:08:53,828
So XSS, That is XSS, cross-site scripting, and CSRF cross-site request forgery are both common web application vulnerabilities, but they differ in their nature and the risk they pose.

13901
24:08:54,126 --> 24:09:03,140
So, XS is a vulnerability that occurs when an attacker injects malicious scripts into a trusted website, which is then executed by the user's browser.

13902
24:09:03,344 --> 24:09:07,904
This can happen when the website does not properly validate or sanitize user input.

13903
24:09:08,014 --> 24:09:19,492
The injected scripts can be used to steal sensitive information such as login credentials or session cookies and can also be used to modify the content of the website or redirect users to malicious websites.

13904
24:09:19,886 --> 24:09:28,464
CSRF, on the other hand, is a type of attack where an attacker tricks a user's browser into making unintended or unauthorized requests to a website.

13905
24:09:28,704 --> 24:09:34,404
This attack relies on the fact that many websites use cookies or other mechanisms for session management.

13906
24:09:34,670 --> 24:09:38,944
And these cookies are automatically sent with every request made to the website.

13907
24:09:39,214 --> 24:09:54,144
By tricking a user into visiting a specially crafted website or clicking on a malicious link, the attacker can perform actions on behalf of the user without their knowledge or consent, potentially leading to unauthorized actions, data manipulation, or account compromise.

13908
24:09:54,382 --> 24:10:06,144
In summary, XSS is concerned with injecting and executing malicious scripts within a website, while CSRF involves tricking users into performing unintended actions on a trusted website.

13909
24:10:06,350 --> 24:10:21,104
Both vulnerabilities can have serious consequences and it is important for web developers to understand and mitigate these risks through proper input validation, output encoding and security mechanisms such as CSRF tokens and secure session management.

13910
24:10:21,422 --> 24:10:26,224
Now moving to next question Is XSS client side attack or server side attack?

13911
24:10:26,384 --> 24:10:35,908
So XSS, that is cross-site scripting, attack can occur on the client side, which means it targets vulnerabilities in the user's web browser or the client application.

13912
24:10:36,174 --> 24:10:45,424
This type of attack takes advantage of security weaknesses in the client side code, allowing an attacker to inject malicious scripts into a trusted website or web application.

13913
24:10:45,742 --> 24:10:53,124
Once the script is executed by the user's browser, it can lead to unauthorized actions, data theft or other malicious activities.

13914
24:10:53,422 --> 24:11:01,504
And it's important to note that while XSS attacks primarily target the client side, they can have significant consequences on the server side as well.

13915
24:11:01,774 --> 24:11:09,572
The injected script may interact with the server or exploit vulnerabilities in the server side code to perform actions on behalf of the attacker.

13916
24:11:09,824 --> 24:11:16,932
Therefore, XSS attacks can have an impact on both the client and server components of a web application security.

13917
24:11:17,198 --> 24:11:19,460
And the next question is what is IOC?

13918
24:11:19,726 --> 24:11:23,716
In cybersecurity, IOC stands for indicators of compromise.

13919
24:11:23,950 --> 24:11:32,064
These are pieces of evidence or artifacts that suggest the presence of malicious activity or a security breach within a system or network.

13920
24:11:32,334 --> 24:11:36,964
IOCs are used to identify, detect and respond to potential security incidents.

13921
24:11:37,294 --> 24:11:40,864
There are several types of IOCs that security professionals look for.

13922
24:11:41,024 --> 24:11:43,780
The most common types include file-based IOCs.

13923
24:11:43,904 --> 24:11:51,984
These include malicious files such as malware, executables, scripts, or documents that have been identified as part of a cyber attack.

13924
24:11:52,206 --> 24:11:57,664
File-based IOCs can be identified through file hashes, file names, or specific file content.

13925
24:11:57,870 --> 24:11:59,940
The next is network-based IOCs.

13926
24:12:00,144 --> 24:12:04,356
These indicators relate to network traffic associated with malicious activities.

13927
24:12:04,654 --> 24:12:13,104
They can include suspicious IP addresses, domain names, URLs, or patterns of network communications that indicate a potential security threat.

13928
24:12:13,358 --> 24:12:15,524
The next we have is behavioral IOCs.

13929
24:12:15,824 --> 24:12:21,444
These indicators are based on abnormal behavior exhibited by users, systems or applications.

13930
24:12:21,710 --> 24:12:31,076
For example, a sudden increase in failed login attempts, unusual file access patterns or unauthorized system modifications can serve as behavioral IOCs.

13931
24:12:31,406 --> 24:12:35,012
The next is registry or system configuration IOCs.

13932
24:12:35,344 --> 24:12:42,544
These indicators involve changes or additions to the Windows registry, system configuration files or other system settings.

13933
24:12:42,766 --> 24:12:47,744
Attackers may alter these configurations to gain persistence on a compromised system.

13934
24:12:47,904 --> 24:12:50,308
The next is threat intelligence IOCs.

13935
24:12:50,606 --> 24:12:57,764
These IOCs are derived from external sources such as cybersecurity vendors, government agencies or threat intelligence platforms.

13936
24:12:58,094 --> 24:13:06,884
They provide information about known malicious actors, their tactics, techniques and procedures, and specific indicators associated with their activities.

13937
24:13:07,246 --> 24:13:13,220
When organizations collect and analyze IOCs, they can proactively detect and respond to security incidents.

13938
24:13:13,582 --> 24:13:22,944
By monitoring for these indicators, security teams can identify potential threats, investigate the scope of an attack, and take appropriate measures to mitigate the risk.

13939
24:13:23,342 --> 24:13:29,792
It's worth noting that IOCs are not static and continuously evolve as attackers develop new techniques.

13940
24:13:29,998 --> 24:13:39,780
Therefore, staying up to date with the latest threat intelligence and sharing information within the cybersecurity community is crucial for effective defense against cyber threats.

13941
24:13:40,046 --> 24:13:42,852
And now moving to another question that is fifth question.

13942
24:13:43,152 --> 24:13:48,740
And it is that can you explain the difference between antivirus and EDR in cybersecurity?

13943
24:13:49,102 --> 24:13:57,764
So antivirus and endpoint detection and response, that is EDR, are both important components in cybersecurity, but they serve different purposes.

13944
24:13:58,062 --> 24:13:58,832
We'll see them.

13945
24:13:59,072 --> 24:14:03,236
That is first antivirus, that is also known as anti-malware software.

13946
24:14:03,406 --> 24:14:11,108
It is designed to detect, prevent and remove known types of malicious software such as viruses, bombs, trojans, and ransomware.

13947
24:14:11,312 --> 24:14:19,236
It relies on signature-based detection where it compares the code patterns or files or programs against a database of known malware signatures.

13948
24:14:19,630 --> 24:14:25,828
When a match is found, the antivirus software takes appropriate action to quarantine or delete the infected file.

13949
24:14:26,112 --> 24:14:35,712
On the other hand, the EDR goes beyond traditional antivirus solutions by providing real-time monitoring, detection and response capabilities at the endpoint level.

13950
24:14:36,161 --> 24:14:43,652
EDR solutions are designed to detect and respond to sophisticated and advanced threats that may evade traditional antivirus software.

13951
24:14:43,918 --> 24:14:52,868
They use a combination of techniques including behavioral analysis, machine learning, and threat intelligence to identify malicious activities and potential threats.

13952
24:14:53,321 --> 24:15:02,372
EDR solutions continuously monitor endpoints such as workstations, servers and mobile devices for any suspicious activities or anomalous behavior.

13953
24:15:02,606 --> 24:15:13,540
They collect and analyze vast amounts of endpoint data, including process executions, network connections, file changes, and system configurations to identify potential security incidents.

13954
24:15:13,870 --> 24:15:23,972
If a threat is detected, EDR solutions provide detailed visibility into the incident, allowing security teams to investigate, contain and respond to the threat in real time.

13955
24:15:24,302 --> 24:15:35,072
While antivirus focuses on known threats and relies on signature-based detection, EDR solutions are more proactive and capable of identifying previously unseen or zero-day threats.

13956
24:15:35,278 --> 24:15:47,712
They offer enhanced threat hunting capabilities, allowing security analysts to search for indicators of compromise, i.e., IOCs, and identify potential vulnerabilities or security gaps in the environment.

13957
24:15:47,952 --> 24:16:02,020
In summary, antivirus is an essential component in protecting against known malware, while EDR provides advanced threat detection, real-time monitoring, and incident response capabilities to defend against sophisticated and emerging threats.

13958
24:16:02,286 --> 24:16:08,964
Combining both antivirus and EDR solutions can significantly enhance an organization's overall cybersecurity posture.

13959
24:16:09,262 --> 24:16:12,740
Now moving to another question that is IPS versus firewall.

13960
24:16:12,832 --> 24:16:16,964
That is, can you explain the difference between IPS and a firewall in cybersecurity?

13961
24:16:17,552 --> 24:16:25,732
So, an IPS that is an intrusion prevention system and a firewall are two distinct but complementary security mechanisms.

13962
24:16:26,062 --> 24:16:27,940
Let's see the differences between them.

13963
24:16:28,462 --> 24:16:34,352
So a firewall acts as a barrier between an internal network and external network, typically the internet.

13964
24:16:34,672 --> 24:16:39,940
It examines incoming and outgoing network traffic based on predefined rules or policies.

13965
24:16:40,302 --> 24:16:51,172
Its primary function is to enforce access controls and permit or deny network traffic based on factors such as IP addresses, ports, protocols and application types.

13966
24:16:51,662 --> 24:16:57,472
Firewalls work at the network layer, layer 3 or the transport layer, layer 4 of the network stack.

13967
24:16:57,902 --> 24:17:02,884
On the other hand, an IPS is designed to detect and prevent network-based attacks.

13968
24:17:03,216 --> 24:17:06,564
It goes beyond the basic access control functions of a firewall.

13969
24:17:06,862 --> 24:17:13,284
An IPS monitors network traffic in real time, searching for signs of known attack patterns or suspicious behavior.

13970
24:17:13,550 --> 24:17:19,876
It can detect and block unauthorized access attempts, malware, network intrusions and other malicious activities.

13971
24:17:20,206 --> 24:17:30,816
IPS systems are typically placed in line with network traffic and can operate at the network layer, that is layer 3, transport layer, layer 4, or even the application layer, that is layer 7.

13972
24:17:31,086 --> 24:17:38,916
To summarize, a firewall acts as a gatekeeper, controlling the flow of traffic into and out of a network based on predefined rules.

13973
24:17:39,310 --> 24:17:41,056
It focuses on access control.

13974
24:17:41,216 --> 24:17:51,696
On the other hand, NIPS is a more advanced security mechanism that actively inspects network traffic, identifies potential threats or attacks, and takes action to prevent them.

13975
24:17:51,790 --> 24:17:54,692
It focuses on detecting and preventing intrusions.

13976
24:17:55,118 --> 24:18:01,136
In practice, organizations often use both firewalls and IPS systems together to provide layered security.

13977
24:18:01,454 --> 24:18:12,004
Firewalls protect against unauthorized access and filter out unwanted traffic while IPS systems add an additional layer of protection by actively monitoring and blocking malicious activities.

13978
24:18:12,334 --> 24:18:14,656
So this was all for firewall and IPS.

13979
24:18:14,736 --> 24:18:17,444
Now moving to what is security misconfiguration.

13980
24:18:17,806 --> 24:18:28,260
So security misconfiguration refers to the improper or insecure configuration of systems, software, applications or network components that can lead to potential security vulnerabilities.

13981
24:18:28,750 --> 24:18:39,172
It occurs when security settings, permissions or configurations are not appropriately defined or implemented, leaving systems and data exposed to potential attacks or unauthorized access.

13982
24:18:39,566 --> 24:18:55,364
Security misconfiguration can take various forms, such as using default or weak passwords leaving unnecessary services or ports open, not applying necessary security patches or updates, misconfiguring access controls or failing to implement secure communication protocols.

13983
24:18:55,616 --> 24:19:06,116
These misconfigurations can provide attackers with entry points into a system, allowing them to exploit vulnerabilities, gain unauthorized access or carry out malicious activities.

13984
24:19:06,350 --> 24:19:13,188
Organizations often face security misconfigurations due to human error, lack of awareness or inadequate security practices.

13985
24:19:13,390 --> 24:19:26,016
It's crucial to regularly review and audit system configurations, apply secure defaults, disable unnecessary features, follow security best practices, and stay updated with the latest patches and updates.

13986
24:19:26,254 --> 24:19:35,844
By addressing security misconfigurations, organizations can significantly reduce their attack surface and enhance the overall security posture of their systems and networks.

13987
24:19:36,334 --> 24:19:40,816
So moving on to next question that is what is WAF and its types.

13988
24:19:41,166 --> 24:19:44,068
So WAF stands for Web Application Firewall.

13989
24:19:44,256 --> 24:19:49,060
It is a security measure used to protect web applications from various types of cyber attacks.

13990
24:19:49,326 --> 24:19:57,348
A WAF acts as a filter between the web application and the external network, monitoring and analyzing incoming and outgoing web traffic.

13991
24:19:57,646 --> 24:20:03,803
There are primarily two types of WFs commonly used in cybersecurity that are network-based WAF.

13992
24:20:04,302 --> 24:20:12,004
This type of WAF is implemented at the network parameter, typically as a dedicated hardware appliance or a virtual appliance.

13993
24:20:12,302 --> 24:20:16,196
It intercepts web traffic before it reaches the web application server.

13994
24:20:16,526 --> 24:20:26,596
And network-based WFs examine packets at the network layer, inspecting IP addresses, ports and protocols to detect and prevent malicious activities.

13995
24:20:27,022 --> 24:20:32,036
They are effective in protecting multiple web applications hosted on the same network infrastructure.

13996
24:20:32,462 --> 24:20:34,564
And the next is host-based WF.

13997
24:20:34,894 --> 24:20:40,484
This type of WF is installed directly on the web application server or within the application itself.

13998
24:20:40,814 --> 24:20:45,924
It operates at the application layer and has a deep understanding of the application structure and logic.

13999
24:20:46,318 --> 24:20:53,828
Host based WFs monitor and analyze HTTP, HTTPS traffic, inspecting the content of requests and responses.

14000
24:20:54,254 --> 24:20:59,076
They are often integrated as a module or a plugin within the web server or the application framework.

14001
24:20:59,438 --> 24:21:06,436
Host based WFs provide granular control and can protect specific applications or modules within a server.

14002
24:21:07,086 --> 24:21:13,536
Both network-based and host-based WFs have their strength and can complement each other for enhanced security.

14003
24:21:13,870 --> 24:21:27,044
Network-based WFs are suitable for large-scale protection across multiple applications and servers, while host-based WFs offer more precise control and visibility into application-specific vulnerabilities and attacks.

14004
24:21:27,598 --> 24:21:44,164
It's important to note that while WFs are valuable in safeguarding web applications, they should be used in conjunction with other security measures like regular application patching, secure coding practices and vulnerability assessments to ensure comprehensive protection against evolving cyber threats.

14005
24:21:44,590 --> 24:21:46,096
Now we'll see another question.

14006
24:21:46,382 --> 24:21:49,860
That is, could you please explain the difference between blue teaming and red teaming?

14007
24:21:50,414 --> 24:21:57,860
So blue teaming and red teaming are two distinct approaches used in cybersecurity to enhance the overall security posture of an organization.

14008
24:21:58,286 --> 24:22:02,468
And blue teaming primarily focuses on defense and proactive measures.

14009
24:22:02,926 --> 24:22:09,572
Blue teams are responsible for protecting the organization's assets, systems and data from potential threats and attacks.

14010
24:22:09,998 --> 24:22:18,308
They typically include cybersecurity professionals within the organizations who work to identify vulnerabilities, monitor networks and implement security controls.

14011
24:22:18,606 --> 24:22:23,940
Blue teams conduct risk assessments, perform security audits, and develop incident response plans.

14012
24:22:24,398 --> 24:22:30,020
They also monitor security systems, analyze logs, and use various tools to detect and mitigate threats.

14013
24:22:30,336 --> 24:22:36,676
Essentially, Blue teams act as defenders, ensuring that the organization's defenses are strong and resilient.

14014
24:22:36,942 --> 24:22:40,096
On the other hand, red teaming takes an offensive approach.

14015
24:22:40,366 --> 24:22:49,284
Red teams are independent groups or individuals who stimulate real-world cyber attacks to evaluate the effectiveness of the organization's security controls and practices.

14016
24:22:49,582 --> 24:22:53,856
Their goal is to identify weaknesses, gaps and vulnerabilities in the system.

14017
24:22:54,222 --> 24:23:04,036
Red teams use tactics similar to those of real attackers, such as social engineering, penetration testing, and vulnerability assessments to uncover potential security flows.

14018
24:23:04,462 --> 24:23:13,796
By adopting the perspective of an attacker, red teams provide valuable insights into the organization's security, vulnerabilities, allowing the blue team to improve their defenses.

14019
24:23:14,190 --> 24:23:19,268
In summary, blue teaming is focused on defense while red teaming is focused on offense.

14020
24:23:19,694 --> 24:23:27,812
Blue teams work to prevent and mitigate cyber threats, while red teams act as adversaries to identify weaknesses and improve the organization's security.

14021
24:23:28,430 --> 24:23:35,780
The collaboration between blue and red team is crucial in maintaining a strong security posture and effectively protecting sensitive information.

14022
24:23:36,302 --> 24:23:37,216
So this was all.

14023
24:23:37,486 --> 24:23:40,356
That is the difference between blue teaming and red teaming.

14024
24:23:40,654 --> 24:23:45,956
Now moving on to the 10th question that is what is false positive and false negative in case of IDS.

14025
24:23:46,478 --> 24:23:58,980
So in the context of intrusion detection system that is IDS, false positive and false negative are two important terms used to describe the accuracy of the system in identifying and classifying security events.

14026
24:23:59,310 --> 24:24:05,828
A false positive occurs when an IDS incorrectly identifies a banning activity or event as malicious.

14027
24:24:06,158 --> 24:24:12,576
On the other hand, a false negative happens when an IDS fails to detect an actual malicious activity or event.

14028
24:24:12,686 --> 24:24:17,776
It occurs when an intrusion or attack goes unnoticed and is not flagged by the system.

14029
24:24:17,936 --> 24:24:25,284
This can be quite dangerous as it allows malicious activities to go undetected, potentially causing harm to the system or network.

14030
24:24:25,518 --> 24:24:30,596
Ideally, an IDS should strive to minimize both false positives and false negatives.

14031
24:24:30,896 --> 24:24:33,296
However, there is often a trade-off between the two.

14032
24:24:33,456 --> 24:24:40,176
Increasing the sensitivity of an IDS to reduce false negatives may result in more false positives, and vice versa.

14033
24:24:40,462 --> 24:24:47,588
Balancing the detection accuracy is crucial to ensure that genuine threats are identified while minimizing the number of false alarms.

14034
24:24:47,886 --> 24:24:55,844
So to improve the effectiveness of an IDS, continuously monitoring fine tuning of detection rules and incorporating machine learning algorithms can be employed.

14035
24:24:56,078 --> 24:25:07,216
Additionally, it's important for security teams to carefully analyze and validate alerts to distinguish between false positive and true threats, ensuring efficient incident response and mitigation.

