14388
25:09:16,718 --> 25:09:30,372
So whatever activity that you have done so far will have created logs and will have created information based on which the victim will come to know that they have been compromised and may be able to trace that activity back to you.

14389
25:09:30,848 --> 25:09:44,605
So to prevent the user or the victim from realizing that they have been hacked and to prevent them to discover who has hacked them, you want to cover your tracks by deleting logs and any references that point to that particular activity.

14390
25:09:44,605 --> 25:09:46,608
You are going to hide the files that you have created.

14391
25:09:46,688 --> 25:09:51,088
So you have installed a Trojan or a Keylogger, these will create files and directories.

14392
25:09:51,168 --> 25:09:53,648
You're going to hide them so that they are not discovered.

14393
25:09:53,934 --> 25:09:55,968
You're going to hide processes that have been created.

14394
25:09:56,048 --> 25:10:06,436
You're going to try to hide all the activity that you have done so that to conceal the actual attack and preventing the user from realizing that they have been compromised.

14395
25:10:07,246 --> 25:10:14,212
So these are the five steps that will be involved in hacking a server network application or any computing device you'll come across.

14396
25:10:14,542 --> 25:10:18,528
The next question refers to what are the various sniffing tools.

14397
25:10:18,766 --> 25:10:33,728
Now, this is a network-based attack where you're trying to capture data packets that have been transmitted over the network, and then you're going to analyze them to see if you can capture any sensitive information like usernames, passwords, bank details, or anything of that sort.

14398
25:10:33,968 --> 25:10:37,888
Now, these tools will also depend on which operating system you are utilizing.

14399
25:10:38,048 --> 25:10:46,448
For example, MSN Sniffer would work on Microsoft operating systems, EaterCab would be based on Linux and so on so forth.

14400
25:10:46,528 --> 25:10:50,848
So on the screen you'll see six different sniffing tools that work on different operating systems.

14401
25:10:51,214 --> 25:10:56,128
Wireshark is something that is common both on Windows and Linux.

14402
25:10:57,280 --> 25:10:59,440
It is used to analyze network in details.

14403
25:10:59,520 --> 25:11:08,836
It is the de facto tool that you will come across in most of your ethical hacking trainings, in most of your organizations when they want to do data captures.

14404
25:11:10,542 --> 25:11:24,004
Now, data capturing or packet capturing is not only done by hackers to gather more information, but it is also a known troubleshooting technique used by administrators and network administrators to analyze any issues that may be going on in the network.

14405
25:11:24,974 --> 25:11:28,036
So while the first tool you see on the screen is Wireshark.

14406
25:11:28,320 --> 25:11:32,324
Like we stated, is available for Windows, Linux as well.

14407
25:11:32,878 --> 25:11:42,628
Then there is TCP dump, which again has the same capability of Wireshark, but is a command line version, whereas Wireshark also has a GUI, a graphical user interface.

14408
25:11:43,182 --> 25:11:45,760
TCP Dump is available on Linux.

14409
25:11:46,574 --> 25:11:49,124
MSN Sniffer, it's a very old tool.

14410
25:11:50,446 --> 25:12:02,800
when we had MSN Messengers, MSN Messenger is no longer there, but Microsoft does or did have a Microsoft Message Analyzer tool, which they have stopped development since 2015.

14411
25:12:03,120 --> 25:12:09,520
but that's another tool that is specific for Microsoft operating systems from Microsoft that can be installed to gather more information.

14412
25:12:09,934 --> 25:12:19,680
Then you got EaterCap, which is a tool to launch man-in-the-middle attacks, data capturing, and is essentially a Linux command line-based tool.

14413
25:12:20,206 --> 25:12:28,772
Then dsniff is another password and network capturing tool which can help you capture data packets, prominently a Linux tool.

14414
25:12:29,454 --> 25:12:30,800
Same with Eater8.

14415
25:12:32,238 --> 25:12:41,732
This is a graphical tool which will allow you to capture data data traffics and map protocols and identify which IP addresses have been communicating with what.

14416
25:12:41,934 --> 25:12:57,028
Essentially, all of the tools have similar functionality except that some have additional functionality like lodging man-in-the-middle attacks or capturing or having specific filters that will help you identify and troubleshoot some network issues that you may be facing.

14417
25:12:57,358 --> 25:12:59,760
Question 9 What is SQL injection?

14418
25:13:00,000 --> 25:13:08,400
SQL stands for structured query language, which is a language that is used by most of your databases or your relational databases.

14419
25:13:09,550 --> 25:13:23,360
the variations of your database would be MySQL, Microsoft SQL, Oracle SQL, you'll have IBM databases, all of these databases utilize the structured query languages to interact with the applications.

14420
25:13:23,534 --> 25:13:34,148
Now, all of these databases have their own syntax, so you'll have to study most of these databases based on which applications and which databases you want to provide security for.

14421
25:13:34,800 --> 25:13:54,916
But as the name suggests, SQL injection vulnerability, or a structured query language injection vulnerability, is where a user can maliciously inject a SQL input or a SQL statement in a query and send it to the database and evoke a response response out of it.

14422
25:13:55,520 --> 25:13:59,600
So this vulnerability is not specifically to the database.

14423
25:13:59,943 --> 25:14:03,823
It, the vulnerability lies more in the application and the coding of that application.

14424
25:14:04,334 --> 25:14:15,840
So when the application receives a query which it needs to be forwarded to the SQL uh database, we need to configure at the application level of what queries are allowed and what queries are not allowed.

14425
25:14:15,920 --> 25:14:21,600
So there are different various aspects of how to manage a SQL injection vulnerability.

14426
25:14:21,774 --> 25:14:39,172
But the basic flaw lies in the application where invalidated input is accepted and sent forward to the database where the database might confuse it into an executable statement and thus create a response that was not warranted.

14427
25:14:39,600 --> 25:14:43,040
There are various types of SQL injections as shown on the screen.

14428
25:14:43,163 --> 25:14:55,236
in-band SQL injection where you can look at an error-based or a union-based injection, a blind SQL injection where it is either Boolean-based or a time-based attack, and then an out-of-bound SQL injection.

14429
25:14:55,662 --> 25:15:05,188
Essentially, you're looking at databases and you're looking at application security where you want to encourage secure coding practices so in unvalidated input is mitigated.

14430
25:15:05,678 --> 25:15:07,748
The next question is what is spoofing?

14431
25:15:08,160 --> 25:15:12,640
Now, in spoofing, you're basically assuming the identity of another person.

14432
25:15:13,006 --> 25:15:19,840
So here the attacker pretends to be some other person or an organization and sends you an email that appears to be a legitimate email.

14433
25:15:20,000 --> 25:15:30,116
It looks almost genuine, it has been constructed to replicate what a genuine email would have been, and it is very difficult to spot a fake one.

14434
25:15:30,670 --> 25:15:34,276
There are different ways to identify whether an email is genuine or not.

