14438
25:15:44,878 --> 25:15:51,280
Moving on to the next question: what is a distributed denial of service attack or a DDoS attack?

14439
25:15:51,470 --> 25:16:01,280
Now, generally a denial of service attack is an attack where legitimate users are prevented access to the resources that they legitimately can access, right?

14440
25:16:01,520 --> 25:16:14,276
So, for example, if it is a bandwidth-based attack, the attacker consumes the bandwidth of the network in such a way that there is no more bandwidth left for legitimate users to access the network.

14441
25:16:14,894 --> 25:16:22,468
Now, a single device may not be able to generate that much amount of traffic to consume the bandwidth of a huge server.

14442
25:16:22,734 --> 25:16:32,516
Thus, the attacker will construct a botnet, and through that botnet, they will launch a distributed denial of service attack to the target victim.

14443
25:16:33,440 --> 25:16:37,840
So a botnet, uh, there are two terms that we want to look at over here.

14444
25:16:37,920 --> 25:16:41,988
The first term is a bot, and the second one being the botnet itself.

14445
25:16:42,254 --> 25:16:54,212
Bot is a software that once installed on a victim's machine allows the hacker to send remote commands to that machine that will make it do uh generate some activity.

14446
25:16:55,120 --> 25:17:04,000
Once we have enough machines on which such bots have been implemented, the collection of these machines would be known as a botnet.

14447
25:17:04,400 --> 25:17:20,880
So an attacker would then instruct this entire botnet to start generating data traffic to be target to be sent to the targeted network or to the targeted server, which will then bog down the server, thus crashing it and preventing users from accessing that particular resource.

14448
25:17:21,294 --> 25:17:25,520
The next question is how to avoid ARP poisoning or ARP.

14449
25:17:25,710 --> 25:17:28,080
Now, first let's understand what ARP is.

14450
25:17:28,366 --> 25:17:34,436
ARP stands for address resolution protocol, which is a protocol used by computers to communicate over the network.

14451
25:17:34,880 --> 25:17:40,960
Once your computer boots up, it starts a discovery process of identifying its neighbors.

14452
25:17:41,134 --> 25:17:53,520
So if I'm in a particular subnet, my machine will proactively send out ARP requests and address resolution protocol to find out which other machines are within the same network and which are live.

14453
25:17:53,680 --> 25:18:00,516
Once it sends out a query, a live machine will respond to that query along with its MAC address.

14454
25:18:01,998 --> 25:18:10,084
This information is then stored in what is known as an ARP table or an ARP table on the machine's cachet.

14455
25:18:10,670 --> 25:18:28,000
So whenever my machine now wants to send out a packet to this particular machine, it will go to the ARP table, it will identify the IP address and the associated MAC address, it will print that onto the data packet as a destination IP and destination MAC and send that packet across to the switch.

14456
25:18:28,080 --> 25:18:35,108
The switch will then identify the MAC address and send the packet to the relevant machine that is connected to that particular switch.

14457
25:18:35,600 --> 25:18:42,720
Now, to confuse the switch into sending it to a different machine, the R poisoning attack is created.

14458
25:18:43,040 --> 25:18:48,900
This attack is generally launched to create a man in the middle attack.

14459
25:18:49,280 --> 25:18:55,840
Now, to prevent this R poisoning from happening in the first place, there are three different aspects that we can utilize.

14460
25:18:56,080 --> 25:19:02,640
First, we can use packet filtering, which will filter filter out and block packets that are the same source address data.

14461
25:19:02,720 --> 25:19:07,440
So you have identified some malicious IP addresses and you want to block out some IP addresses.

14462
25:19:07,520 --> 25:19:17,360
So you're using a packet filter firewall where you have constructed the firewall to filter out certain packets originating from particular range of IP addresses.

14463
25:19:17,582 --> 25:19:22,148
This firewall and this technique will then block those kind of packets coming in.

14464
25:19:22,960 --> 25:19:26,000
Second, keeping away from trust relationships.

14465
25:19:26,286 --> 25:19:34,080
Organizations will develop protocols that do not depend on trust relationships, and thus you want to keep this protocol away from that.

14466
25:19:34,240 --> 25:19:42,400
Once you have created a trust relationship, these machines should not be sending out ARP requests to other machines in the first place.

14467
25:19:42,560 --> 25:19:50,468
Since the trust relationship has been defined and these machines know whom to communicate with, such kind of protocols should then be disabled.

14468
25:19:51,214 --> 25:19:53,440
Or you can use an ARP spoofing software.

14469
25:19:53,520 --> 25:20:00,560
So there are some there are softwares out there that will look for ARP spoofing and prevent that from happening in the first place.

14470
25:20:00,640 --> 25:20:03,396
So if somebody has sent out a spoofed ARP packet.

14471
25:20:04,014 --> 25:20:08,784
That packet will be picked up by this software and it will be mitigated.

14472
25:20:09,806 --> 25:20:20,944
Network visualizers like glasswire, antiviruses like Sophos, they have inbuilt capabilities of identifying ARP spoofing attacks and mitigate them in the first place.

14473
25:20:21,358 --> 25:20:23,904
In the next question, we are going to discuss what is ransomware.

14474
25:20:24,142 --> 25:20:31,104
Now, ransomware is a type of malware that blocks victims to access personal files and demands ransom to regain access.

14475
25:20:31,278 --> 25:20:32,224
There are three categories.

14476
25:20:32,384 --> 25:20:35,824
Before we go into the categories, let's just revisit what ransomware is.

14477
25:20:35,984 --> 25:20:37,504
Let's start with the word malware.

14478
25:20:37,584 --> 25:20:46,564
Malware is a malicious software that poses as a legitimate software but has a payload that will have a security impact on your machine.

14479
25:20:47,374 --> 25:20:54,064
So, in this instance, viruses, Trojans, all of these can be classified under malware.

14480
25:20:54,224 --> 25:20:55,424
So can ransomware.

14481
25:20:55,664 --> 25:21:00,164
A Trojan is a software that will give you a backdoor access to a particular device.

14482
25:21:00,384 --> 25:21:03,904
A virus will do some destructive activity on that device.

14483
25:21:04,144 --> 25:21:15,364
A ransomware will basically encrypt the data of that particular user on that particular machine, thus rendering that my that data inaccessible to the users themselves.

14484
25:21:16,238 --> 25:21:21,092
And in turn, will demand a ransom to provide access to that particular data.

14485
25:21:21,744 --> 25:21:33,264
So the three types of ransomwares: the first one is scareware, which uses social engineering to cause anxiety or the perception of a threat to manipulate users into buying unwanted software.

14486
25:21:33,486 --> 25:21:49,504
So this preys on the gullibility of humans, where you can see a pop-up appearing on your screen, which can scare you into believing that you may have been attacked or there is a virus on your machine, and then instructs you to download a particular software to mitigate that particular virus.

14487
25:21:49,664 --> 25:21:58,020
Now, the malware will be in this software that you will be downloading, and then a ransomware will be installed and your data will be encrypted.

14488
25:21:58,464 --> 25:22:07,172
Screen lockers where locking users' computers by preventing them from logging in and displaying an official looking message.

14489
25:22:07,344 --> 25:22:26,304
You will see a screensaver once you boot up, which prevents you from accessing the login page, so it will not allow you to log into your own machine, but it will give you a warning that your data has been encrypted and you need to connect to a particular email address and send bitcoins over there to get a decryption key to access your own data.

14490
25:22:26,574 --> 25:22:35,984
And then the encrypting so ransomware displays a message demanding payment in return for the private asymmetric key, which is needed to decrypt the symmetric keys for encrypted file.

14491
25:22:36,064 --> 25:22:52,304
So once your files have been encrypted, you might just have a blank screen in front of you where you'll receive a warning message where it instructs you to pay up a ransom in bitcoins or in some cryptocurrency to some particular digital e-wallet which is not traceable.

14492
25:22:52,558 --> 25:22:57,252
And once you make that payment, they will send you the decryption key and then you can access your data.

14493
25:22:57,504 --> 25:23:02,944
Then talking about the next question, what is the difference between an active and a passive cyber attack?

14494
25:23:03,104 --> 25:23:16,464
Now, when we talk about cyber attacks, cyber attack is an activity that is caused by a malicious user who wants to try to get access or do some security incidents on the victim's devices.

14495
25:23:16,782 --> 25:23:18,544
So there are two ways that can happen.

14496
25:23:18,624 --> 25:23:20,944
It's either in an active manner or a passive manner.

14497
25:23:21,024 --> 25:23:28,064
In an active manner, the intruder attempts to disrupt a network's normalcy, modifies data, and tries to alter the system's resources.

14498
25:23:28,144 --> 25:23:41,424
So this is more active, where the attacker will proactively try to destroy the network so that communications fail, or they might try to modify the data where, uh, using a ransom where they can encrypt it.

14499
25:23:41,582 --> 25:23:51,024
or they might delete that data using a virus, or steal that data using a Trojan, or they might even alter the data so that it is no longer trustworthy.

14500
25:23:51,278 --> 25:23:55,264
Whereas in a passive attack, the intruder intercepts data traveling through a network.

14501
25:23:55,344 --> 25:23:58,064
Here, the intruder eavesdrop but does not modify the message.

14502
25:23:58,144 --> 25:24:08,304
So they're just listening in, they're just observing what is going on, they're not manipulating the data, they're not stealing anything, it's just that they are monitoring the activity that's going on.

14503
25:24:08,686 --> 25:24:11,584
Then the next question: what is a social engineering attack?

14504
25:24:11,790 --> 25:24:14,628
Now, social engineering attack is a people-based attack.

14505
25:24:14,894 --> 25:24:17,264
The victim here is the human by itself.

14506
25:24:17,344 --> 25:24:19,300
The vulnerability also lies in the human.

14507
25:24:19,584 --> 25:24:25,024
It may be executed through a computer, but end of the day, the gullibility is of the human.

14508
25:24:25,198 --> 25:24:30,596
So it is the art of manipulating people so that they end up giving up confidential information.

14509
25:24:31,184 --> 25:24:38,624
Now, we always read in the papers where somebody got manipulated, their passwords got hacked, and somebody's life savings got wiped out, right?

14510
25:24:38,704 --> 25:24:43,460
Because they shared the OTP with someone or they shared the password with someone.

14511
25:24:43,726 --> 25:24:54,052
Now, creating a scenario where these people will fall prey to this attack and share this kind of personal information to unknown people, that is where the social engineering attack comes in.

14512
25:24:54,862 --> 25:24:59,940
Creating that scenario which will ensure that these people give out this confidential information.

14513
25:25:00,224 --> 25:25:02,404
Now there are three categories in this attack.

14514
25:25:02,944 --> 25:25:08,100
The first one is a phishing attack, second is a spear phishing attack, and a third is a railing attack.

14515
25:25:08,398 --> 25:25:11,524
Now, phishing attack is basically a generic attack.

14516
25:25:12,064 --> 25:25:14,704
It is targeted to the world at large.

14517
25:25:14,864 --> 25:25:18,372
Whoever falls prey to that attack will be a victim.

14518
25:25:19,278 --> 25:25:26,464
Whereas a spear phishing attack is a targeted attack towards a specific individual or a group of individuals or towards an organization.

14519
25:25:26,766 --> 25:25:39,984
So there is a lot more research that goes into spear phishing where you analyze the victim, you try to figure out what their vulnerabilities are, and you tailor make, or you customize the attack to that particular vulnerability.

14520
25:25:40,224 --> 25:25:46,276
Once you have that attack, you launch it against those people, those people will then fall prey to this attack.

14521
25:25:46,734 --> 25:25:51,824
And a whaling attack is where you're attacking top-level executives.

14522
25:25:51,904 --> 25:25:58,564
So the C level executives of an organization, politicians, movie stars, wealthy and powerful people.

14523
25:25:59,104 --> 25:26:03,236
So any of these people, when they're attacked, it will be known as a whaling attack.

14524
25:26:03,758 --> 25:26:06,304
Next question: What is man-in-the-middle attack?

14525
25:26:06,384 --> 25:26:15,524
Now, this is something that we had touched base when we talked about ARP, where the ARP poisoning attack needs to be executed to leverage a man-in-the-middle attack.

14526
25:26:15,904 --> 25:26:21,984
Now, in the man in the middle attack, the attacker attacking computer takes the IP address of the client.

14527
25:26:22,286 --> 25:26:25,584
Unaware of this, the server continues to communicate with the attacker.

14528
25:26:25,744 --> 25:26:29,604
Now, if you remember in a previous question, we have also talked about spoofing.

14529
25:26:29,998 --> 25:26:48,624
So, in this scenario, a attacker has spoofed their IP address to replicate themselves as a genuine client, and now with that spoofing in mind, they might either through a R poisoning attack or just because of the spoofed IP address become a man in the middle.

14530
25:26:48,704 --> 25:26:58,244
That means that they are now eavesdropping on the conversation between the actual client and the server by posing themselves as a server.

14531
25:26:58,544 --> 25:27:09,184
In this scenario, the attacker is now a go-between between the client and the server and can eavesdrop and can copy the data if they want, they can modify the data as well.

14532
25:27:09,422 --> 25:27:19,504
So, as you can see on the screen, the attacker becomes man in the middle, which means that they are now eavesdropping on the conversation that is happening between the client and the server.

14533
25:27:19,758 --> 25:27:23,584
The next question who are black hat hackers and white hat hackers?

14534
25:27:23,664 --> 25:27:27,044
The main thing is the differentiation between a black hat hacker and a white hat hacker.

14535
25:27:27,344 --> 25:27:32,544
Now, black hat hackers are skilled individuals who illegally hack into a system.

14536
25:27:32,654 --> 25:27:35,684
The motive behind such an attack is mostly for monetary gain.

14537
25:27:36,014 --> 25:27:38,864
These individuals are known also known as security crackers.

14538
25:27:38,944 --> 25:27:49,124
Now, if you look at your criminal hackers, those who have malicious intent, those who do hacking for the intent of personal gain or for the matter of disruption.

14539
25:27:49,424 --> 25:27:54,212
The main thing that black hat hackers lack is authorization.

14540
25:27:54,542 --> 25:28:04,548
They are not authorized to do the activity that they are about to do, and they are going to get unauthorized access to devices or to data which is going to cause losses to the organization involved.

14541
25:28:05,184 --> 25:28:09,892
Whereas on the other side, a white hat hacker are also known as ethical hackers.

14542
25:28:10,464 --> 25:28:18,532
These are the individuals who discover vulnerabilities in a computer network, and they help the organizations mitigate these vulnerabilities.

14543
25:28:18,830 --> 25:28:22,064
They help the organizations defend themselves from black hat hackers.

14544
25:28:22,144 --> 25:28:29,264
So the main difference between these two types of hackers, a black hat and a white hat, is the intent and the authorization.

14545
25:28:29,550 --> 25:28:37,504
So black hat hackers will have malicious intent, they will try to personally gain from that attack from by finding out vulnerabilities.

14546
25:28:37,678 --> 25:28:41,824
They also will not have authorization to conduct whatever activity they are doing.

14547
25:28:41,984 --> 25:28:51,424
Whereas on the other side, white hat hackers will be hired by organizations, they will be provide authorization for certain activity that the white hat hacker can do to find out those vulnerabilities.

14548
25:28:51,662 --> 25:29:01,584
Once those vulnerabilities have been found out by the white hat hacker, they will report it to the management and guide them in implementing security controls to mitigate those vulnerabilities.

14549
25:29:01,902 --> 25:29:07,104
The main difference between a black hat and a white hat is the authorization and the intent.

14550
25:29:07,502 --> 25:29:09,540
The next question what are honey pots?

14551
25:29:10,766 --> 25:29:16,448
Now, honey pots are a very interesting device that can be introduced in a network.

14552
25:29:17,008 --> 25:29:23,908
These basically are decoy servers that are implemented in a network to attract the attention of an attacker.

14553
25:29:24,288 --> 25:29:34,180
It is there to lure an attacker into attacking that particular device, thus creating a security blanket for the rest of the devices.

14554
25:29:34,798 --> 25:29:45,568
So if an attacker has been able to bypass a firewall and is now trying to scan a particular network, when they scan, they will come across various devices that are there in the network.

14555
25:29:45,728 --> 25:29:49,248
They will then proceed to do a vulnerability scan on these devices.

14556
25:29:49,646 --> 25:30:02,276
The honeypot at that point in time will provide as an or prove as an attraction to these attackers because it will demonstrate some vulnerabilities to the hacker which will divert their attention.

14557
25:30:02,510 --> 25:30:05,248
So these vulnerabilities are simulated on these devices.

14558
25:30:05,328 --> 25:30:06,724
These actually do not exist.

14559
25:30:07,054 --> 25:30:18,048
But the moment the attacker then starts interacting with the honeypot, the honeypot will identify that as a malicious traffic and will warm the warn the administrator about a possible attack that is going on.

14560
25:30:18,350 --> 25:30:27,428
The administrator will then investigate through the honeypot of what activity is going on and then reconfigure the security controls to block the attacker or mitigate the attack itself.

14561
25:30:28,142 --> 25:30:38,768
So it is more of a decoy server that will showcase or simulate some vulnerabilities to an attacker, thus to lure them and safeguard the rest of the network.

14562
25:30:39,022 --> 25:30:43,620
Moving on to the next question: define cryptography, encryption, and decryption.

14563
25:30:43,968 --> 25:30:54,308
Now, cryptography is used by security professionals to scramble data into non-readable format, which is used in securing that information.

14564
25:30:55,630 --> 25:31:02,404
So it involves converting data from a readable format into a non-readable format and then reversing it back to readable format again.

14565
25:31:03,054 --> 25:31:09,408
For example, the word computer is now scrambled into looking like an unreadable format.

14566
25:31:09,568 --> 25:31:18,852
Now, if you look at this word that it has been scrambled into, it would be very difficult for a human to figure out what the actual word was.

14567
25:31:19,214 --> 25:31:29,168
Now in this scenario, we have taken an algorithm where we have made a shift of the alphabet where we have added two alphabets to the current one.

14568
25:31:29,328 --> 25:31:36,208
So C plus 2 becomes E, O plus 2 becomes Q, M plus 2 becomes O.

14569
25:31:36,526 --> 25:31:38,372
So we have done a shift of 2.

14570
25:31:38,768 --> 25:31:44,448
And thus the key over here for this algorithm is the alphabet plus 2.

14571
25:31:44,528 --> 25:31:50,724
So any person who figures that out will be able to unscramble this and convert this back into readable text.

14572
25:31:51,248 --> 25:32:00,324
The fact of scrambling a readable text data into something that is unreadable by using a particular key is what cryptography is all about.

14573
25:32:01,358 --> 25:32:08,848
Now as we discussed the decryption again is replacing the alphabet and taking it further back by two characters.

14574
25:32:09,102 --> 25:32:16,048
So E minus 2 becomes C, Q minus 2 becomes O, O minus 2 becomes M, and so on so forth.

14575
25:32:16,128 --> 25:32:24,288
So anybody who knows this key, the shift key, anybody will able to decrypt this particular character.

14576
25:32:24,368 --> 25:32:25,968
So this depends on the user.

14577
25:32:26,048 --> 25:32:35,588
If I want to utilize alphabet plus 5, then the spacing, the shifting of that character will be the fifth character from that particular character, and so on so forth.

14578
25:32:36,046 --> 25:32:39,940
And now the last question: what is the difference between ciphertext and clear text?

14579
25:32:40,654 --> 25:32:44,053
Ciphertext refers to the text which is encrypted and totally undesirable.

14580
25:32:46,254 --> 25:32:48,928
The message received after decryption is known as clear text.

14581
25:32:49,102 --> 25:32:50,608
This text is comprehensible.

14582
25:32:50,848 --> 25:32:53,568
So the word computer is clear text.

14583
25:32:53,648 --> 25:32:59,140
That means that it has not been treated to any cryptographic measures, it is what it is intended to be.

14584
25:32:59,630 --> 25:33:07,716
However, if the moment we encrypt it, that means we scramble it into unreadable text by using any of the algorithms that we'll be looking at.

14585
25:33:08,494 --> 25:33:10,468
That text is known as a ciphertext.

14586
25:33:11,182 --> 25:33:13,828
And without the key, this becomes unreadable.

14587
25:33:14,702 --> 25:33:18,928
The clear text, as discussed, is the plain word that we have utilized.

14588
25:33:19,008 --> 25:33:23,620
We are using the English language in this instance, so the plain word computer is the clear text.

14589
